window.__BIRINCI_STORIES__ = {"site_title":"Edifying sayings and stories","lang":"en","categories":[{"title":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","blurb":"Stories of belief, worship, and the inner path.","count":36,"stories":[{"stem":"lawful-morsel","title":"A Lawful Morsel","paragraphs":["Fresh flour and clean water cannot make good dough if the yeast is spoiled. Nor can such dough produce good bread.","To raise a child of good character, one must nourish that child with a lawful morsel.","— Imam al-Ghazali","Moral: A child’s moral formation begins with the food placed on the family table. Lawful earnings and clean sustenance are the foundation of sound character.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"calamity-and-blessing","title":"Adversity and Blessing","paragraphs":["If adversity brings you closer to Allah, it is a blessing.","If a blessing draws you away from Allah, it is a hardship.","Moral: A hardship that brings you closer to Allah is a blessing, while a blessing that draws you away from Him becomes a hardship. What matters is not living in comfort, but which path you take.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"church-bell","title":"The Church Bell","paragraphs":["Long ago, a country had a custom: when a commoner died, the church bell rang once; for a nobleman, twice; for a relative of the king, three times; and for the king himself, four times.","One day, a court that people trusted to uphold justice wrongfully convicted a citizen. The church bell rang five times.","The bewildered townspeople gathered at the church. “Father, who could be more important than the king? Why did the bell ring five times?”","“Of course there is — justice,” the priest replied. “Today, justice has died.”","Moral: When justice dies, something greater than the king dies. Stand by the truth so that injustice does not go unpunished.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"faith-is-half-of-success","title":"Confidence Is Half the Battle","paragraphs":["A school principal invited several of his fellow teachers into his office and said to them:","“You are our most capable teachers, so we’re entrusting you with a group of our brightest students. Over the coming year, give them the best education you can and help raise them to a higher level. I wish you every success.”","Teachers, students, and parents considered this initiative a very good idea and were eagerly awaiting the end of the school year.","At the end of the year, their test results surpassed those of other students across the city.","The principal congratulated the teachers and said: “I have a confession to make. Those students we entrusted to you were not the most capable children at all - they were average. We chose them by chance.”","Hearing this, the teachers became proud and began to consider the students’ success a result of their professionalism.","“But I have another confession,” the principal continued. “You weren’t our most capable teachers either. I drew your names at random from a bag. You succeeded because you believed.”","Moral: Confidence can bring hidden abilities to life. High expectations may be the first step toward strong results.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dervishs-spoons","title":"The Dervish’s Long Spoons","paragraphs":["One day a dervish was asked:","“What is the difference between people who truly love and those who merely talk about love?”","“Wait, and I’ll show you,” he replied.","He invited several people who loved only in words and set a meal before them. First came bowls of hot soup, then unusually long spoons called “dervish spoons”. The dervish laid down one rule:","“You must hold your spoons at the very end of the handle.”","They agreed. They tried again and again, but the spoons were too long to bring to their own mouths. Soup spilled over them, they burned themselves, and at last they left the table hungry and embarrassed.","Then the dervish invited people who understood what true love meant. People with radiant faces and eyes full of love came and sat down. He served the same soup and gave them the same rule. This time, each person filled a spoon and held it out to someone across the table. They fed one another, laughed together, and rose from the table well fed and grateful.","When the guests had gone, the dervish turned to those who had asked the question:","“Now you see. At the table of life, the one who thinks only of himself stays hungry. The one who thinks of his brother and his friend and reaches out to them is filled in turn. In life’s marketplace, the one who gives is the one who gains, not the one who only takes.”","Moral: Those who think only of themselves stay hungry at life’s table. Those who feed the person across from them are filled in return. The one who gives always gains.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dervishs-robe","title":"The Dervish’s Robe","paragraphs":["One day a wounded bird came before the king and complained that a dervish had broken its wing. The king immediately summoned the dervish.","“This bird says you broke its wing. Why did you do it?”","The dervish tried to defend himself:","“Your Majesty, I wanted to hunt this bird. I approached, but it didn’t fly away. I came closer, and still it stayed. I thought it would surrender. But when I reached for it, it tried to escape, and that is when its wing broke.”","The king turned to the bird:","“This man is right. Why didn’t you flee? He had not attacked you suddenly. You saw him coming and could have flown away in time. And now you come before me and complain.”","The bird replied:","“Because he was dressed as a dervish. If he had been a hunter, I would have flown away at once. But I thought, ‘A dervish fears Allah. Surely he won’t hurt me.’”","The king was silent for a moment, then ordered:","“The bird is right. Break the dervish’s arm!”","The bird protested immediately:","“Please, Your Majesty, don’t do that.”","“Why not?”","“When his arm heals, he will do the same thing again. Take away his dervish’s robe instead, so that other birds won’t be deceived as I was.”","Moral: The appearance of holiness does not make a person holy. Judge people by their actions, not their clothing; a robe alone does not make a dervish.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"your-hand-at-work-your-hope-in-god","title":"Work with Your Hands and Trust in Allah","paragraphs":["A dervish spent much of his time in prayer and solitude. He did not want to depend on others, so he also worked each day, buying bread with his earnings and sharing what remained with the poor.","One day, while worshipping in the desert, he saw a fox whose crippled legs could barely carry it. “How does it survive?” he wondered. “Wild animals live by hunting, and hunting takes a swift foot. This poor creature has no feet. How does Allah provide for it?”","Just then, a lion appeared with a gazelle in its jaws. It ate its fill and walked away. The crippled fox that had been lying in wait crawled over and fed on what remained.","The dervish marveled and praised Allah, who had sent the fox its provision by means of the lion. Then he thought, “If the fox receives food without working, why shouldn’t I? I could give the time I spend on work to worship and earn greater reward. From now on I will not work. Allah, who feeds the fox by the lion’s hand, will provide for me too.”","He stopped working and prayed day and night. But however long he waited, no provision came. No one knocked at his door, and no one brought a piece of bread. Day by day he wasted away until he was nothing but skin and bone.","Finally, one day when he was exhausted, he heard a voice from the unseen:","“I gave you a healthy body, a strong arm, and a swift foot. But you likened yourself to a crippled fox. Why choose the fox as your example instead of the lion? Why do you covet another’s leftovers? Rise and work like a lion. Earn your living, and let those without hands or feet hope in you. Feed yourself and the needy.”","Ashamed, the dervish repented and returned to work. Once more, he earned his bread and shared it, abandoning the wish to live at others’ expense.","Moral: A person who covets the share of the needy despite having the strength is not trusting in Allah - he is simply covering up his laziness with faith.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"drinking-the-sherbet-of-martyrdom","title":"Drinking the Sherbet of Martyrdom","paragraphs":["Soldiers mortally wounded in battle in Ottoman times were given sherbet made with Zamzam water, which doctors carried with them in small barrels, to sweeten their mouths before death. The expression “drinking the sherbet of martyrdom” originated from this.","Moral: Behind every expression there stands a history and a sacrifice. The words we use preserve memories of the past.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-fasting","title":"Fasting","paragraphs":["During Ramadan, several young men saw an older man sitting in a corner eating and approached him with a teasing question.","“You’re eating, uncle — aren’t you fasting?”","“Of course I am. I’m only drinking water and eating.”","They laughed. “What sort of fast is that?”","“I am fasting. I do not lie, look at anyone with an evil eye, mock people, waste, humiliate anyone, gossip, or covet what belongs to others. Illness prevents me from going without food.” Then he asked:","“And you? Are you fasting?”","Ashamed, they lowered their heads. “No. We’re only going without food.”","Moral: If a person abstains from food and does not abstain from lying, backbiting, insulting, and evil deeds, he is not fully living the true spirit of the fast.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"fortieth-day-rite","title":"The Fortieth-Day Rite","paragraphs":["In Turkic lands, tradition keeps a mother and newborn at home without visitors for forty days after the birth. The custom is said to date back to the Göktürks.","On the fortieth day, a close relative invites the mother and baby to visit and presents symbolic gifts: coins for prosperity; cotton for a long life and a gentle heart, so that he lives until his hair turns white; sugar for sweet words and a cheerful face; rice so that blessing may come to the home; salt so the taste of his mouth never fades; and an egg for a full belly and a healthy body.","Moral: In each of our customs a blessing and a love lie hidden. A baby’s gifts express hopes for an entire lifetime.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"friend-of-god","title":"Friend of Allah","paragraphs":["An elderly man notices a barefoot child, is moved by compassion, and buys him a pair of shoes.","The child holds the shoes in his hands and gazes at them for a while. Then he looks up and asks:","“Are you Allah?”","The old man’s hands begin to tremble and his eyes fill with tears.","“No, my child. I’m just an ordinary person.”","The child thinks for a moment, then says softly:","“Then you must be a friend of Allah. Tonight I cried and told Him I had no shoes.”","Moral: Sometimes Allah’s help arrives through the hands of good people. Perhaps you can be the answer to someone’s prayer.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"blessings-god-has-given","title":"The Gifts of Allah","paragraphs":["If Allah has given someone a blessing, He will also look at how they used it:","If He granted you power, were you just?","If He gave you high status, did you show compassion?","If He gave you wealth, what was your character?","If He gave you a peaceful life, were you grateful?","If He gave you a life partner, were you faithful?","If He gave you children, how did you raise them?","Moral: Every blessing is also a test. What shows who you are is not what you have gained, but how you use it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hajj-pilgrimage","title":"The Hajj Pilgrimage","paragraphs":["During the Hajj, when the time came to cast stones at the devil, one pilgrim began striking himself with the stones instead. A young man nearby asked in surprise:","“Uncle, why are you hitting yourself?”","The man replied:","“My son, what business do we have with the devil here? First we must stone the devil within ourselves, discipline our nafs, and purify ourselves.”","* * *","The heat belongs to fire, not to the griddle;","karamat lies in the head, not in a crown.","Whatever you seek, seek it in yourself; it is not in Jerusalem, Mecca, or the Hajj.","Moral: The real struggle is not with the devil outside, but with a person’s own nafs. Karamat is not in the stone or the crown; it is inside the person.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"forbidden-money","title":"Ill-Gotten Money","paragraphs":["One day, I saw a man drop some money in the street. Usually I would have called out, “You’ve dropped your money!” This time I followed the devil. I picked it up and slipped it into my pocket.","When I got home, I told my wife the story.","“Add a little of our own and let’s go to the movies,” she said. I agreed, and we made plans for the weekend.","“You give your word now, but then you may suddenly back out,” she said. “Let’s buy the tickets online now, so we can be at ease.” So I did.","I work as a repairman. Soon afterward, a wealthy customer called to offer me a job at his garden on Saturday. I asked whether I could come on Sunday, but he said it had to be Saturday. Of course, that was the day I had booked the movie. I lost a well-paid job.","When Saturday came, my wife and I were about to leave when her parents arrived unexpectedly.","I was ready to say, “we’re off to the movies,” but my wife got in first. “We’re going to the movies—come with us!” There was nothing I could do.","My mother-in-law disliked public transportation, so we took a taxi. Naturally, I paid.","At the cinema, I bought two more tickets and before the film, a box of popcorn for each person. After all, the son-in-law had found money in the street; he must treat them! I had spent less on meat for the sacrifice than I spent on that popcorn. We soon finished it, so during the film I had to go and buy more. My windfall was already costing me dearly, but the worst was still to come.","After the movie, I ran into an old acquaintance with whom I had a longstanding quarrel. Despite the presence of my in-laws, he insulted me. I lost my temper, and we began fighting. My father-in-law joined in. By the time the police arrived, my nose and his arm were broken. We settled the dispute at the station, but I still had to pay both our medical bills.","Recently, I saw a man buying a doner kebab. When the seller handed him his change, some coins fell to the ground. The man left them there. I couldn’t help myself. “Brother, if you won’t think of yourself, at least think of whoever finds that money! Please pick it up. Don’t bring trouble on someone else!”","Moral: Money not earned by the sweat of your brow brings no good; often you lose many times more than what you found. Do not reach for what is not halal.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"help-yourself-o-exalted-god","title":"You Yourself Help, O Exalted Allah!","paragraphs":["To the restless, left without a home,","to the orphan, left alone,","to the needy, left hungry and thirsty,","to the sick, left in hardship,","to the one with a wounded heart, and to the one whose eyes wait on the road,","to the hopeless who can see no way forward,","to the heedless, left without light,","to the one who has a wish, who builds dreams,","to the faithless, and to the one left without You —","help them Yourself, O exalted Allah!","Moral: The last refuge of the helpless, the lonely, and the hopeless is the Creator. Prayer is the language of deepest need.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-fate-allows-we-will-meet","title":"If Fate Allows, We Will Meet","paragraphs":["Someone who used to say, “We’ll meet,” now says, “We’ll meet if fate allows.” I find that strange. I have not changed, and my feeling toward him is as warm as before; I would still be delighted to see him. Yet those words seem to place a barrier between us. I know nothing of his fate, and he knows nothing of mine. Do our two fates know each other? Can they arrange a meeting on our behalf?","Perhaps we should not expect anything from fate. Perhaps this is simply another way of saying, “Out of sight, out of mind.”","I did not like this phrase “if fate allows” at all.","Moral: Do not let distance grow between you and those you care about. Do not put it off by saying “if fate allows” — we meet; cheer the heart today.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"light-of-the-universe","title":"The Light of the Universe","paragraphs":["The British painter William Holman Hunt’s work “The Light of the Universe” is exhibited at the Royal Academy in London. It shows a philosophical-looking man standing before a door at night, holding a lantern in one hand, knocking with the other, and waiting in hope that it will open.","An art critic asked him, “It’s a beautiful painting, but I cannot understand its meaning. Will the door he is knocking on never open? Have you forgotten to paint a handle?”","Hunt replied with a smile, “This is no ordinary door. It is the door of the human heart, and it opens only from within. There is no handle outside. If the one inside does not open it, you cannot enter.”","Moral: The door of the heart opens only from the inside. Love and goodness do not force their way in - you must let them in yourself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"let-prayer-come-and-find-you","title":"May the Prayer You Need Find You","paragraphs":["Our elders used to say:","Be a support to a poor person, a door a destitute person can knock on, a hand that strokes an orphan’s head, and shoes on a child’s feet. Open a way for the one in distress, and help the one whose affairs have grown difficult, so that a prayer may find you when you need one yourself.","Moral: When you help those in need and stroke an orphan’s head, you become worthy of others’ prayers. The hand with which you do good will one day open toward you as well.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"eat-less-and-stay-on-the-right-path","title":"Eating Moderately and Living Honestly","paragraphs":["Moderate eating keeps the doctor away; honest living keeps the judge away.","“Moderate eating keeps the doctor away” means that if a person does not overeat and eats in measure, they can protect their health better and may need a doctor less. Eating little here does not mean going hungry; it means mastering the appetite and eating in due measure.","“Honest living keeps the judge away” means that a person who lives honestly, justly, and lawfully, and does not infringe on others’ rights, meets fewer disputes and legal problems. Here, “honest living” means both staying on the right path and acting conscientiously.","Moral: Eat in moderation and act with integrity, so that you need neither a doctor nor a judge.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"my-alif-has-been-dotted","title":"My Alif Has Been Dotted","paragraphs":["There is an expression in Turkish: “elifim noktalandı.” It comes from writing the letters “alif” (ا) and “ha” (ه) side by side in the old Ottoman script (اه). Because the “ha” after “alif” resembles a dot, people say “alifim noktalandı,” and metaphorically it means “my joy turned to sorrow.” The letter “alif,” with its long, thin appearance, expresses freedom and beauty, while the letter “ha,” with its bent, almost crushed appearance, expresses distress and collapse.","Together, the two letters form the sigh “ah.”","Moral: A single letter—or a single moment—can turn joy into sorrow. Expressions preserve the feelings and stories behind them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"not-leaving-the-right-path","title":"Staying on the Right Path","paragraphs":["Once there was a man who had grown tired of wasting his days in idleness and living off his inheritance. Wanting to live honestly, he went to the king and asked him to show him the way.","After thinking for a moment, the king handed him a jar filled to the brim with olive oil.","“Carry this jar across the city without spilling a drop. If you spill even one, you will pay with your life.”","The king sent two soldiers with him to make sure the order was obeyed. The man concentrated with all his might and reached the far end of the city without spilling a drop. When he returned, he said:","“Your Majesty, I have done exactly as you asked.”","The king asked:","“What did you see on the way?”","That day the city was packed with a market crowd so thick that a thrown needle would not have reached the ground, but the man replied:","“Nothing, Your Majesty. I was so afraid for my life and so careful not to spill the oil that I could not look away from the jar for a moment.”","The king said:","“If you are this careful in every task you do and in every duty given to you, devote yourself to your work, and never forget that Allah’s eye is always upon you, you will never leave the right path.”","Moral: A person who gives full attention to every task, and does not forget that Allah sees him, does not stray from the right path.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"o-god-heal-our-sorrows","title":"O Allah, Grant Relief from Our Troubles","paragraphs":["By Your names Ar-Rahman and Ar-Rahim, grant relief from our troubles.","By Your names Al-Ghaffar and At-Tawwab, forgive our sins and accept our repentance.","By Your name Ar-Razzaq, bless the sustenance You provide.","By Your name Ash-Shafi, grant healing to our sick.","By Your name Al-Fattah, open for us the doors of goodness.","Moral: When we recognize our helplessness and pray sincerely, the heart finds peace. Prayer is the language of hope and trust in Allah.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"o-god-give-first-to-the-mountains-and-stones","title":"O Allah, Give First to the Mountains and Stones","paragraphs":["An old prayer from the Turkic lands says:","“O Allah, give first to the mountains and stones, the forests, the animals, and the waters. Then give to the people, to my neighbor, and to those in need. If anything remains, give to me last.”","Moral: True generosity means thinking of others before oneself. This prayer carries within it an entire people’s tradition of sharing.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pray-while-washing-your-dishes","title":"Pray While You Wash the Dishes","paragraphs":["As you wash the dishes, offer a prayer of thanks. The dishes show that you had something to eat, that you fed someone, that you cared for someone, and that you cooked and set the table.","Remember those who have not even a dish to wash and no one to set a table for.","Moral: Value blessings that look ordinary, too. If you have a dish to wash, food has come to your table and you have fed someone. Do not forget to give thanks.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"ramadan-prayer","title":"Ramadan Prayer","paragraphs":["May Almighty Allah accept our prayers, bless our sustenance, heal our sick, forgive our sins, open doors of goodness before us, and fill our hearts with love and mercy.","May your Eid al-Fitr be blessed!","Moral: Sincere prayer fills the heart with hope and mercy. The wish for blessings, healing, and forgiveness brings a person closer to the Creator.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sacrificial-meat","title":"Sacrificial Meat","paragraphs":["I was in fifth grade. We were not in need of anyone. My father, a builder, had joined a housing cooperative, and we hoped to move into our own home within six or seven months.","One morning we woke up to a terrible shaking. There had been an earthquake. Thankfully, nothing bad had happened to us or to those around us. No one in our building was hurt. But although our house and our lives were safe, this event brought another trouble to our family: as a result of the earthquake, construction work had stopped, and my father was unemployed.","No matter how hard we tried to save, the money my father earned from the repair jobs he found from time to time was not enough to support our household.","My father couldn't find work all winter. He would go with hope and return sad.","A few days before Eid al-Fitr, he came home beaming: someone had offered him work. “They told me to bring my papers,” he said, hurrying out again. “I need to get them there today.”","A few hours had passed when our next-door neighbor came in with a panicked expression on his face.","“Don’t be afraid, but your father has had a minor accident,” he said.","At the hospital, we found him pale, his arm and legs bandaged. A car speeding through a red light hit him. As we prayed that he was alive, ashamed, he tried to hide the tears running from his eyes. “Why did I survive?” he said. “Now I’ve made things even harder for you.”","After a while, my father came home. Our stove wasn’t burning, our house was cold. Several of our neighbors had called the municipality and asked for coal for us. “No way,” they said — we had to register in advance.","Eid al-Fitr came and went without sweets. Despite all the hardships, the weather had warmed a little, and the bandage had come off my father’s arm, though his leg was still bandaged.","Then came Eid al-Adha. I thought to myself: “There are twelve apartments in our building, many of them will sacrifice. At least they will give us a share, and my mother will cook my favorite meat dishes.” As I watched from the window, in the empty lot opposite, the sacrificial animals were slaughtered and skinned, and bowl after bowl of meat was carried to the homes. Every time the door was opened, the delicious smell of meat being fried in the houses spread to our house. But unfortunately, no one brought us a piece of meat.","Meanwhile, my grandmother called from the village to congratulate us on our holiday. She sensed that I was worried by the sound of my voice as she spoke. She asked insistently and anxiously: “Has your father gotten worse?”","“No,” I said quietly. “The neighbors didn’t give us sacrificial meat.”","In the summer months, we would go to the village to my grandmother. She had a cow and five or six chickens. Her cow gave good milk. When she milked it, my grandmother would sing songs to her.","On the third day of the holiday, there was a knock on our door early in the morning. It was my grandmother who came. We welcomed her with joy. She hugged us all, crying. “My lambs, my lambs, I brought you a lot of meat,” she said.","We filled the refrigerator with meat. My sister cut some up to cook, and the delicious smell further stirred the appetite that had been growing for two days. Too hungry to wait for the table, I took a fork, speared a piece, and was blowing on it as I brought it to my mouth when my father’s words to my grandmother almost pierced my ears:","“Ah, mother, ah! Why did you slaughter that cow?”","I felt sick and my head spun. I threw the fork and ran outside. The cow she sang to as she milked it, my grandmother’s hope—had it been slaughtered because I craved meat?","When the table was ready, they took me by the arm and made me sit. Again I dipped the fork in, unwilling and ashamed, but the piece stuck in my throat and tears filled my eyes. “What’s wrong?” they asked.","“My tooth,” I said. “It hurts terribly.”","Moral: A celebration is complete only when others can share in it. In times of abundance, it is our human duty not to forget those who do not have even a piece of meat on their table.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"stoning-the-devil","title":"Stoning the Devil","paragraphs":["During the Hajj, when it was time to cast stones at the devil, one pilgrim began striking himself with the stone instead. A young man nearby asked in surprise:","“Uncle, why are you hitting yourself with that stone?”","The man replied:","“My son, what business do we have with the devil here? First we must stone the devil within ourselves—discipline our nafs and cleanse ourselves.”","Moral: The real struggle is not against the devil outside us, but against one's own uncontrolled desires. Purifying oneself is harder than condemning others, but far more valuable.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dervishs-clothes","title":"The Dervish’s Appearance","paragraphs":["A dervish was once asked:","“Why are your clothes so untidy?”","He replied:","“I spend so much time working on my inner self that I have little time left to worry about my appearance.”","Moral: A person who cultivates the inner life does not judge worth by outward appearance. What truly makes a person beautiful is not clothing, but a pure heart and a luminous spirit.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"idle-devil","title":"The Idle Devil","paragraphs":["A dervish lost in thought suddenly noticed the devil sitting beside him. “Why are you sitting so quietly, not harming anyone?” he asked.","The devil wearily raised his head. “You people cause one another so much suffering that there’s no work left for me.”","Moral: People oppress each other so much that the devil has no work to do. Most evil comes not from outside, but from within us.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"most-beautiful-portion","title":"The Finest Lot","paragraphs":["The dervish was asked:","“What is the finest thing that can fall to a person’s lot?”","The dervish said:","“To meet someone who wants to listen to you.”","Moral: Meeting someone who wants to listen to you from a sincere heart is a great blessing. Feeling that you are listened to is like being loved.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mullah-and-the-scholar","title":"The Mullah and the Scholar","paragraphs":["A visiting scholar came to Tamerlane’s court. After a long conversation through an interpreter, he asked to meet the country’s scholars. Timur gathered all the scholars. None dared debate the foreign scholar. At last, seeing how afraid they were, the Mullah took the debate upon himself.","At the gathering, the scholar drew a circle on the ground. The Mullah divided it in half. Seeing that the scholar stayed silent, he divided it into quarters, claiming three for himself and offering one to the visitor. The scholar nodded. Next, he held out a fist with the back of his hand downward. The Mullah turned his own hand over, pointing his fingers toward the ground. Again, the scholar nodded.","Finally, the visitor pointed to himself and mimed eating. The Mullah took an egg from his pocket and flapped his arms like bird wings. The scholar laughed, kissed his hand, and the Mullah left.","Afterward, Tamerlane asked the visitor through the interpreter, “What did those gestures mean? We understood none of them.”","“Opinions about the world differ. By drawing that circle I said the earth is round and wanted to know your scholar's opinion about it. Mashallah to your scholar,” the scholar explained. “He drew the equator, then divided the globe into four parts, three of them water and one of them land. I asked about plant life, and he showed that plants depend on rain and sunlight.","“Finally, I asked about humans and animals. With the egg, he reminded me to include birds.”","After the visitor had gone, Tamerlane summoned the Mullah for his explanation.","“The scholar?” said the Mullah. “I thought he would be a learned man, but he turned out to be a fool and a glutton. First he drew a circle, meaning a pan of omelet. I divided it in half: ‘You can’t have it all—there’s me to consider.’ He still looked dissatisfied, so I divided it into four and claimed three shares to teach him a lesson about greed. Seeing the omelet slip away, he agreed. Then he raised his hand, asking for a pot of pilaf.","“I turned my hand down to say, ‘If it has a dark crust on top, I’ll eat it.’ Then he showed me his stomach to show he was hungry from traveling. I took out my egg and told him, ‘I’m hungrier than you, and this is all I have. Don’t ask me to share it!’”","Moral: The same sign is read differently in each person’s world. We often see the other in the mirror of our own thoughts.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"gods-pleasure","title":"The Pleasure of Allah","paragraphs":["Khoja Ubaydullah Ahrar says:","“I have explored every path that leads to Allah’s pleasure. The shortest I have found is bringing joy to others.","“I have explored every path that leads away from Allah’s pleasure. The most dangerous I have found is breaking someone’s heart.”","Moral: The shortest path to Allah's pleasure is bringing joy to others, while breaking someone’s heart distances a person from His pleasure.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"power-of-truth","title":"The Strength of Truth","paragraphs":["Truth’s sword does not bend, and its wrist does not twist.","Moral: Truth is unbending and unyielding. Time passes and power changes, but in the end truth finds its place again.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"price-of-a-miracle","title":"The Price of a Miracle","paragraphs":["(A True Story)","Lisa was eight years old when she heard her parents talking about her younger brother, Oliver. All the little girl knew was that her brother was very sick and that her parents had no money for his treatment.","Only a very expensive operation could save Oliver. There was no one to lend them money. Lisa heard her father whisper helplessly to her tearful mother, “Only a miracle can save him now.”","The girl went into her room and took out a glass jar that she had kept in a secret place in the closet. She poured out all the money in the jar on the floor and counted it carefully—she did this several times. When she was finished counting, she carefully put the coins in the jar, closed the lid, and went out the back door to the pharmacy.","At the pharmacy, she waited for the pharmacist to notice her. He was deep in conversation. Although Lisa tried to catch his attention with various movements and sounds, he stayed silent. Finally, she slammed part of her money on the glass counter. He turned, startled.","“What do you want, my girl?” he asked irritably, and without waiting for an answer he added, “I am talking with my brother, whom I have not seen for a long time.”","“I need to talk about my brother too,” Lisa answered with the same irritation. “He’s ill, and I need a miracle to make him better.”","“What? You want a miracle?” the pharmacist asked, even more irritated.","“His name is Oliver. There’s something bad growing in his brain. My parents say only a miracle can save him now. How much does one cost?”","“I’m sorry, but we don’t sell miracles here, my girl. I won’t be able to help you,” the pharmacist said more gently.","“I have money. If it isn’t enough, I’ll find more. Please just tell me the price.”","A smartly dressed man who had been watching the conversation from the sidelines until now—the pharmacist’s brother—leaned down and asked Lisa:","“What sort of miracle does your brother need?”","“I don’t know. I only know that he is truly sick, and my parents say he needs an operation. My parents can’t pay, so I brought my own money.”","“How much do you have?”","“One dollar and eleven cents. That’s all, but if it is needed, I can add a little more.”","“One dollar and eleven cents,” he said with a smile. “What a coincidence. That’s exactly the price of the miracle your brother needs.”","The man took the money from the girl, took her hand, and said:","“Take me home with you. I’d like to meet your brother and your parents. Let’s see whether I have the miracle your brother needs.”","This well-dressed man was Dr. Carlton Armstrong, a renowned neurosurgeon.","The operation was completed without charge, and Oliver soon came home. His parents were overjoyed. “This was a true miracle,” his mother whispered to his father. “I wonder what that operation would have cost.”","Lisa smiled. She knew the price: one dollar and eleven cents, and an unshakable faith in the miracle that would save her brother.","Moral: Faith is a powerful force. Sometimes, when a child’s pure faith meets the kindness of a human being, a miracle is born.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"where-does-calamity-come-from","title":"Where Does Misfortune Come From?","paragraphs":["Someone asked a dervish:","“Where does misfortune come from?”","He replied:","“Examine your eyes. Have you looked at what is forbidden?","“Examine your tongue and ears. Have you spread or listened to gossip?","“Examine your hands. Have you taken what is forbidden or earned your living dishonestly?","“Examine your feet. Have they carried you where you should not go?”","Moral: The root of what happens to us can often be found in our own actions. Check your eyes, your tongue, your hands, and your steps.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-are-you-waiting-for-the-last-day-of-the-world","title":"Why Wait for the End of the World?","paragraphs":["One day, a professor at a university in America addressed his students as follows while starting a class:","“Imagine for a moment that today is the last day of the world. Tomorrow at this time everything will already be over. You have no chance of being saved. If so, what would you do?”","The students gave different answers:","“I would pray and ask Allah to forgive me.”","“I would say goodbye to everyone I love.”","“I would spend the day with my family.”","“I would visit my mother or my father.”","“I would eat all my favorite foods one last time.”","“I’d sleep as long as I wanted.”","“I would walk through the forest one last time and enjoy the greenery.”","“I would watch the sunrise and sunset one last time.”","“I would watch the stars in the evening.”","“I would cook my favorite dish and invite everyone I love to dinner.”","“I would go to the place I have most wanted to visit and wait for death there.”","“I’d find the people I’ve hurt and ask their forgiveness, and so on.”","The professor wrote the answers on the board, then smiled.","“Why wait for the end of the world to do these things?”","Moral: Do not wait for the last day to do good. Tell the people you love that you love them, forgive, and value your dear ones today.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","blurb":"Parental love, raising children, and building a home.","count":22,"stories":[{"stem":"fathers-footprints","title":"A Father’s Footsteps","paragraphs":["A father said to his son:","“Son, watch your step carefully.”","The boy replied:","“You’d better watch yours, Dad. I’m following in your footsteps.”","Moral: The most powerful form of guidance is personal example. Children often imitate not what adults say, but the steps they take.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"festive-gift","title":"A Festive Gift","paragraphs":["“Wise elder,” I asked, “what should I buy for the holiday?”","He replied:","“Cheer a few people up. Make a few children smile. And, if you can, earn the prayers of a few poor people.”","Moral: The most precious gift is measured not by money, but by kindness and attention. What makes a celebration meaningful is sometimes not what you buy, but what you give.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mothers-love","title":"A Mother’s Love","paragraphs":["Erich Fromm","I am one year old—completely happy, because my mother is always beside me.","I am two—my mother is with me only sometimes. I wonder what is happening.","I am three—my mother is at work, and my nanny is with me.","I am five—my mother is in a meeting, and my grandmother is with me.","I am seven—my mother is at a seminar; I am at school with my teacher.","I am thirteen—a teenager. My mother still has to work, and my friends are beside me.","I am seventeen—my mother comes home exhausted. I am at school, in class, or at a café with friends.","I am twenty—young. My mother is not at home, but my friends are with me.","I am twenty-three—I have started working. My mother is home now, while I am at work.","I am thirty—and I wonder: when did my mother and I last spend a lovely time together? There is something strange here.","I am thirty-five—married. My mother lives in her home, and I in mine.","I am forty—my mother waits for me just as I once waited for her as a child.","I am fifty—I am older now, but one wish remains unchanged: to join my heart to her heart, embrace her, and lay my head on her tired knees.","I am fifty-five—I am now always beside a mother, the mother of my children. My own mother has passed away, and I live with the ache of missing her.","In the daily course of life, our most serious negligence is sometimes not giving enough time to our children and those we love. Children are the most precious characters in the magnificent film of life that changes rapidly before our eyes. If we fail to notice them growing, those passing moments can never be recovered.","Life has no stop, start, forward, or back buttons that can show past scenes again. May we understand that while there is still time.","Love your child today. Hold them, take their hand, and remain beside them. Do not miss this day of his childhood, because tomorrow will already be another day.","“A mother’s love is a selfless, protective, and warm refuge. Precisely because it is unconditional, it cannot be obtained by force.”","Moral: A mother’s love is unconditional and irreplaceable. Make time for those you love today, because time is the most precious thing we can never recover.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mothers-advice-fakir-baykurt-never-forgot","title":"The Mother’s Advice Fakir Baykurt Never Forgot","paragraphs":["As a child, the Turkish writer Fakir Baykurt (1929–1999) once said to his mother, “Mother, my heart wants tea — come, let’s drink a cup together.” She took him to Topal Hüseyin’s teahouse and ordered a cup for him.","Eager to drink, he took a large mouthful before realizing how hot it was. Startled by the pain, he dropped the cup.","Because it fell on the earth, the glass did not break. He expected his mother to slap him, but she simply asked for another cup.","This time Baykurt drank the second tea while blowing on it. For years afterward, he asked why she had not slapped him. She never answered.","Years later his mother, Elif Khanum, attended a lesson at the village school where he taught. He recalled:","“During the lesson I grew agitated, worked myself up, and roared. Afterward, outside, I asked, ‘Well, Mother, what did you think of my teaching?’","“‘Eh, what can I say, son — it wasn’t bad.’","“‘The inspectors praise me and give me good marks, and all you say is that it wasn’t bad?’","“She said these words, which could be a lesson for everyone: ‘For years you’ve asked why I didn’t slap you over the tea. Now listen.","“‘If I had lost my temper on the day you spilled the tea, the lion inside you would have withdrawn in hurt. If I had beaten you, that lion would have died. Then you could not have become such a teacher. Behave as I did, and stay calm. Don’t try to kill the lion inside these children with a beating.’”","Moral: Don't kill the \"lion\" inside a child — his self-confidence — by beating him. True upbringing is given not through fear, but through patience and love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"father-and-son","title":"Father and Son","paragraphs":["After a bitter argument with his father, a young man left home. Hurt and angry, his father forbade even his son's name to be mentioned in the house. Each night, his mother sat on his bed and fell asleep holding his pillow close, breathing in its familiar scent.","“I miss our son,” she pleaded. “Let’s find him and bring him home.” But her husband refused.","A long time passed.","One day, unable to bear seeing his wife always in tears, the father took a slip of paper from his pocket and handed it to her.","“You’ll find him at this address,” he said. “But don’t tell him I gave it to you.”","He said a few more things, but she seemed not to hear him; only the address stayed in her mind. Overjoyed, she hurried out. The address led to a repair shop, where she found her son working in overalls. For a few minutes she watched him through her tears, then walked toward him. He had heard nothing from his family and believed they had forgotten him. Startled to see her, he pulled her into a tight embrace.","He asked after everyone except his father. Finally, he said:","“How is that man? Is he still angry?”","His mother avoided the question.","“Come home, son,” she said.","“No, Mother. I can’t live under the same roof as him again.” He turned back to his work.","She watched him for a while, then called him over to give him the sweets she had prepared.","He accepted them, but said:","“Please don’t insist. I’m not coming home. He hasn’t looked for me once. He doesn’t care about me.”","His mother lowered her head.","“Well, if you’re determined to stay, that’s your choice. But your father asked me to tell you to keep away from the friend you’ve been spending time with this past month. He says that person is no good for you, and that you should make peace with your old friend.”","Her son froze in astonishment.","Back at home, the mother confronted her husband.","“If you knew where he was, why didn’t you tell me? Is that why you seemed so calm?”","The father, who had always seemed irritable, softened and tears ran from his eyes.","“He is my very soul,” he said. “My soul.”","“How long have you known?” she asked.","“Since the day he left. Sometimes I went during my lunch break to watch from a distance and to see what he was eating and drinking. That’s why I came home late some evenings too. I went to see what he was doing and who he was with.”","As the parents held each other and cried, someone knocked. The mother wiped her eyes and opened the door.","Their son stood there. He kissed their hands and embraced them. Now he understood: the father he thought had forgotten him had never stopped watching over him.","Moral: Don't hide your love, show it. Sometimes a timely step can prevent years of resentment.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"from-a-wise-fathers-advice-to-his-son","title":"From a Wise Father’s Advice to His Son","paragraphs":["My beloved son,","Cultivate the understanding that helps you build good relationships. It is an inexhaustible source of happiness and joy.","Our desire for life is never fully satisfied. Whether a life is long or short does not determine its worth.","Fruit is ready to fall only when it is ripe. In the same way, people have always preferred those who have lived long and gained experience.","In youth, people often do wrong without thinking. Later, when they remember them, those wrongs pierce their hearts like a dagger and weigh on their souls.","Those who follow an honest path in youth earn respect in old age.","Strength is a good thing, but it must be guided by reason.","Allah, the Creator of all worlds, did not place us here to live forever. He willed that at the end of life a person taste death.","By Allah’s leave, two habits have kept me healthy up to this age. I have tested them for years: eating in moderation, and walking so that what I eat can be digested.","I also want you to understand that there are three kinds of people.","The first think clearly and can more or less see the future and think about it.","The second cannot tell whether their path is straight or crooked, and they come to that state through the influence of others, not by their own wish, but accept advice and listen when it is given.","The third are aware of nothing, yet believe they know everything and refuse all advice. They are the most unfortunate of all.","My son,","If Almighty Allah has made you one of the people I placed in the first group, I will rejoice.","If you belong to the second, listen to good advice.","Do not become one of the third. Such people earn favor neither with Allah nor with others.","Moral: Wisdom is the source of happiness, and strength is the servant of wisdom. He who listens to advice will be exalted; but he who thinks he knows everything will perish.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"grandfather-and-grandson-at-the-market","title":"Grandfather and Grandson at the Market","paragraphs":["A grandfather took his grandson to the supermarket. As they filled their basket, the boy kept shouting, “Buy me this! Buy me that!” Each time, his grandfather said, “Stay calm, Arif. We’ll go home as soon as we’ve finished.” Finally, his frustration reached its limit, and the boy grabbed a Kinder Surprise egg from a shelf and crushed it underfoot. “Stay calm, Arif,” the grandfather repeated. Then he turned to an employee. “I’m very sorry. I’ll pay for that too.”","After they paid and left, a woman who had watched them approached the grandfather. “I admired how you treated Arif. You are a very patient person. Bless you!” she said. The grandfather thanked her and smiled. “The boy’s name is Rafiq. I’m Arif.”","Moral: Being able to control yourself in a tense moment is a sign of true strength. Keeping calm sometimes means mastering yourself, not others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hold-my-hand","title":"Hold My Hand","paragraphs":["As a father and his young daughter crossed a bridge, he grew anxious.","“Hold my hand tightly, sweetheart, so you don’t fall into the river.”","The little girl replied:","“No, Dad. You hold mine.”","Puzzled, he asked:","“What’s the difference?”","“There’s a big difference. If I am holding your hand and something happens to me, I might let go. But if you hold mine, I know you won’t let go, whatever happens.”","Moral: The power of love is in not letting go in a difficult moment. Do not simply wait for those you love to reach for you; reach out and hold on to them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-am-tired-mother","title":"I’m Tired, Mom","paragraphs":["“I’m tired, Mom.”","“Of what, son?”","“Everything. Work, friends, going out, trying to please everyone. I’m exhausted.”","“Rest your head in my lap, son. Let it all go for a moment. Give your pains to me.”","“Thank you, Mom. I’m so glad you’re here. With you, I forget both my age and everything I have lived through. May you live long.”","“A mother is the one who gives life, son. I am always beside you.”","“Mother, we tire you a great deal too. Forgive us.”","“You don’t wear me out. I’m tired because I no longer have a mother of my own. There’s no lap where I can rest my head. Don’t you wear yourself out, son. Know the worth of what you have.”","Moral: A mother’s embrace is the most comfortable refuge in the world. Whoever has a mother always has a place to lay their weariness. Know the worth of what you have.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mother-and-son","title":"Mother and Son","paragraphs":["The man set the suitcases against the wall, straightened up, and took a deep breath. “It’s a lovely place, Mom.”","“Of course it is, son,” she replied. For a while they stood silently, looking around the room.","Then the woman opened her small suitcase and took out her nightgown, slippers, a few towels, and a framed picture. She placed the picture on the table next to her bed.","In the photograph, a small boy in school uniform stood stiffly, his eyes brimming with tears and his lips pressed together.","The man approached the window and opened the curtain. A pale daylight filled the room. It was freezing outside. He leaned his hands on the heater and watched the muddy cars passing by for a while. Then, without even turning his head, he asked, “Is this closet enough for you, Mom?”","Lost in thought, she did not even hear her son. Seated on the bed, leaning slightly to one side, she was gazing at the frightened boy in the worn frame.","It was a picture they had taken on the first day of school.","The memory returned. The day before school began, she had spent hours telling him how wonderful it would be. Even so, she had barely persuaded him to enter the classroom. She waited all day, reassuring him at every break: “I’m here, son. I’m waiting for you.” Each time he held her hand even tighter and cried. “Don’t leave me, Mom. I’m scared.”","The sun was setting. Her son took his hands off the heater and leaned on the misted window. “Shall we open the other suitcase and put your things away?”","“Don’t let me keep you. I can do it. There isn’t much.” She reached out her trembling hands and took her son’s hand. For a moment she seemed about to say more. Then she swallowed. “The children will be worried too.”","The door closed with a creak. She was left alone with the peeling paint and with the frightened son in the photograph, standing like a soldier. After sitting on the bed a little, she put on her slippers and went to the window. Below, her son was getting into his car.","The old woman was afraid. Despite the fact that she had listened for hours the day before to how beautiful this place was, she was still afraid.","His handprint still marked the glass. She pressed her cheek against it, her eyes filled and her lips pressed together.","“Don't leave me, son, I'm so scared,” she whispered.","Her words seemed to melt into the misted glass and flow toward her son as he waved from the car.","That night, those who lived on the same floor heard her crying. Sobs wandering the corridors at night were the only sound they had grown used to at the nursing home.","Moral: The child who once held his mother’s hand may grow up and leave her alone. Loyalty to a parent is not only material care, but not depriving them of love and attention.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"only-my-mother-would-weep","title":"Only My Mother Weeps","paragraphs":["Only my mother’s tears are real; all the rest are pretense.","Moral: In the world you may see much love, but a mother’s selfless, sincere love is irreplaceable.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"raising-children","title":"Parenting","paragraphs":["A boy constantly defied his parents, neglected his schoolwork, and did not fully complete his homework. Even when his mother beat him and his father shut him in a room as punishment, it did no good.","One day, on the way to school, his mother saw a crowd around a bear trainer. As the man beat a tambourine, a bear with a ring through its nose performed for the spectators.","“I can’t manage my own child,” she thought, “yet this man can train a bear.”","She approached him. “How do you make that wild animal obey? I can’t get my son to listen.”","“Everything has its method,” he said with a smile. “I have mine.”","“Then please help me.”","The trainer looked at the boy. “There is no difficult task. Entrust him to me for a week, and I’ll make him obedient.”","The woman was happy and started taking the boy to the bear trainer every day.","Though only a few days had passed, his behavior slowly began to improve, and he no longer objected to his mother. After a week the boy became exactly as she wanted, and she was delighted. She thanked the trainer and offered him her blessings.","The neighbors were very surprised by this. That disobedient, rebellious boy was gone, and in his place was a calm, peaceful, well-mannered boy.","His teacher was pleased with the change and congratulated the mother. Soon, however, a different problem emerged. Though he was no longer as arrogant and angry as before, he stole classmates’ belongings, did secret things, and lied in plain sight. The teacher phoned the mother and said, “I don’t know what you will do, but your child has become very strange. He has started stealing and lying.” Desperate, she went looking for the trainer, but he had left.","A neighbor suggested that she consult a wise, kindhearted man.","She took her son to him and explained. “Let him visit me for a week,” he said. “I’ll see what I can do.” She agreed.","She began bringing the boy to him.","After two meetings, the man spoke to her with concern. “What have you done to this child? I see animal traits in him.” She was surprised. “I haven’t done anything. I only used to take him to the bear trainer because he was so naughty.”","“An animal trainer?” he exclaimed. “Can an animal trainer raise a child? Animals are trained with fear, and people with conscience. Animals are sometimes brought to order by beating, cursing, and punishment; if that fails, they are shut in a dark room and left helpless. How could you entrust your own child to an animal trainer?”","“Upbringing is not pressing the child, but finding the way to his heart,” he continued. “Take your child and go home. Be close to him, kiss and embrace him, listen to him, and do not withhold your love. As he sees that he is loved, his trust in you will grow and his manner will soften.”","“The remedy for your child is neither the bear trainer nor I; it is only you, the parents.”","Moral: A child is raised not with fear, but with love. A child is raised not by a trainer or an outsider, but by a loving parent.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"puppies-for-sale","title":"Puppies for Sale","paragraphs":["A little boy entered the shop after seeing a sign in the window saying “We have puppies for sale.”","“How much do you sell puppies for?” he asked.","“Between thirty and fifty dollars. Each one has a different price,” said the shopkeeper.","The boy emptied his pocket onto the counter and carefully counted the money.","“I only have thirteen dollars and twenty-seven cents,” he said, disappointed. Then he took a deep breath.","“Can I at least see the puppies?”","The shopkeeper smiled and called to his wife. She came from the back room with five plump, fluffy puppies following her. One struggled to keep up. The boy fixed his eyes on that puppy.","“What’s wrong with him? Why can’t he walk?” he asked.","“The vet says he was born with a congenitally defective fibula. Walking will always be difficult for him.”","The boy’s face lit up.","“That’s the puppy I want.”","“I don’t think you’ll want to buy him, my child. You can have him for free.”","The little boy was very angry. He looked the shopkeeper straight in the eye and said:","“I don’t want him for free. He’s worth just as much as the others. I’ll give you what I have now, then bring five dollars every week until I’ve paid thirty.”","The shopkeeper said:","“You won’t really want that puppy, son. He won’t be able to run and play with you like the others.”","“But we should give him a chance too. He is as valuable as the other puppies.” The boy bent down and raised his trouser leg, revealing a leg weakened by polio and supported by metal braces. He looked up and spoke softly.","“I can’t run and play either. This puppy and I both need friends who will understand us and stand by us.”","Moral: Every living thing needs a friend who will understand and support it. The one who is different is just as valuable as the others; we need to give him a chance.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"rotten-seed","title":"The Rotten Seed","paragraphs":["After his wife died, the father scorned his young son who constantly suffered from illnesses, calling him a “rotten seed,” and sent him to an orphanage.","Years passed.","One day he saw his grown son outside a school. He could not believe his eyes. The boy had grown healthy and strong. Filled with regret, the father stared at his bright eyes.","“I did not believe you would recover or that you would become anybody. You have become a strapping wrestler. How did this miracle happen? How did you get free of the illnesses?”","With moist eyes he fixed his gaze on the father who had once scorned him, and pointed to the elderly woman who was approaching with a smile.","“When you sent me away, you said a rotten seed could never grow. She adopted me and never stopped caring for me. She told me, ‘Every seed watered with love will sprout.’”","Moral: Words either build or destroy. It is possible to elevate or destroy someone with a single word—every seed watered with love will germinate.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"spinach","title":"Spinach","paragraphs":["We lived in a remote village without even a proper road. My mother kept cooking spinach. As a child, I thought that after my father died, my mother’s mind had become confused.","“You need to eat a lot, it gives strength to the body,” my mother would say, and for breakfast, lunch, and dinner, she would only feed us spinach, and always cornbread alongside it. I hated spinach already, I couldn’t stand to see this vegetable.","“When I’m grown and have a job and a family, there’ll be no spinach in my house,” I told her. She said she looked forward to that day and promised to dance at my wedding, however old she was.","The day came, I moved from our village to the city to study in high school. I somehow managed to make ends meet with the small scholarship I received. Once a month, when I came home, I didn't want to eat a spoonful of the spinach dish my mother put on the table. I only wanted to go back to the dormitory and eat delicious food.","I completed university, a master’s degree, and a doctorate with government loans. By then, my mother was old and bent. When I came to the village with the girl I was going to marry and we held the wedding, she kept her promise and danced at my wedding, tears streaming down her face. I did not understand those tears then.","Years passed. My mother passed away two years ago.","One day, in my mother’s old chest I found the memoir notebook she kept in those days. On one page she had written: “All I had left were spinach seeds. I kept planting spinach, and a little corn as well. There was no money. I kept cooking this food so my children would not go hungry. But I did not complain. I told them I served spinach three times a day because it was good for them. Saying it broke my heart. For my children, this was all I could do. I love them more than my own life.”","At fifty, I learned to love spinach. My wife keeps asking in surprise why I buy spinach so often for the house. She thinks I love spinach. She does not know that its smell brings back the sacrifices my mother made to raise me and the sight of her dancing through tears at my wedding.","Moral: Years later, we often understand a mother’s sacrifice from the time when she had nothing. Love is sometimes hidden in a plate of simple food.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"fallen-teeth","title":"Fallen Teeth","paragraphs":["A king once dreamed that all his teeth had fallen out. He summoned an interpreter of dreams and asked what the dream meant.","The interpreter said:","“Your Majesty, all your relatives will die before you.”","The king was angered by the answer and sent him away. Then he called another interpreter and asked the same question.","The second man replied:","“Your Majesty, you will outlive everyone else in your family.”","Very pleased with the answer, the king rewarded the man, and sent him on his way.","Moral: The same truth can be spoken harshly or with tact. Words can wound or heal; wisdom lies in knowing how to speak them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"most-beautiful-gift","title":"The Most Beautiful Gift","paragraphs":["For a father — honor,","For a mother — respect,","For a child — compassion,","For a spouse — love,","For a friend — loyalty,","For society — service,","For the deceased — prayer,","For the world — humanity,","For the hereafter — goodness in this world.","Moral: The most beautiful gift is to show each person the care they need: honor for a father, respect for a mother, loyalty to a friend, service to society.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"builder-of-the-nest","title":"The Nest Builder","paragraphs":["The female bird builds the nest.","Moral: A woman’s work builds a home and holds a family together. That work deserves recognition and appreciation.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"nails-lasting-lesson","title":"The story of a nail","paragraphs":["In a village there lived a boy who was very quick-tempered and impatient. Even the slightest remark would make him angry, and when he lost his temper, he did not hesitate to hurt the person in front of him.","His father was deeply concerned about this side of his son's character. One day, he gave him a bag of nails and said:","‘My son, whenever you get angry with someone and say something hurtful, hammer one of these nails into the fence around the yard.’","At first, the boy thought this was pointless. But the next day he became angry and hammered a nail into the fence. Then he lost his temper again and hammered in another. As the days passed, the number of nails in the fence began to grow.","After a while, the boy began to pay attention to his behavior. Reaching for a nail every time he became angry made him think. At last, one day he went to his father and said:","‘Father, I will try not to lose my temper anymore.’","His father smiled and replied:","‘Very good. Now, for every day that you control yourself and hurt no one, pull one nail out of the fence.’","The boy did as he was told. Days passed, then months, and at last all the nails had been removed from the fence.","The boy called out joyfully to his father:","‘Father, look! I have pulled out all the nails.’","His father took him over to the fence and said quietly:","‘You have removed all the nails. But look: the marks they left are still there. The same thing happens when you get angry and lash out at someone with your words. You may apologize afterward, you may feel sorry, and that person may even forgive you. But a wound in the heart does not always heal without a scar.’","The boy looked at the holes in the fence for a long time.","From that day on, when he became angry, he learned first to remain silent and then to think.","Moral: A word spoken is like a nail: it is driven in during a moment of anger, but its mark may remain for a long time. That is why we must think before speaking and pause before causing hurt.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"value-of-your-family","title":"The Value of Your Family","paragraphs":["One evening, a little boy asked his father, “How much do you earn in an hour?” The father wanted to know why. “I’m just curious,” the boy said. Reluctantly, his father answered, “A hundred dollars.”","The boy looked down, then asked, “Could you lend me fifty?” His father grew angry, assuming he wanted a pointless toy or something unnecessary. He refused and sent the boy to bed. The child went to his room and quietly closed the door.","After pacing for a while, the father calmed down. He had not even asked what the money was for. Feeling guilty, he knocked on his son’s door. “Are you awake?” The boy said he was still awake.","He went in and apologized. “I had a difficult day and took it out on you. Here are the fifty dollars. Spend them as you wish.” The boy joyfully thanked him and pulled some money from beneath his pillow. As he counted it, his father’s irritation returned. “Why ask for more when you already have money?”","“Because I didn’t have enough. Now I have exactly one hundred dollars.” “Can I buy an hour of your time? Could you come home early tomorrow and eat with me?”","These words deeply moved the father, and his eyes filled with tears. He hugged his little son and apologized to him.","Moral: The greatest need of children is the attention, love, and time spent with them by their parents.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-am-waiting-for-the-teeth","title":"Waiting for the Teeth","paragraphs":["On a cold winter evening, an elderly couple entered McDonald’s. The husband bought one hamburger, a large portion of fries, and a large Coke. At their table, he cut the burger in half and put that half in front of his wife, counted the fries one by one, and gave her half. Then he took a sip of Coke and passed it to his wife.","Other customers watched sympathetically. “Poor things. They’re sharing one meal.”","But while the man ate eagerly, his wife left her food untouched, taking only an occasional sip of Coke.","At last one young employee’s patience ran out, and he approached the couple.","“Excuse me, sir. I’ve been watching you for a while: you are eating, but your wife hasn’t touched the food. If she doesn’t like it, could I bring her something else?”","The elderly man said:","“Thank you, but we’re quite happy. We’ve been married for sixty years. We share everything.”","Still puzzled, the employee turned to the woman.","“Then why aren’t you eating? What are you waiting for?”","The old woman pointed to her husband and replied:","“The teeth, dear. I’m waiting for the teeth.”","Moral: No one should be left in need of the one they love. May Allah not leave anyone helpless, even in their own family.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"weeds-must-be-pulled-from-the-root","title":"Weeds","paragraphs":["When we were schoolchildren, we spent the summer holidays with our family in our garden. My late father wanted to accustom us to hard work, so he left us chores while he went to work. We watered the melons he had planted. We carried drinking water from the well in our neighbor Uncle Aghasalim’s garden, since our own well water was unsuitable.","We learned to turn the soil and clear the weeds, but they were not so easy to clear. One day, Father explained, “You must work deeper. Until you remove the roots, the job isn’t finished.”","Moral: Deal with the roots of a problem and a bad habit. Clearing away only what shows above the surface does not stop it from returning.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","blurb":"Love, loyalty, and the trials of a shared life.","count":20,"stories":[{"stem":"beloved-is-one-who-gives-love-to-people","title":"Those Who Give Love Receive Love","paragraphs":["Many years ago in China, a young woman named Li-Li married and moved in with her husband and mother-in-law. She soon found it difficult to get along with her mother-in-law. Their personalities were very different, and they argued frequently. These quarrels offended traditional expectations of family harmony and upset everyone around them.","After several months, the constant fighting had made life unbearable for Li-Li and her husband. Hoping to find a way out, she went to an old friend of her father’s, a spice merchant, and told him her troubles. He prepared an herbal mixture and told her to add a little to her mother-in-law’s food each day for three months. He said the poison would act so gradually that no one would suspect the daughter-in-law of the mother-in-law’s death.","To avoid arousing suspicion, especially her husband’s, he told Li-Li to treat her mother-in-law kindly and cook the most delicious dishes for her. Delighted, Li-Li went home and followed his instructions.","Each day she prepared delicious meals and added a little of the supposed poison. She was careful to be kind, so as not to arouse suspicion. In time, her mother-in-law’s attitude changed, and she began treating Li-Li like her own daughter. Overcome with remorse, Li-Li returned to the spice merchant and begged him for an antidote. She no longer wanted her mother-in-law to die.","The old man laughed gently. “Dear Li-Li, all I gave you was vitamins. The real poison was in your mind. As you treated her with kindness, that poison gave way to love. Now you have become like mother and daughter.”","Moral: The real poison often lies in our own thoughts. When we treat others with love, hatred can fade and enemies can become friends.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"advice-for-those-who-marry","title":"Advice for Newlyweds","paragraphs":["Wise elders offered the following counsel to a young couple beginning married life:","“Do not compare your marriage with anyone else’s. Do not say, ‘Look at what that person’s husband is like,’ or ‘Look at what that person’s wife is like.’","Do not look for a flawless person; none of us is flawless. Do not dig into each other’s past—let what has passed stay in the past.","Do not expect your spouse to read your mind. No one can. Speak openly about your wishes and concerns, and try to resolve difficulties through conversation.","Accept that alongside your shared life, each of you has a private world. Hedgehogs draw only as close as their spines allow; if they move too near, they hurt one another. Respect each other’s personal boundaries.","In marriage, be neither owner nor slave, but companions on the same road.","Do not promise, ‘Everything will always be wonderful.’ Say instead, ‘When life becomes difficult, I will be beside you.’","Even if you have been upset with each other during the day, remember to say good night before going to sleep. Let tomorrow begin with reconciliation.”","Moral: A happy marriage rests not on comparison or the pursuit of perfection, but on open communication, mutual respect, and care for each other’s personal boundaries.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-not-let-your-hearts-part","title":"Do Not Let Your Hearts Drift Apart","paragraphs":["Why do people shout when they argue? If the person is right next to you, why do you need to shout? Can't you talk to him calmly? Why do you shout when you get angry?","When people quarrel, their hearts draw apart. They have to shout in order to cross that distance and hear each other. The angrier they become, the louder they shout.","But what happens when people fall in love?","They do not shout; on the contrary, they speak softly. Their hearts are very close, and the distance between them is very small.","What happens when their love grows stronger?","They no longer need to speak loudly; they whisper, and love draws them closer still. In the end even a whisper is unnecessary: they look at each other and understand everything without words. Two people who love each other become this close.","So when you argue, do not let your hearts drift apart. Avoid words that widen the distance between you. One day, it may become so great that you cannot find your way back.","Moral: Shouting reflects the distance between hearts. Even in an argument, choose words that leave a way back to each other.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"early-marriage","title":"Early Marriage","paragraphs":["I had just graduated from university and begun teaching. One day, I was in the staff room marking exam papers. I was correcting some of the girls’ wrong answers so that they would get a good grade and move up to the next class.","A man came in and asked why I was changing their answers. “If these girls fail,” I explained, “their fathers will take them out of school and marry them off at twelve or thirteen. If they move up, their marriage age will be at least fifteen or sixteen. In that time, even the least motivated student is bound to learn something.”","About half an hour later, the principal called me in and asked what I had been doing in the staff room. I told him I had been marking papers.","“You met someone in the staff room. What did you say to him?” he asked.","“Did he complain about me?” I asked.","“Quite the opposite. He told me he had come to inspect the school, but that there was no need.”","“Why not?” I asked in surprise.","“He said, ‘You have a wise young teacher. Please pass on my thanks.’”","Moral: A teacher’s small act of care can change a girl’s fate. Early marriage can rob children of their education; we must not turn a blind eye to it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"friendship-and-love","title":"Friendship and Love","paragraphs":["Write your quarrels on leaves, so that when autumn comes the leaves dry and fall, and the quarrel disappears.","Write your anger on clouds, so that rain falls, the clouds scatter, and the anger disappears.","Write your hatred on snow, so that the sun comes out, the snow melts, and the hatred disappears.","Write friendship and love on the hearts of newborn children, so that as they grow, both spread throughout the world.","Moral: Write quarrels, anger, and hatred on passing things so they disappear with them. Write friendship and love on hearts, so that they live and spread through the world.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-kiss-your-eyes","title":"I Kiss Your Eyes","paragraphs":["Do you know why it is said \"I kiss your eyes\"?","The eyes suffer the pain of longing most of all. That is why they most deserve the reunion.","Moral: The one who has longed the most deserves the reunion most. Love repays the one who has borne its labor.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-love-you","title":"I Love You","paragraphs":["They say a nightingale fell in love with a white rose… Every night he would go and perch beside her and declare his love. But the white rose ignored his feelings and paid no heed to his devotion.","One day the nightingale gathered all his courage, perched beside the white rose again, and said he loved her madly. But the rose snapped: “Leave me alone — I don’t love you!” and scolded him. However much he insisted, the rose rejected him.","The love was so strong that the nightingale, shaken by despair, begged the rose’s forgiveness, pulled a thorn from her, drove it into his own heart with all his strength, and dyed the pure white rose red with his blood!","The rose understood, though late, that he had truly loved her very much. But what was the use? It was already too late. Beneath the branches of the rose dyed red, the nightingale closed his eyes forever.","Since that day, red roses have meant “I LOVE YOU.”","Moral: Affection may go unrecognized until it is too late. Know the worth of the one you love in time, so you never have to say it is already too late.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"loving-from-afar","title":"Loving from Afar","paragraphs":["Two friends were talking.","“I think I’ve fallen for someone.”","“That’s wonderful! Does she know?”","“No.”","“Why don’t you go up to her and tell her?”","“I would, but I’m afraid of losing my peace. Right where I stand I’ll get into trouble and fall in love. We’d start going to the movies and the theater, walking hand in hand. Then there might be quarrels, apologies, ‘Why did you look at her?’ and ‘Why did you look at this one?’ If we’re going to break up in the end, why go through it all?”","“It’s better that I just love her from afar.”","Moral: Fear of losing someone can keep us from expressing our feelings. Yet a love never lived is a loss of its own.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"purple-jacket","title":"The Purple Chepken","paragraphs":["In ancient times there was a custom in Turkish villages: a bride’s dowry included, along with other items, a purple chepken, a traditional jacket. It lay at the bottom of her chest, with the hope that she would never wear it.","The purple chepken was a garment that could ruin a man. If a woman put it on and went out onto the roof, the chimney, or the village square — anywhere everyone could see — it was a public appeal for help. It meant: “I do not love my husband, he torments me, I married unwillingly, I want a divorce, I am in a grave situation, help me.” The villagers were expected to protect her, while her husband faced public shame.","The custom encouraged husbands to settle family grievances without violence, with kindness, respect, and compromise. They knew what it would mean if their wives wore the purple jacket.","A man whose wife made this appeal faced divorce and found it difficult to marry again. Everyone knew that a man who drove his wife to wear the purple chepken could not be a husband. No one would give a daughter again to a man who showed a woman no respect and used violence. It is said that, for this reason, the number of women subjected to violence was almost zero.","When a woman appeared in the jacket, flowing waters would stop, and daily work would stop. Women put aside their milking, spinning, and weaving. Mothers and grandmothers gathered to defend her.","Music and celebrations fell silent. Her distress was treated with the gravity of a death in the community.","Her husband faced such disgrace that he could not go outside, could not go to the teahouse, and nobody would look him in the face.","Moral: Violence against women is unacceptable. When society considers it a duty of conscience to protect women, there is no room for oppression.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"rose","title":"The Rose","paragraphs":["If you water a rose too much, it will rot; if you water it too little, it will wither; if you water it right, it will live.","Moral: In both love and upbringing, measure is essential. Too much control can harm, just as neglect can; attentive care helps life flourish.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"beware-of-those-you-love","title":"Take Care of Those You Love","paragraphs":["On a sunny day in the park, a woman told the man beside her that the boy in the red shirt on the swings was her son. The man smiled and said that the boy in blue was his son, Jamil.","After a while, he checked his watch and called:","“Jamil, it is time to go home.”","“Five more minutes, Dad, please!”","The father agreed. Later he called again, but the boy asked for another five minutes. Once more, the man said yes.","The woman remarked admiringly:","“You are a very patient father.”","The man answered with a sorrowful smile:","“This isn’t patience. Last year my older son, Kamal, was hit by a drunk driver on a nearby road. I had never given him enough of my time. Now I would give anything for five more minutes with him. I promised myself I wouldn’t make the same mistake with Jamil.”","“He thinks that each time he has gained five more minutes of play. But I am the one who gains: I live the happiness of watching my son for five more minutes.”","Moral: Every extra moment with those you love is a gift. Before it is too late, give them five more minutes today.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-breaks-a-marriage","title":"An Eraser for Marriage","paragraphs":["This is what happened between a father and his newly married young son.","The father asked his newly married son for a sheet of white paper, a pencil, and an eraser.","“Why?”","“Just bring them.”","The son found paper and a pencil, but no eraser.","“Then go and buy one.”","Puzzled, the son bought an eraser and sat down beside him.","“Write something.”","“What should I write?”","“Anything you like.”","The son wrote a sentence.","“Now erase it.”","He erased it.","“Write another sentence.”","“For God’s sake, father, what do you want?”","“Write one more.”","The son wrote again, and his father asked him to erase it.","After repeating this several times, the father asked:","“Is the paper still white?”","“Yes. But what does that mean?”","His father rested a hand on his shoulder.","“A marriage needs an eraser too, son. You must be able to forgive some of your spouse’s mistakes and let them pass. Your spouse must also have such an eraser, so that your mistakes can be erased. Otherwise, the page you share will soon be covered with them.”","“If you want love and respect, offer them to each other. And remember the eraser.”","Moral: Marriage is the art of forgiveness. For love and respect to continue, both parties must be able to \"erase\" each other's flaws.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mother-of-pearl-flower","title":"The Mother-of-Pearl Flower","paragraphs":["The divorce of an eighty-year-old couple was being heard, and their condition saddened everyone. The woman was firmly silent, and her eyes were sunken from crying. The judge gently asked:","“Tell me, auntie, why do you want a divorce?”","After taking a deep breath, the old woman began to speak in a choked voice:","“This man has worn me out; for exactly fifty years he has made me weary of life,” she said, and fell silent.","The courtroom fell silent, interrupted only by a journalist’s camera flash. Who knows what headline the papers would give this after fifty years of life. Many journalists were following the trial. What would she say? Everyone was listening. The old woman's eyes filled, and she continued: “I had a mother-of-pearl flower that I loved very much. He doesn’t know. I grew it from a leaf taken from flowers he gave me fifty years ago. We never had children, so I regarded this flower as my child. One day, it began to wither.”","“I promised to get up before dawn every night to water it. In fifty years, he never once got up at night and said, ‘Let me water this flower.’ Until last night. Last night my strength gave out, I had fallen asleep, and I could not wake. I spent fifty years with such a man. I have given him my life, my hopes, everything, and received nothing in return. I assure you, I can live my life better without him.”","The judge turned to the elder:","“Do you have anything to say, grandfather?”","Leaning on his cane, with a guilty look on his face, he turned to the judge and began:","“I was a gardener during my military service. I worked so that the garden would grow beautiful and healthy. That was where I met Fatima. I used to bring her the finest flowers I could grow. Soon after we married, she complained of neck pain, so I took her to a doctor. He told us that if she slept a long time without waking, the muscles in her neck would stiffen and her condition would worsen. He advised her to break her sleep every night, wake, and walk a little, but she wouldn’t listen to either of us. In those days our flower happened to begin to wither. I told her it would recover if she got up at night to water it. I asked her to promise, and she did.”","“Every night, I woke her and watched her water the flower she regarded as her child. Seeing her care for it, I felt as though I were that flower.”","He paused, then continued:","“Every night after she fell asleep, I got up to empty the excess water from the pot. It is not good to water a mother-of-pearl flower at night, Your Honor. Last night, I could not wake up either. We’re getting old. The flower could have gone one night without water, but my wife’s condition could have grown worse. She blamed me for not waking her. I said nothing.”","At that moment everyone in the hall, including the journalists, was in tears…","Moral: Love is sometimes hidden not in words, but in care carried quietly for years. Let us protect the one we love, and let us keep from breaking hearts.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"red-dress","title":"The Red Dress","paragraphs":["The red dress was hanging in my mother's wardrobe. It seemed to shine next to the worn, faded clothes she had worn all her life.","When I came to my mother’s bedside, I could see that she was nearing the end of her life.","Seeing the red dress, I said to her:","“What a beautiful dress, Mom. I’ve never seen you wear it.”","“I never have,” she said softly. “Sit beside me, my beautiful girl. I want to give you a lesson before I leave this world.”","I sat down on the edge of the bed. My mother took a deep breath. “I don’t have much time left,” she said and continued:","“I thought I had always taught you good things. Now I realize I taught you many wrong things.”","“What do you mean, Mom?”","“Listen to me. I believed a good woman should always put everyone else first. Their wishes mattered; mine were crushed under everyone else’s. Who knows, perhaps one day my own wishes would have come true too, but that day never came. My whole life became a sacrifice for your father and all of you children.”","“That’s true, Mom. You did everything a mother could do.”","“Ah, my girl, nothing I did for you or for them did me any good. Do you understand? I never wanted anything for myself. Your father is sitting in the next room, upset and staring at the walls. When the doctor told him I was dying, he came in and said:”","“You must not die, do you hear me? What will I do after you?”","“My girl, I always burned myself out for you. Seven days a week, I was always the first in the house to get up and the last to go to bed. I always ate the burned bread and the smallest pieces. Every penny I had saved went on your clothes and books, even things you didn’t really need. Not once in my life did I go out shopping and buy myself anything lovely. I only bought that red dress you saw last year. I had a little money saved for the washing-machine repair. When I brought the dress home, your father upset me terribly.”","“Where do you think you’ll wear that? To the opera?” he asked.","“Actually, he was right. I never wore that dress except when I tried it on in the shop.”","“I used to think that someone who forgets herself in this world would be happy in the next. Now I think Allah wants us to fulfill our wishes in this world, while we are still alive. If a miracle happened and I could get up from this bed, I would live completely differently.”","These were my mother’s last words to me:","“Promise me you won’t do what I did.”","I promised my mother and she breathed her last.","Moral: A person who always forgets herself one day realizes she never lived her own wishes. Love others, but also know the worth of your own life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"true-love","title":"True Love","paragraphs":["There was an island where all the feelings lived.","One day news spread that the island was sinking. The feelings began to prepare in haste to leave.","Love was in no hurry to leave. She waited until the last moment, as if sure a miracle would save her. When the island was almost gone, Love finally decided to ask for help.","At that moment Wealth was sailing away in a very large boat.","Love asked: “Wealth, can you take me with you?” Wealth said: “No, I can’t. My boat is full of gold and silver — there is no room for you.”","Love then turned to Arrogance, sailing in a beautiful sailboat, and asked for help: “Arrogance, please help me.”","Arrogance replied, “I cannot help you, Love. You are soaking wet; you will ruin my sail.”","Sadness was nearby. Love asked him too: “Sadness, may I come with you?”","Sadness said: “Oh, Love, I am so sad that I need to be alone.”","Happiness was not far from Love, yet so excited that she did not hear Love’s call.","Suddenly a voice said: “Love, come with me.” Love was so glad that it did not even occur to her to ask who her savior was. When they reached the shore, the one who had helped Love left her there and went on.","On the shore, realizing how much she owed, Love told a wise person what had happened and asked: “Who was it that helped me?”","The wise one answered: “It was Time.”","“Time? But why did Time help me?” Love asked.","The wise one smiled: “Because only Time knows how important and precious Love is.”","Moral: Only time knows the worth of true love. Time keeps sincere love alive and lets the false fade into forgetfulness.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-women-have-endured-in-this-world","title":"What Women Have Endured","paragraphs":["What a woman has endured in this world...","Even in fairy tales, they made her either a witch or a stepmother...","What didn’t they say about a woman in the stories.","They would not let her be herself. They should have known her as a person who gives life, raises children, and loves without expecting anything in return.","Woman...","They told her that her hair was long and her mind was short.","“Your hands are covered in flour. Sit where you belong. Don’t interfere with anyone.”","Woman, how much you have endured.","Few people knew your worth.","Few people could see your love, your heart, your goodness.","They called you wicked when you asked for your rights and virtuous when you kept silent.","When you showed your love, some thought they were your master.","Sometimes, those you raised to the heavens with your love preferred to stay there and forgot about you.","They did not ask what was going on or how you were.","They never asked whether you were there or not.","They forgot that you had a soul.","They forgot that you were human.","They made you ill, crushed you, and treated you as nothing.","Yet you wanted only to beautify, to help, and to change things toward the good.","Your soul wandered in this world in need of love.","They talked about heaven and made you experience hell.","They looked into your eyes and denied even what they saw there. They would not hear the cry within you.","What you loved most was being a mother and looking with love into your child’s eyes. In this world, only your child’s embrace felt sincere to you.","Moral: There can be no peace or love where a woman is not valued. It is the duty of society to see her work and heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-is-loyalty","title":"What Is Loyalty?","paragraphs":["It is not forgetting,","Not turning your back on someone on a difficult day,","Not closing the doors...","Not laughing when your friend is crying,","Loyalty is sharing every pain with him...","It is not just a word...","Loyalty means sharing your friend's state,","Balm for the wounded,","Water for the thirsty,","Loyalty is understanding: not discarding a rose because it has thorns.","Moral: Loyalty means not leaving someone on a hard day, and not throwing away the rose because of its thorn. It is not a word, but a deed.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"can-a-broken-heart-love-again","title":"Can a Broken Heart Love Again?","paragraphs":["A man asked a dervish:","“Can a broken heart love again?”","“Yes, it can,” the dervish replied.","“But have you ever drunk water from a broken vessel?” the man asked.","“Tell me,” said the dervish, “have you ever given up drinking water because a vessel broke?”","Moral: Even if the heart is broken, it does not stop loving. Just as one does not stop drinking water because a vessel is broken, so too does a person continue to love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"woman-and-the-mirror","title":"The Woman and the Mirror","paragraphs":["A woman wakes up in the morning and looks in the mirror. She sees that she has three strands of hair left on her head.","“I’ll braid my hair today,” she decided. She did, and had a lovely day.","The next day she wakes up and looks in the mirror again... She sees that she has two strands of hair left on her head.","“I’ll part it down the middle.” She did, and had another lovely day.","The next day she wakes up and sees in the mirror that she has only one strand of hair left on her head.","“Never mind, so be it,” she says. “I’ll make a ponytail today too.” She does, and spends a truly splendid day.","Another day she looks in the mirror and sees that she has no hair left on her head.","“Wonderful!” she exclaimed. “Today I won’t have to do my hair at all.”","Moral: Our outlook on life affects our mood. Do not expect happiness only from someone else; even on a hard day, try to find a reason to rejoice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"yellow-and-red-flowers","title":"Yellow and Red Flowers","paragraphs":["In earlier times, yellow flowers placed in a house window meant, “Someone in this home is ill; please do not make noise as you pass.” Red flowers conveyed a different message: “A young woman who has come of age lives here; please mind your words.”","Moral: Respect for people can sometimes be shown with a delicate sign. You do not always need to raise your voice in order to give a warning.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","blurb":"Choosing friends, compassion, and how we treat people.","count":31,"stories":[{"stem":"compliment","title":"A Compliment","paragraphs":["Maria lived with her mother in a small apartment in New York and worked as a secretary for a large company. Her life was quiet, ordinary, and rather dull. At work, hardly anyone noticed her.","One morning, on her way to the office, Maria saw a newly opened hat shop and went inside out of curiosity. A woman and her young daughter were there, along with another customer looking for a hat.","Maria tried on several hats. One appealed to her more than all the others. The moment she put it on, the little girl exclaimed with delight:","“Mom, look how beautiful that woman looks in the hat!”","The girl’s mother turned to Maria and said:","“That hat really suits you.”","The other customer joined them:","“It really does. You look wonderful in it!”","Maria stepped in front of the mirror. She had never been so pleased with her reflection. Smiling, she went to the counter and paid for the hat.","When Maria left the shop, an entirely new world seemed to open before her. The flowers looked brighter than ever, and the fresh air smelled especially sweet. Even the sounds of traffic and passing voices seemed to form a harmonious melody. Maria felt beautiful and happy.","At the office entrance, the security guard opened the door and wished her good morning. In the elevator, someone asked which floor she needed and pressed the button for her. Throughout the day, her colleagues admired her and told her how lovely she looked, as though they were seeing her for the first time. Her manager even invited her to lunch to ask for her opinion on a new project.","When the magical workday ended, Maria was still smiling. She thought about how suddenly her life had changed and was certain that she owed it all to her new hat.","Maria took a taxi home. Her mother opened the door, stared at her in amazement, and said:","“Maria, you look wonderful! Your eyes are shining just as they did when you were a child!”","“Yes, Mom, it is all because of my new hat. I have had an amazing day!”","“What hat?” her mother asked, puzzled.","Alarmed, Maria touched her head. The hat was gone. She wondered whether she had left it in the taxi, at lunch, or in the office. Then she replayed the scene in the shop: putting on the hat, hearing the compliments, walking to the counter, taking out her purse, and setting the hat down beside the register…","Suddenly she understood. She had forgotten the hat in the shop and walked out without it, her eyes still shining with joy.","The hat had not made Maria happy. It was the confidence and delight awakened by other people’s kind words. The world had not changed; Maria’s way of seeing it had.","Moral: A single kind word can change how a person sees themselves—and sometimes their entire world. Happiness is not found in outward possessions, but within us and in the sincere attention shown to a person.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"glass-of-milk","title":"A Glass of Milk","paragraphs":["A poor boy who sold small goods from door to door to pay his tuition discovered one day that he had no money left and was desperately hungry. He decided to knock on the next door and ask for something to eat.","A young woman answered. Embarrassed, the boy could not bring himself to ask for food and requested only a glass of water. Seeing that he was hungry, the woman brought him a large glass of milk instead.","After drinking it slowly, the boy asked:","“How much do I owe you?”","“Nothing,” she replied. “My mother taught us to be kind without expecting anything in return.”","The boy could say only:","“Thank you from the bottom of my heart.”","As he walked away, he felt stronger not only physically, but also in spirit. His faith in Allah and in other people had been renewed.","Years passed. One day, the same woman became seriously ill. Unable to determine the cause of her illness, the local doctors sent her to specialists in a large city.","Dr. Howard Kelly was called in for a consultation. When he heard which city the patient came from, he immediately went to her room. The moment he saw her, he recognized the woman who had given him a glass of milk years before and rekindled hope in his heart.","Dr. Kelly personally took charge of her treatment and resolved to do everything he could to save her. After a long treatment, the woman recovered.","When the time came for her discharge, Dr. Kelly requested the final bill. He wrote a few words along its edge and had it delivered to the patient’s room.","The woman opened it anxiously. She feared that she would never be able to pay the amount and would spend the rest of her life repaying the debt. Then she noticed a sentence written in the margin:","“Paid in full with one glass of milk.”","Signed: Dr. Howard Kelly.","Moral: A selfless act of kindness is never lost. It is entered into an unseen account and may return to us one day—sometimes when we least expect it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"handful-of-roasted-chickpeas","title":"A Handful of Roasted Chickpeas","paragraphs":["A boy entered a nut shop with his mother and kept insisting, “I want some roasted chickpeas.” The shopkeeper saw that the mother did not intend to buy any and felt sorry for the child:","“Come here, son. Take a handful for yourself.”","“No, I’ll take them only if you give them to me,” the boy replied.","The shopkeeper handed him a generous handful and asked:","“Why didn’t you take it yourself? You wanted me to give it to you.”","The boy smiled:","“Because your hand is bigger than mine.”","“Mashaallah, what a clever child you are!” the shopkeeper smiled too.","Moral: Sometimes whom you ask is as important as what you ask for. When the giver’s hand is larger, the gift may be larger too.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pair-of-boots","title":"A Pair of Boots","paragraphs":["One day, a schoolteacher asked her pupils to write a story on any subject and promised a pair of boots to the author of the best one. Every child completed a story and handed it in.","The teacher read each piece and liked them all. Unable to choose the best, she decided to draw the winner by lot.","Each pupil wrote a name on a slip of paper, folded it, and placed it in a box on the teacher’s desk. After mixing the slips, she drew one and announced:","“Amina!”","The classroom erupted in applause. Amina pressed the boots to her chest and could not hold back tears of joy.","That evening, the teacher returned home and tearfully told her husband what had happened. He was puzzled:","“You found a wonderful solution. Why are you crying?”","The teacher replied:","“Because every slip said Amina. Not one child wrote their own name. They all wanted the girl from the poorest family in our class to have those boots.”","Moral: It is also a victory to value another person’s joy above one’s own. True compassion is revealed in the willingness to surrender an opportunity for someone else.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-alone","title":"Being Alone","paragraphs":["I had gone to visit an acquaintance. I entered his house and went into his room. I found him sitting silently with his head bowed. He looked sorrowful.","“Are you sad?” I asked.","He raised his head and looked into my eyes. “No. I’m lonely.”","Moral: The biggest pain is to feel lonely even among people. Seeing the loneliness of someone next to you is true sensitivity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"discussion-and-conflict","title":"Discussion and Conflict","paragraphs":["As they did every day, Javid Bey and his team gathered in the meeting room to discuss the state of their project. Each person briefly described the previous day’s progress and any difficulties they had encountered.","It was Kamal’s turn. Like the others, he reported on the work he had done, but — as if hoping for help from his colleagues — he spoke in more detail about the problem he faced. As soon as he finished, Nadir accused him in front of everyone: “You are to blame; because of you our work has stopped and we can do nothing.” The room froze. Kamal was deeply embarrassed and did not know what to do.","Before the exchange could escalate, Javid Bey, the project manager, stepped in. “Let’s stop here for today and return to this later,” he said. The team dispersed.","Javid Bey knew what to do: meet one-on-one and speak in a friendly way. On the pretext of a glass of tea, he invited him to the company café. After a little small talk, he said calmly, “My son, the way you treated Kamal today was wrong. You accused him without understanding the situation, and you did it in front of everyone. Put yourself in his place. He was hoping for our help and was accused instead. I’m sure he hoped we would think the problem through together and find a solution.”","Nadir lowered his head in shame. Javid Bey’s counsel had worked; it was a good lesson for Nadir — he understood he had done wrong.","Later, whenever Nadir remembered the conversation, he thanked Javid Bey. He was also grateful that no one at the company knew of the conversation between them.","Moral: Conflict seeks who is right; discussion seeks what is right. If you want the truth, discuss — do not quarrel.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"everyone-is-the-same-here","title":"Everyone Is Equal Here","paragraphs":["A young, newly appointed municipal leader decided to explore his district on foot. After walking around alone, he stopped for tea at a teahouse on a side street.","“Uncle, may I polish your shoes?” asked a boy of twelve or thirteen.","His shoes were already polished, but he did not want to disappoint the boy.","“All right. If you do a good job, I’ll pay you double.”","“I polish the same way for everyone,” the boy replied.","The official said:","“What do you mean?” he asked in surprise.","The child answered:","“Our teacher told us to treat everyone equally, whatever we do. I’m earning money to buy medicine for my sick mother. If I treat you differently, that medicine won’t make her better!”","The young head learned one of the most important lessons of his life. Deeply moved, he gave the boy the largest banknote in his pocket and his business card. He promised to help the fatherless child, who was attending school while caring for his sick mother.","He also visited the teacher to thank him for teaching the boy such honesty.","To keep the lesson before him, he had the words “Everyone is equal here” written on the back of his desk nameplate.","Though it was hard, he always tried to put it into practice.","Moral: Treat a person the same because they are human, not because of their rank. Society is changed by those who choose what is right in small moments.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-forgive","title":"Forgiveness","paragraphs":["Forgiving someone does not mean you must love them, speak to them, or continue the relationship. Nor does it mean accepting that they are innocent or right.","It means freeing yourself from the captivity of hurt, resentment, and hatred, and from the pain of an old wound.","Forgive and be free. But learn the lesson of what happened, so you are not wounded in the same place again.","Moral: Forgiving is not about justifying the other person - it is about freeing yourself from the prison of hatred and resentment. Forgive and be relieved, but do not forget the lesson.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-meet-and-to-know","title":"Meeting and Knowing","paragraphs":["For some, we can only say this:","We were happy to meet them; we were sad to discover who they were.","Moral: Becoming acquainted can gladden you, but knowing a person closely sometimes disappoints you. Seeing everyone as they are is not easy.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"go-but-come-again","title":"Go, but Come Again","paragraphs":["In an Ottoman custom, hosts who wanted their guests to come again placed the guests’ shoes at the door with their toes pointing into the house. When the guest left the house, he seemed to walk backwards. The gesture meant, “Go, but come again.”","Moral: A subtle gesture can convey great kindness. The value given to a person is often seen in small moments.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"gossip","title":"Gossip","paragraphs":["A sharp-tongued, impolite man was brought before the village elder for spreading gossip.","“It was only a joke!” he protested. “People misunderstood me. I did nothing wrong.”","But the victim of the gossip stood firm:","“You’ve ruined my good name,” said the person he had maligned. “I want justice.”","Under pressure, the man backed down.","“I take it all back. Please forgive me.”","The elder listened in silence, thinking that the man had not yet grasped the weight of what he had done and that, in time, he would repeat the same sin. Then he said:","“Take a feather pillow to the market square, cut it open, and scatter the feathers. Then gather every one and bring them to me. Only then can you be forgiven.”","Reluctantly, the man obeyed. “The old man has lost his mind,” he thought. But the moment he cut the pillow, the wind carried the feathers in every direction. He chased them back and forth, yet managed to recover only a few feathers in his palm.","He lowered his head and returned to the elder:","“I couldn’t gather the rest,” he admitted.","Then he sighed deeply:","“Just as I can’t take back the words I spread.”","Moral: A word that is spread is like a feather thrown to the wind - it cannot be collected again. Therefore, stay away from gossip.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"how-to-ward-off-insults","title":"How to Ward Off Insults","paragraphs":["(An instructive story from the life of the samurai)","An elderly, wise samurai taught his students both wisdom and martial arts. One day, a warrior known for cruelty and rudeness interrupted a lesson. He usually defeated opponents by insulting them until they grew angry and made mistakes. He tried the same tactic on the samurai, who ignored him and continued teaching.","After several unsuccessful attempts to provoke him, the warrior left in a rage.","The students, who were watching this scene carefully, asked their teacher:","“Master, why did you let him insult you? Why didn’t you fight?”","The samurai replied:","“If someone offers you a gift and you refuse it, who owns it?”","“The person who offered it,” the students answered.","“The same is true of envy, hatred, and insults. If you refuse to accept them, they remain with the person who brought them.”","Moral: An insult you do not accept returns to its owner, just like a gift sent back. Answer evil not with evil, but with composure.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"humanity","title":"Humanity","paragraphs":["Kamal had studied hard for years. At last, the day of the exam that would shape his future arrived. His father, Kazim, was demanding and followed his progress closely. He was certain his son would succeed.","Kamal found his exam room. He was nervous when the question sheets were handed out, but he relaxed when the exam began. He answered them easily and felt sure he had done well. With half an hour left, he glanced toward the window.","Something outside caught his attention. Without hesitation, he jumped up and ran for the door.","“Come back!” the examiner called. “If you leave, your exam will be invalid. You’ll lose a whole year, my son!” But Kamal kept running and left through the back door.","Having heard what had happened, Kazim scolded Kamal and slapped him when he came home a few hours later. Kazim was deeply distressed. He demanded to know why Kamal had abandoned the exam. Kamal stayed silent, certain that an explanation would only make his father angrier, and he would not have understood.","For the rest of the year, Kazim repeatedly reproached him. Hurt but quiet, Kamal kept his head down and continued studying.","The next year, he passed and entered medical school. He became an excellent doctor. Although he had lived in the city for years, he volunteered to work at a remote village hospital. A year later, his family came to visit. By then, the matter of the exam had long been forgotten.","One snowy evening, the telephone rang. Someone in a nearby village needed a doctor. Kamal took his bag, and Kazim did not want to leave his son alone on the mountain roads, so he decided to accompany him. They walked about an hour on foot through the strong blizzard, reached the village, found the house, and knocked. An older man opened the door and led them to his sick wife.","Kamal examined her and arranged treatment. She thanked him in a trembling voice. “In this cold you were a remedy for our suffering, my son. What would the world be without people like you? Being truly human isn’t easy. Let me tell you something.”","“Years ago, I had gone to the city when, in the middle of the road, I was suddenly seized by severe pain. The streets were empty—there was an exam that day. Before five minutes had passed, a young man rushed over in alarm. In that heat he got me to the hospital in half an hour. I was pregnant and terrified for my child. The doctor said that if I had been ten minutes later, I could have died. My son was born that day.","“The young man told me his name was Kamal. ‘I saw you through the exam-room window,’ he said. ‘You were alone, so I left the classroom and came to help. Even if I lose a year, I don’t regret it.’","“I named my son Kamal and raised him on that story and constantly advised him to be a person like Kamal. Now he is a teacher, and he tries above all to teach his students humanity. I wish I could find that young man and receive his forgiveness. Anyone can become a doctor, a teacher, or an engineer. The hardest thing is to be a good human being. What a fine person that young man was. He did not leave me alone in the middle of the road.” Dr. Kamal answered, “You owe me nothing, Mother. Be at peace.”","Kazim tried to hide his tears. But when they left the house, his tears streamed down his face. “Kamal, my son,” he said, and could go no further — a sob choked him. He cried the whole way.","Moral: One may have a profession; the truly hard thing is to be human. The compassion a young man shows in time becomes a good that continues for generations.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-want-a-friend","title":"I Want a Friend","paragraphs":["I want neither wealth nor possessions,","I want a true friend.","What possession has ever recited a prayer of mercy for its owner when he dies?","I want a friend who will say a prayer of mercy for me.","Moral: A loyal friend is a greater treasure than any possession. A friend who will remember you with a prayer of mercy when you leave this world is more precious than any wealth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-the-road-does-not-tire-you-it-is-because-of-your-companion","title":"If the Road Does Not Tire You, It Is Because of Your Companion","paragraphs":["My mother used to tell me, “I was very young when I married your father. I could barely cook or sew. One day, before visiting a neighbor, your grandmother laid the quilt whose wool she had washed and dried in front of me. ‘Finish stitching this before I get back,’ she said. I tried, but the needle would hardly pass through the quilt. I kept pricking my fingers. Soon I was crying from the pain and from not knowing what I would tell her.","“Then your father came home for lunch. Seeing my tears, he took the needle, quickly finished stitching the quilt. He ate and returned to work without mentioning it to his mother. When she came home, she was delighted to find the work done.”","It was not said for nothing, “When the road does not tire you, it is because of your companion.”","Moral: It's not the road that tires you or lightens your load, but the person next to you. A true companion makes even the hardest task easier.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"meddling-in-others-lives","title":"Meddling in Other People’s Lives","paragraphs":["May Allah never leave anyone so idle that they have time to meddle in other people’s lives!","Moral: Unwanted advice can become a burden. Calling interference “kindness” does not prevent it from doing harm.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"know-your-friend","title":"Know Your Friend","paragraphs":["To know a friend’s character, watch how they face trouble, hardship, and a quarrel. Then see how they act when their own interests are at stake.","Moral: A friend is known in trouble, in hardship, and in the test of advantage. Everyone is a friend in good times; a true friend is revealed in adversity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"compassion","title":"Compassion","paragraphs":["It was a cold winter night, and I was walking home from work. Ahead of me, a man was swaying and clinging weakly to a pole. He looked drunk. As I approached, I saw that he was in his thirties, clean and neatly dressed. Some passersby made loud remarks about the dangers of alcohol; others laughed at him.","“Are you all right? Do you need help?” I asked.","His answer came out as a groan.","“I’m ill.”","I put an arm around his waist to steady him and helped him to a taxi stand. The snow, which had stopped earlier that evening, was falling again. The only movement on the empty streets came from stray dogs chasing one another across the freezing roads.","I was beginning to give up hope when a taxi pulled up. I explained what had happened and told the driver we needed to hurry.","We settled the man in the back seat and drove to the hospital. Bless the doctors — they came to help at once and gave him the necessary treatment. The driver and I waited at his bedside.","The doctor on duty told us that, at the very least, we had saved him from freezing. Still unable to speak, the man looked at us and smiled. The driver was beside me too. When he came to, we drove him home.","His wife knew that he was prone to diabetic comas. Terrified, she had gone out into the street with their son. When they saw us, they rushed over. The family embraced, their eyes shining with joy.","No trace remained of the tiredness that had lasted for hours; our eyes filled at the happiness of that family, who did not know how to thank us.","When we said goodbye, I asked the driver what I owed him.","He shook his head:","“You owe me nothing, my friend. I’m the one who owes you,” he said. “You let me share in something good. You let someone who had perhaps not cried for twenty years feel that beautiful feeling.”","As I hugged the driver and we parted, I did not feel the night’s cold, and I decided to walk home.","Who knew? Perhaps I would meet someone else who needed help along the way.","Moral: Humanity is built on compassion. Compassion is not crying with the one who cries, but finding a way to stop the tears.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"people-who-need-something-from-you","title":"People Who Need Something from You","paragraphs":["Instead of resenting people who contact you only when they need something, consider that they may have fallen into hardship and need your help. That thought may bring you peace.","Moral: Instead of resenting those who seek you only when they need something, think that they come because they trust you — and your heart will be at ease.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"permission-or-apology","title":"Permission or Apology","paragraphs":["A saying attributed to Rear Admiral Grace Hopper suggests that it is better to ask forgiveness than to ask permission.","The saying means: do not ask permission beforehand — do the work, then apologize if someone is displeased.","Sometimes waiting for permission is a missed opportunity. Do what you know is right, and apologize later if necessary.","Imagine that a company has a clear problem that is slowing down the work process. For example, every week the same information is manually transferred to several different spreadsheets, which takes hours. An employee realizes that this can be solved with simple automation.","If he first sought lengthy permission, the matter might pass from one manager to another for weeks, and the opportunity might be lost. Instead, the employee recognizes that a limited test poses little risk, sets up a separate system that leaves the original data intact, and demonstrates the result. If it works, everyone benefits. If the lack of prior approval causes concern, the employee can say, “I tested it to speed up the process. If I have broken the rule, I apologize.”","Moral: This is not about ignoring rules. When a mistake can be corrected, the risk is low, and the benefit is clear, waiting too long for permission can stifle initiative. Sometimes starting the work responsibly and showing the result is more useful than waiting for endless approval.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"how-we-treat-people","title":"How We Treat People","paragraphs":["Let your attitude toward people be like your attitude toward a fire:","don't go too far away lest you freeze;","don't come too close lest you burn.","Saadi Shirazi","Moral: Relationships need balance. Keep a measure: do not draw so far away that you grow cold, and do not come so close that you burn.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mockery-is-unacceptable","title":"Mockery Is Unacceptable","paragraphs":["We should teach our children from a young age that it is unacceptable to ridicule or humiliate other people for their differences or appearance.","Moral: Mocking someone’s difference or appearance is a vile act. We must teach this to our children from the earliest age.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"kindness-builds-stronger-bonds","title":"Relationships","paragraphs":["In the most delicate and profound layers of human life lies a truth: our relationships are a mirror of our inner selves. The warmth born at the family hearth, the bonds rooted in kinship, the mutual understanding that grows in friendship—each is among life's most precious treasures. The respect and kindness fostered among colleagues are like an unseen light that gives spirit to our daily work.","Kindness is not simply a way of behaving; it is an expression of a person's inner richness. It is a silent bridge that brings hearts closer, warms cold moments, and makes understanding possible without words.","If we protect and strengthen this bridge, life will become more meaningful, people will grow closer, and the world will become a little more worth living in.","Moral: Relationships grow stronger through mutual respect, understanding, and kindness. To maintain our closeness, we must remember to care for one another.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"spend-your-time-with-people","title":"Spend time with people","paragraphs":["My father needed to transfer money, and we spent an hour at the bank. Growing impatient, I said:","“Dad, let’s do your banking over the internet. What do you say?”","“What would that give me, son?” my father asked, looking at me in surprise.","“You wouldn’t have to spend an hour here just to make a transfer. You could shop online too. It would be so much easier.”","I was very excited about the idea of getting my father involved in the world of the internet.","My father asked:","“Then I wouldn’t need to leave the house?”","“That’s right, that’s right, Dad.”","I explained that even the groceries he bought could be brought to the door, and that Amazon delivers everything to the buyer’s home.","My father's answer left me speechless:","“In this hour, I’ve seen four friends and talked to staff who have known me well for a long time.","“You know I am lonely. I need to see people. I like getting dressed and coming to the bank. I have time, and I enjoy talking face to face.","“When I was ill two years ago, the seller from the nearby shop where I always bought fruit came to visit. He was deeply distressed that I was ill, sat beside my bed, and shared in my condition.","“Some time ago, when your mother fell on her morning walk, our neighborhood grocer picked her up, put her in his car, and quickly drove her home. He knew where we lived.","“Wouldn’t I be deprived of these human feelings if I did everything online?","“Why should I be in contact only with a computer just to have things delivered?","“I want to know the person I deal with instead of an “electronic seller.” I want a human relationship.","“Can Amazon give me that?","“Technology is not life.","“Spend your time with people, not with devices, son.”","Moral: Technology provides convenience, but it does not replace life. The real value is in live communication, in the warmth between people.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"friendship-of-horses","title":"The Friendship of Horses","paragraphs":["Two horses live on a farm near my house. From a distance, they look quite ordinary. Up close, you notice something unusual: one is blind. Its owner could not bear to part with it and had a safe, comfortable stable built for it. That alone is quite astonishing.","As you approach, you hear a bell attached to the other, smaller, healthy horse’s bridle. The blind horse follows the sound.","Watch closely, and you will see that the horse with the bell keeps checking on its companion, while the blind horse listens and slowly follows the healthy horse.","Each evening on the way back to the stable, the smaller horse stops from time to time and looks back, making sure its friend is close enough to hear the bell.","Moral: The Lord does not abandon us in difficult times. When we need it, He sends help through a person's hands, and sometimes we are called to be that help.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sound-of-the-doorbell","title":"The Sound of the Doorbell","paragraphs":["I was laid off last winter. To make ends meet, I had to work as a newspaper carrier temporarily. Since the mailbox of one of the houses I delivered newspapers to was sealed, I had to knock on the door. An old man opened the door.","“Uncle, why have you sealed the mailbox?” I asked.","“I did it deliberately.”","Then he smiled strangely and continued:","“Please knock or ring each day and give me the paper in person.”","“Of course, but this is inconvenient for both of us and a waste of time,” I said in surprise.","“It’s no trouble. I’m always here.”","“My wife has died, my son lives abroad, and I’m alone. Who knows how much time I have left?”","At that moment, I saw the old man’s misty, moist eyes.","“Haven’t you subscribed in order to read the newspaper?” I asked.","“I never read the newspaper. What I subscribed to was the sound of the doorbell.”","Then he clasped his hands together and said:","“Young man, do one more kindness for me. Here is my son’s number. If I ever fail to answer when you knock, call him and let him know.”","Moral: For some people, one ring of the doorbell, one greeting, is worth the whole world. Let's not forget to knock on the door of the lonely.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"those-you-should-not-befriend","title":"Those You Should Not Befriend","paragraphs":["Do not befriend an arrogant person. He knows neither your condition, nor your mood, nor your heart; you will be hurt.","Do not befriend someone who thinks himself very clever. He talks a great deal, talks emptily, and talks poorly; you will be hurt.","Do not befriend a dishonorable man. He knows neither honor, nor heart, nor friendship; you will be hurt.","Moral: Friendship with an arrogant, self-satisfied, dishonorable person brings regret. When you choose a friend, look at their heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"caring-call-brightens-the-day","title":"Thoughtfulness","paragraphs":["One of the most precious blessings in a person's life is to receive attention and care. Sometimes even a brief call from a relative, a friend, or an acquaintance, simply to ask how we are, rises like the sun in our hearts. In that moment, we realize that somewhere in this vast world there are people who remember us and consider our presence important.","Everyone needs to be loved, remembered, and valued. A sincere greeting, a heartfelt inquiry, or an unexpected call can sometimes be worth more than the most precious gift. For these small gestures help us feel our place in society and our worth in the hearts of those around us.","A few minutes of attention may brighten someone's day, awaken hope in their heart, and deepen their attachment to life a little more.","Sometimes it is not medicine that prolongs a person's life, but words that touch the heart, the care they receive, and the sense of closeness they feel. For one of the surest sources of happiness is knowing that we have not been forgotten.","Moral: Asking how someone is and remembering them may seem like small things, yet they can offer great emotional support. Let us not withhold our care, so that no one feels forgotten.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"welcome-my-bey","title":"Welcome, My Bey","paragraphs":["When my late father, Hafiz Ali, was at Çanakkale, my mother was seven months pregnant with me. I never met him. We did not even have a photograph.","Those were very difficult days. Mobilization, occupation, liberation, poverty, hardship...","Our childhood was spent constantly running around in search of bread, filled with pain.","Whenever my mother wanted to go somewhere, she would always come to me and say:","“Son, I’m going to the market. Tell me at once if your father comes.”","“I’m going to your aunt’s. Tell me at once if your father comes.”","“I’m going next door. Tell me at once if your father comes.”","She was always watching the road, waiting for him.","I grew up and opened a shop. Whenever she went out, she stopped there to tell me where she was going. “Let me know if your father comes,” she always added.","Years passed, and she grew old. Leaning on her cane, she still came to remind me, “Call me if your father returns.”","One day, she became ill and fell into bed.","On her deathbed, she thanked us for caring for her and asked our forgiveness. Then she turned to me. “If your father comes, tell him your mother always waited for him.” Suddenly she raised her head, looked toward the door, and smiled. “Welcome, my bey, welcome.” Then she died.","* * *","From the memoirs of Cevdet Alqalp, an old shoemaker living in Balıkesir.","Written by: researcher and writer, geography teacher at Bursa Çınar Anatolian High School Mustafa Doğru.","* * *","March 18 is the day of the Çanakkale Victory.","If Çanakkale had fallen in 1915, fictitious states would have been set up in Anatolia, the peoples living there would have been set against one another and slaughtered, and the country would have become a colony of the English, the French, and the Italians.","* * *","Including our Azerbaijani soldiers who were martyred at Çanakkale, may all martyrs rest in peace. May no mother’s eyes remain on the road.","Moral: A mother’s lifelong loyalty and waiting is the purest expression of love. The memory of those martyred for the homeland, and of the mothers whose eyes stayed on the road, is sacred.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-people-shout-when-they-argue","title":"Why Do People Shout When They Argue?","paragraphs":["One day during class, a teacher asked his students:","“Why do people shout when they argue?”","“Because they lose their temper,” a student replied.","“But the other person is right beside them. Why do you shout? Even if you are angry, can you not speak with them calmly?”","Each of the students gave an answer, but none of them satisfied the teacher. Finally, he said:","“As people grow displeased with each other and argue, their hearts grow apart. They have to shout to hear each other across that distance. The louder they shout, the farther apart their hearts are.”","After a moment of silence, the teacher continued:","“When people love each other, they speak softly. Their hearts are very close, so the distance between them is small. And when love is stronger, they do not even speak softly; they whisper and draw closer. As their hearts draw near, they no longer need to whisper—they look at each other and understand everything without words. My dear students, when you argue, do not let your hearts drift apart, and do not say words that can increase the distance between you.”","“One day, the distance may become too great to find your way back.”","Moral: Screaming is the sound of hearts drifting apart. A whisper is enough in love — don't increase the distance between you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"your-friends","title":"Your Friends","paragraphs":["One day a young man decided to marry. After a week of wedding preparations, one evening he said to his father, “Father, I have a request. I cannot do all the work alone. Please take this list and call everyone on it to invite them to my wedding.” “Gladly, my son!” his father answered.","On the wedding day, the young man approached him anxiously. “Father, I asked you to call all my friends!”","“Son, I did just as you asked.”","“There were fifty names. Only fifteen are here.”","“I called all fifty. I told each of them that I was calling in your name, that you were in trouble and needed your friends’ help, and I asked them to be at this time and place. Don’t worry, son. Your true friends are here.”","Moral: A true friend is one who is there for you when you are in trouble. It is not the many acquaintances that are valuable, but the few friends who stand the test.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","blurb":"Honesty, generosity, and personal integrity.","count":42,"stories":[{"stem":"generous-man","title":"A Generous Man","paragraphs":["A generous man was once asked:","“Do you expect the poor to be grateful to you for the blessings you distribute to them?”","He replied:","“I am like a ladle in a cook’s hand. The ladle serves the food, but the gift comes from the cook.”","Moral: Those who do good should not consider themselves the true owners of what they give. They are only instruments; the true Giver is Allah.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mature-person","title":"A Mature Person","paragraphs":["There comes a time when you value the sincerity of old friends more. You do not seek meaningless acquaintances, and you stay away from ignorant people and from relationships that tire you inwardly. Then you understand that you have already matured.","Moral: Maturity is the ability to step away from harmful relationships and protect one’s inner peace. Knowing whom—and what—to keep at a distance is also a sign of full maturity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"meaningless-question","title":"A Meaningless Question","paragraphs":["One day, a graduate of the University of California, Berkeley, visited a rabbi in New York and asked for help studying the Talmud.","“Do you know Aramaic? No? What about Hebrew? Did they at least read the Torah to you as a child?”","The young man answered no to every question.","“But I defended a dissertation on Socratic logic! I would like to study the Talmud to fill some gaps in my education.”","“The Talmud is one of the greatest works of human thought, but you are not yet ready to study it. Still, since you insist, I will give you a logic question. If you answer correctly, I will teach you. Two people climb down the same chimney. One emerges with a clean face and the other with a face covered in soot. Which one will go and wash?”","“You call that a logic question?” the Berkeley graduate asked smugly. “The person with the dirty face, of course!”","“Wrong. The person with the clean face will see the other’s sooty face, assume that his own face is dirty too, and go to wash. The person covered in soot will see a clean face in front of him and suppose that he has remained clean.”","“What subtle logic! Could you give me another chance?”","“All right. Two people climb down the same chimney. One has a dirty face and the other a clean one. Which will go and wash?”","“But we have just discussed that. The person with the clean face will go.”","“Wrong again. Both will wash. The person with the clean face will see his companion’s dirty face and think that he is dirty too. The other will notice that his companion looked at him and then went to wash, so he will realize that his own face must also be dirty.”","“I hadn’t thought of that. Could you give me one more question?”","“Very well. I will ask for the last time: two people climb down the same chimney. One has a clean face and the other a dirty one. Which will go and wash?”","“Now it is clear: both of them will go!”","“No. Neither will go. The person with the dirty face will see a clean face before him and assume that he is clean as well. The person with the clean face will notice that his companion is not going to wash and conclude that there is no need to do so.”","Growing desperate, the young man begged the rabbi for one more question. The rabbi began:","“Two people climb down the same chimney. One has a dirty face and the other a clean one…”","“Neither of them will wash!” the young man answered hastily.","“Wrong again,” the rabbi said calmly. “As a specialist in Socratic logic, think for yourself: how could two people climb down the same chimney together and only one get soot on his face? The question itself makes no sense. Spend your life answering meaningless questions, and neither your answers nor your life will gain meaning.”","Moral: An answer to a meaningless question cannot lead to truth. The right question is already half the right answer.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"attitude-toward-a-problem","title":"Attitude Toward a Problem","paragraphs":["Is there a problem in your life?","If not, there is no reason to worry.","If there is, can you solve it?","If you can, there is no reason to worry.","If you cannot, there is still no reason to worry.","Moral: If a problem can be solved, work toward a solution. If it cannot, worrying will not change it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"avoid-these-questions","title":"Avoid These Questions","paragraphs":["Do not ask someone who is unemployed, “Are you still out of work?” Avoid asking an employee, “How much do you earn?”, or someone without children, “Don’t you have children yet?” or a single person, “When are you getting married?”","Nor should we embarrass someone by asking, “You didn’t know that?” A question that seems ordinary to us may touch another person’s deepest wound.","Moral: Some questions wound the heart. Not asking such a question may protect someone from being hurt.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"be-sure-of-your-purpose","title":"Be Sure of Your Purpose","paragraphs":["Two men were struggling to move a heavy chest through a doorway. After a long effort, they stopped, exhausted and drenched in sweat.","One said:","“What a heavy chest! We still haven’t managed to get it inside.”","The other asked in surprise:","“Weren’t we supposed to take it outside?”","Moral: Clarify the goal before beginning the work. Otherwise, you may labor for hours while directing all your strength the wrong way.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-cool-headed","title":"Keeping a Cool Head","paragraphs":["Assess the situation you are in calmly. If you do, you can make the right decision and turn the situation to your advantage.","Moral: The best decision is made with a calm mind, not in a panic. Cool-headedness can turn even a difficult situation to your advantage.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"being-human","title":"Being Human","paragraphs":["“It is easy to become a scholar, but difficult to become a human being,” we often hear. What, then, does being human mean?","It means living with virtue, wisdom, a cultured manner, justice, honor, and dignity.","Being courteous, perceptive, well-mannered, and humble—showing regard for others without praising oneself.","Thinking before acting, keeping a sense of balance, remaining consistent, and standing firm in adversity.","Keeping one’s word; refusing to worship money or position; neither stealing nor taking what belongs to another.","Rejecting lies and slander, never betraying others for personal gain, and defending what is right.","Remaining free of hypocrisy and hatred, refusing to turn people against one another, and knowing how to love.","Moral: True worth lies not in knowledge alone, but in humanity. A heart capable of respect also makes room for love and justice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-self-confident","title":"Believe in Yourself","paragraphs":["Value yourself!","Do not give in to weakness.","Add strength to your strength!","Do not lose hope!","Be aware!","Be determined!","Act with good intentions.","Protect your dignity!","Have faith!","Moral: Value yourself, do not lose hope, protect your dignity. Self-confidence is a person's strongest support.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-not-do-these-things","title":"Do Not Do These Things","paragraphs":["Do not dwell on your wealth in front of someone who is poor, your health in front of someone who is ill, or your happiness in front of someone who is suffering. Do not speak of your freedom before a prisoner, your strength before someone weak, your child before someone who has none, or your parents before an orphan.","Moral: Do not boast of a blessing someone else lacks and reopen their wound. They need understanding, not reminders of their sorrow.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dont-open-your-mouth","title":"Don't Open Your Mouth","paragraphs":["If you're not going to say something kind, don't open your mouth at all.","Moral: The power of a word lies in using it in the right place and for good. If you have nothing worthwhile to say, silence speaks louder.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dont-say-i-couldnt-deliver-or-i-couldnt-manage","title":"Don't Say I Couldn't Get It Done, I Couldn't Manage","paragraphs":["Days follow one another. Some people end the day in joy, some in sorrow. But do not lose heart over the work you could not finish today by saying “I didn’t get it done, I couldn’t manage.” Above all, do not lose hope. When the sun rises tomorrow, a whole day will be before you.","Moral: Every day is a new page. You can't change yesterday, but how you write today is in your hands.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"education-or-character","title":"Education or Character?","paragraphs":["A king asked his minister:","“Minister, which matters more: education or character?”","The minister answered without hesitation:","“Character, Your Majesty.”","The king had it proclaimed throughout the realm and ordered that the finest animal trainer be found and brought before him. When the man was brought before him, the king asked:","“How long would it take you to teach a cat to serve guests with a tray in its paw?”","“Six months, Your Majesty.”","Six months later, the man was summoned to the palace with the cat he had trained.","“Have you taught it?” the king asked.","“Yes, Your Majesty.”","The court gathered, and the cat began serving the guests. As it approached the minister, the king asked again:","“Minister, which matters more: education or character?”","Before answering, the minister took out the mouse he had kept ready in his pocket and released it. The cat immediately dropped the tray and chased it.","Then the minister turned to the king.","“Character, Your Majesty.”","Moral: Education does not change character; when the chance comes, true nature shows itself. Being knowledgeable is good, but character is more important.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"everything-is-in-our-own-hands","title":"Everything Is in Our Own Hands","paragraphs":["(From Indian folklore)","A boy decided to confuse an old monk. He caught a bird, concealed it in his hands behind his back, and asked:","“Wise old man, is the bird I’m holding alive or dead?”","“If he says it’s alive,” the boy thought, “I’ll squeeze it to death. If he says it’s dead, I’ll open my hands and let it fly away. Either way, I’ll embarrass him.”","The monk thought for a moment, then answered:","“My son, the answer is in your hands.”","The boy stood still and lowered his head.","The monk continued:","“War and peace, punishment and forgiveness, good and evil—all lie in your hands. A person's path in life is determined not by his intellect, but by his intentions. Every person is capable of both good and evil. There is always a choice, and that choice is ours.”","Moral: Good and evil are a person’s own choice. Our fate is determined not by our intellect, but by our intentions.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"explaining-ones-sorrow","title":"How We Express Sorrow","paragraphs":["One who merely tells their sorrow is a sorrowful person; one who expresses it in beautiful words is a writer; one who conveys it through their bearing is a lover; one who hides it with a smile is a sage.","Moral: People carry their sorrow in different ways: some talk, some write, some keep quiet and smile. The deepest sorrow is often hidden in the deepest silence.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-give-up","title":"Letting Go","paragraphs":["Letting go is a beautiful trait!","from those who do not know how to appreciate,","those who trample on sincerity,","those who change for the sake of gain,","from arrogant and boastful people,","those who are envious, gossipers,","those who make a habit of lying,","those who do not know what decency is,","those who destroy their values,","those who turn a blind eye to ignorance,","those who despise their homeland, deny their nation,","those who forget their native language,","those deprived of a mother’s love, a father’s honor, and a child’s affection.","Letting go is a beautiful trait!","from those who believe they cannot be replaced.","Moral: Walking away from some people is not weakness; it is self-respect. Not leaning on someone who does not value you is a virtue too.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"he-would-not-have-given-so-little","title":"He Would Not Have Given So Little","paragraphs":["A king offered a gold coin to a lovely child he met on the road, but the child refused. When the king asked why, the child said:","“When I go home, my mother and father will ask, ‘Where did you steal this money?’”","“Tell them the king gave it to you.”","“Then they really won’t believe me. They’ll say, ‘If the king had given you money, he wouldn’t have given so little.’”","The king was so pleased that he gave the child a bag of gold.","Moral: Great things begin in the family, in proper upbringing. A child who grows up honestly becomes an honest person tomorrow.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"honey-or-dry-bread","title":"Honey or Dry Bread?","paragraphs":["It is better to sit with a scholar and eat dry bread than to sit with an ignorant man and eat honey.","Moral: Your companions can lift you up or bring you down. It is better to be content with dry bread beside a scholar than to eat honey with an ignorant person.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"how-can-i-escape-my-thoughts","title":"How Can I Escape My Thoughts?","paragraphs":["One day, a man came to visit one of the renowned scholars of his time and asked:","“How can I escape the painful thoughts that keep me prisoner?”","Instead of answering, the scholar stood, wrapped his arms around a nearby post, and began shouting with all his might:","“Separate me from this post — save my life from it!”","The man, looking at him in surprise, asked:","“Why are you doing that? I came to ask for help because I thought you were wise. But I see that you do not seem like a wise man. The post isn’t holding you—you’re holding it. Let go, and your life will be saved!”","The scholar let go of the post and said to the man:","“You’ve answered your own question. Your thoughts are not holding on to you; you are holding on to them. Let them go, so that your life may be freed as well.”","Moral: You yourself hold the thoughts that oppress you; it is in your hands to let them go. Change your perspective, and your burden will lighten.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-am-aware-now","title":"Now I Understand","paragraphs":["Reflections from someone who has weathered hard times:","I no longer try to make people love me. I know that anyone who dislikes me will always find an excuse.","I know my worth now. I do not have to prove myself to anyone.","I no longer want to let boastful, arrogant people into my life.","I know that the one who wants has a purpose, and the one who does not want always has a “but.”","I do not confide in gossips. Anyone who carries other people’s words will not keep my secret either.","I do not try to win over a stubborn person. I have learned that such a person cannot be turned from their opinion.","People who lie, make empty promises, and fail to keep their word have no place in my life.","I stay away from people who cannot forgive, who are merciless and harsh. I value people who can acknowledge a mistake and apologize.","I know I am happier with people around whom I can be myself.","Moral: True maturity is not about changing others, but about knowing who to let into your life and who to stay away from.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-got-busy-with-cleaning","title":"I Got Busy with Cleaning","paragraphs":["Today I did some cleaning.","And throughout my whole body.","I cleaned all my muscles, my nerves, my veins, and even my blood.","I cleared out everything that had been gathering in the depths.","First, the disappointments. I let them go without even looking at what they were.","You should have seen","how much space they had taken up.","Into the space that opened, I carefully placed the feeling of forgiveness. In the place of every hurt, I planted a seed of forgiveness.","Then I swept out jealousy.","How jealous I had been.","Letting it go was easy, and I felt happy,","as though I had recovered something I had lost.","Thank Allah, I found no hatred or grudges in my heart.","I don’t know how I cleaned.","Next came my fears.","At first I was afraid even to take them out.","I had grown so used to living with them.","How had I become accustomed to so much pain and worry?","Each day, fresh anxieties had brought new fears.","Meanwhile, I had kept putting off happiness and hope.","If only I had cared for my happiness and my hopes in time—if I had increased my hope a little more each day, given without expecting a return, loved without expecting anything back, and, most importantly, known the worth of the love and the strength within me. Then there would have been no need for such a cleaning.","I had difficulty clearing out my fears.","They had linked together like a chain and were almost locked. Still, I let them go without reproaching myself for once keeping them inside me like a nightmare.","I knew that if I had gotten angry at my fears and shouted at them, they would have come back at me.","Today I did some cleaning.","I planted joy, trust, and love.","I planted giving without receiving, loving without being loved, and sharing.","I planted wildness, naturalness, and forgiveness inside me.","I planted passion in every cell of my body.","I planted enthusiasm, excitement, and quiet.","All beautiful thoughts come to me in silence.","And I planted acceptance:","not surrender, but acceptance of things as they are.","Moral: True cleanliness is in the heart: throwing away disappointment, fear, and resentment and planting love, forgiveness, and hope in their place. The one who cleans his inner world finds peace.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-would-not-trade-it-for-anything","title":"I Would Not Trade It for Anything","paragraphs":["A day,","a beautiful day,","a sunny day —","I would not trade it for anything.","For its sake, I do not love war;","for its sake, I do not love oppression;","for its sake, I do not love lies.","I know they cannot live in the sun,","I know they cannot live side by side with love —","neither injustice,","nor fear,","nor hunger…","Necati Cumalı","Moral: On a beautiful, sunny day there is no place for strife, oppression, or lies. In the light, injustice, fear, and hunger cannot live.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"that-was-not-your-right","title":"That Was Not Your Right","paragraphs":["In the Battle of Çanakkale, morphine was given only to the wounded who still had hope of survival. One of the gravely wounded brought to Dr. Tarık Nusret was his own son. Seeing that the boy could not be saved, he withheld the injection. Two hours later, holding the lifeless body of his son, who had died a martyr, he whispered, “Forgive me, my son. That was not your right.”","Moral: Sometimes the greatest justice is a person’s standing against their own interest. A father who knows what is rightful will not give an unjust share even to his own child.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"telling-lies","title":"Lying","paragraphs":["“Do you ever lie?”","“Yes.”","“What kind of lie is it?”","“Only to myself. Every day I tell myself I’m stronger, more knowledgeable, and more capable than anyone else, and so on.”","“Perhaps you are.”","“No. There are people stronger than I am, and people more capable.”","“Then why say it?”","“Believe it or not, these words give me great enthusiasm. I want to become stronger, more educated, and to work on myself more.”","Moral: Sometimes the kind words you tell yourself — “you can do it” — become the force that drives you forward. A person is as strong as he believes in himself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"may-your-face-always-smile","title":"May Your Face Always Smile","paragraphs":["A person who sells vinegar with a smile will have more customers than a person who sells honey with a frown.","Moral: A smiling person is loved more than a frowning one. A smile costs nothing, but it wins hearts.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dont-be-words-for-mouths-dust-for-feet","title":"Don’t Become Talk on Tongues or Dust Under Feet","paragraphs":["“A tale on every tongue” means being the subject of gossip, rumors, or constant public discussion.","“Dust under feet” means lowering yourself, devaluing yourself, and remaining under other people’s feet.","Moral: Protect your dignity. Do not give people reason to gossip about you, and do not let yourself become worthless, like dust under feet.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-not-complain-about-any-day-you-have-lived","title":"Do Not Complain About Any Day You Have Lived","paragraphs":["Do not complain about any day you have lived:","Good days make you happy,","Bad days give you experience,","The worst days teach you a lesson,","The best days give you memories.","Moral: Every day gives a person something: a good day gives joy, a bad day experience, the worst day a lesson, and the best day an unforgettable memory. Do not count any day you have lived as wasted.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"no-one-listens-to-constant-complainers","title":"No One Listens to Those Who Complain Constantly","paragraphs":["A man who had seen the world gave this lesson to a group who kept complaining about the same problems. He told them a joke, and everyone laughed. He told it a second time, and only some of them laughed. The third time, nobody’s lips even moved.","Complaining about the same problems again and again, instead of seeking a solution, is just like that. Nobody listens to a person who complains constantly.","Moral: Hearing the same complaint over and over wearies people, like hearing the same joke again. Do not settle for repeating your sorrow; look for a remedy.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"not-every-sorrow-is-told-to-people","title":"Not Every Sorrow Is Told to People","paragraphs":["Not every sorrow should be shared with everyone.","A day will come when they strike you with that very sorrow.","Moral: Do not open your sorrow to everyone; a day will come when they hurt you with it. Choose carefully whom you tell.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"other-peoples-opinions","title":"Other People's Opinions","paragraphs":["One day, a person lost the comma, and every sentence ran together. Then the period disappeared, and his thoughts stretched on, and he could not separate them from one another. Next went the exclamation mark, taking joy, anger, and feeling with it. When the question mark vanished, the person forgot how to ask questions and had to accept everything as it was, without question. Finally, the colon disappeared, along with the ability to explain.","At the end of his life, only quotation marks remained in his hand, and inside them were only other people’s opinions.","Moral: A person who loses his own values and roots one by one fills with foreign ideas. If you do not protect your spiritual roots, in the end you cease to be yourself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"our-qualities-can-become-our-enemies","title":"Our Advantage Can Become Our Calamity","paragraphs":["The peacock’s enemy is its own tail.","A nightingale’s sweet tongue is what puts it in the cage.","A fox's ornate fur is what puts it in the trap.","Some qualities we like and love in ourselves sometimes become our weak spot and bring calamity on us.","Moral: Sometimes the trait we boast of is exactly our weak point. The tail gives the peacock away, and the tongue gives the nightingale away — do not rely too much on your advantage.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"expressions-you-should-stay-away-from","title":"Words to Avoid","paragraphs":["Avoid these phrases, especially in the family, and never use them against your spouse:","“Don’t talk nonsense!”","These words humiliate your spouse and tell them that you do not value their thoughts and feelings and that you do not understand them. Instead, try saying gently, “I see it differently” or “I don’t entirely agree.” In this way you both state your own view and preserve mutual respect.","“Don’t exaggerate!”","This can make your spouse feel that their view of events is wrong and that they are being criticized. Instead, ask, “Can you tell me more about what’s worrying you and how you feel?” That opens the way to understanding.","“Look at their husband (their wife)! That one does this and does that — and what about you?”","This sentence is very dangerous, because it brings a third person into the family. Comparing your spouse with someone else brings them insecurity. Your spouse will think your mind is on that person, and their sense of self will be wounded. They will feel unloved. Instead of such comparisons, explain your expectations clearly.","“You don’t understand!”","This is an insult: you are humiliating your spouse. If you think they will not understand you on this subject, or you do not want to share your thought, say, “I don’t want to talk about this; don’t be hurt; let’s talk about something else,” or “This probably doesn’t interest you.”","“Don’t go there, don’t talk to them, don’t do this, don’t wear that, don’t talk like that, bring this, take that!”","Your spouse is not a slave to your commands. Respectfully discuss what you do not want or dislike, and agree on it. Make requests rather than issuing orders, and remember to say “please.” That prevents needless quarrels.","Moral: Words can hurt the person we love the most. In the family, we should use the language of respect and requests, not orders and insults.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"strength-and-sorrow","title":"Strength and Sorrow","paragraphs":["My God, give me strength enough for my sorrow, or sorrow no greater than I can bear.","Muhammad Fuzuli","Moral: A person asks either for strength to bear the sorrow, or for sorrow no greater than that strength can carry. Do not take on a burden heavier than you can bear.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"flower-of-honesty","title":"The Flower of Honesty","paragraphs":["Before a prince could take the throne, tradition required him to marry. He invited the most beautiful young women in the country to the palace to choose a bride.","The daughter of a palace servant loved the prince and wanted to attend. Her mother tried to dissuade her, but the young woman was determined.","On the appointed day, the women arrived in their finest clothes, each hoping to impress him.","The prince gave each woman a seed. “Plant this in a pot and return in six months,” he said. “I will marry the woman who grows the most beautiful flower.” They went home full of excitement.","The servant’s daughter planted her seed and tended it carefully, but nothing sprouted. She tried everything she could and asked gardeners for advice, without success. Six months later, she returned with an empty pot. All the other women carried beautiful flowers.","The prince walked through the hall, examining every pot. Then he announced in a loud voice that he had chosen the servant’s daughter.","Everyone was astonished. The others protested. How could he choose the only woman who had brought no flower?","The prince explained:","“She grew the most precious flower and brought it to me: honesty. Every seed I gave you was fake; none of them could bloom.”","Moral: The most valuable adornment is honesty. A person rises not by outward appearance, but by true words and a clear conscience.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"goose-to-be-plucked","title":"The Goose to Be Plucked","paragraphs":["On a cold winter day, a king changed his clothes and went out walking, taking his chief vizier with him. Beside a ravine they met an elderly man soaking hides in the water and beating them.","The king greeted the old man:","“Peace be upon you, venerable elder.”","“And upon you, ruler of the world,” the old man replied.","“What did you do during the six?” the king asked.","“Unless you add six to six, it does not come to thirty-two,” the tanner replied.","The king asked again:","“Did you never rise in the night?”","“We did, Your Majesty, but others benefited.”","The king laughed and asked his last question:","“If I send you a goose, will you pluck it?”","“Certainly—and without making it cry out.”","The king and vizier walked on. The king asked:","“Did you understand our conversation?”","“No, Your Majesty,” the chief vizier answered in distress.","The king grew angry.","“Find out what it meant by this evening, or you will lose your head.”","After taking the king to the palace, the frightened vizier hurried back in alarm to the ravine. The tanner was still at work.","“What were you and the king talking about?” he asked.","The old man looked him up and down.","“Forgive me, I will not say it for nothing. Give me a hundred gold pieces and I will tell you.”","The vizier handed over the coins.","“You greeted the king as ruler of the world. How did you know he was the king?”","“I’m a tanner. No one but a king could wear the fur he had on.”","The vizier scratched his head.","“Then what did ‘Unless you add six to six, it does not come to thirty-two’ mean?”","The tanner collected another hundred coins before answering.","“He asked whether I had worked during the six months of summer, since I was working in this winter cold. I told him I had to work all twelve months to feed the thirty-two teeth in my mouth.”","The vizier went on:","“And what did he mean about rising in the night?”","The tanner accepted another hundred coins.","“He was asking whether I had children. I told him I had children, but all of them were girls; they had married and were of benefit to others.”","The vizier lowered his head.","“And the goose he said he would send?”","The tanner laughed.","“That one you can work out for yourself.”","Moral: A wise word is revealed to those who understand it. Not everyone understands what they hear — some answers a person must find for themselves.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"strongest-shield","title":"The Strongest Shield","paragraphs":["The strongest shield in life is distance.","Moral: Sometimes the distance a person keeps is what protects him best. Being too close to everyone can hurt a person.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-best-things","title":"The Three Best Things","paragraphs":["A father told his son:","“In life, aim for the finest food, the finest bed, and the finest home.”","The son asked:","“But we’re poor. How can we have those?”","His father replied:","“If you eat when you are hungry, that will be your best food. If you sleep tired after hard work, that will be your best sleep. And when you make room for others in your heart, you have the finest home of all.”","Moral: The best food is found in hunger, the best sleep after hard work, and the best home is found in hearts. Happiness depends not only on means, but also on how you look at life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"think-speak-and-act-positively","title":"Think, Speak, Act Positively","paragraphs":["Think positively, because your thoughts become your words.","Speak positively, because your words open the way to your actions.","Act positively, because your actions become your habits.","Develop positive habits, because your habits shape your values.","Hold your values high, because they affect your destiny.","Moral: Thought becomes word, word becomes deed, deed becomes habit, and habit becomes destiny. A positive destiny begins with positive thought.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"try-to-think-this-way","title":"Try to Think This Way","paragraphs":["Don’t say “I don’t do this well.” Say “Maybe there is something I don’t know? I’m sure that if I refresh my knowledge and work at it, I can do it.”","Don’t say “I have given up on this.” Say “Maybe I can solve it another way? Let me try and see; maybe it will work.”","Don’t say “This is very hard.” Say “It is hard, but not impossible; it needs a little extra time and effort.”","Don’t say “The work I do satisfies me; I can’t do better.” Say “By working on myself I can improve and get a better result.”","Don’t say “I made a mistake; I wish I hadn’t.” Say “It isn’t the end of the world; mistakes help me learn.”","Don’t say “He is very intelligent; I can’t be like him.” Say “If I learn how he achieved this, I can gain the same success.”","Moral: Changing your mindset is the first step to changing your life. Saying “I can't” closes the door. Saying “I can learn” opens up new possibilities. Failure is not the end, it is an opportunity for growth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"turning-hardship-into-opportunity","title":"Turning Hardship into Opportunity","paragraphs":["Sometimes in life, things go very smoothly, and sometimes we face unexpected difficulties. The important thing is to be able to turn the difficulties we encounter into opportunities.","Moral: The same hardship breaks one person and strengthens another. Try to learn from the obstacle before you and turn it into an opportunity to move forward.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"who-handles-honey-licks-his-finger","title":"Who Handles Honey Licks His Finger","paragraphs":["A beekeeper filled containers with the honey he had collected and brought them to a grocer to sell. They struck a bargain on the price and began to weigh the honey.","Because the grocer had only a one-kilo weight, he broke off pieces of honeycomb by hand and weighed the honey kilo by kilo. But before placing each piece on the scale, he never failed to lick his honey-smeared fingers.","Once, twice, three times… The beekeeper saw that some of the honey never reached the scale — it stayed on the grocer’s fingers and went to his tongue. He frowned and showed his displeasure. The grocer paid no attention and kept weighing, licking his fingers as he went.","At last the beekeeper’s patience ran out:","“Uncle, this won’t do — you’ve licked away half the honey!”","Still licking his honeyed fingers, the grocer smiled slyly and replied:","“I have no ill intent, brother. Whoever handles honey is bound to lick a finger.”","Moral: Even a small share in a job usually brings some gain from it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"you-cannot-descend-a-well-on-his-rope","title":"You Cannot Go Down a Well on His Rope","paragraphs":["A master named Ali, who made ropes from flax fibers, worked carelessly, producing rotten ropes that often snapped and caused accidents. People began calling him “Ali of the rotten ropes.”","One day, a lamb fell into a deep well. An acquaintance who meant to climb down asked the lamb’s owner for a rope, but he did not like the rope that was brought.","“I know this rope. Ali made it. You can’t go down a well on one of his ropes.”","“Don’t be unfair. You can go down a well on Master Ali’s rope,” the lamb’s owner answered with a smile. “Whether you can climb back out on that same rope, I cannot say.”","Moral: The quality of your work earns you trust, while poor work brings your name into disrepute. Lost trust is hard to restore.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","blurb":"The power of speech, the wisdom of silence, and right conversation.","count":12,"stories":[{"stem":"communication","title":"Communication","paragraphs":["If you often communicate with older people and with children, you will find experience in one and innocence in the other.","Moral: Experience is learned from the elderly, and purity from the child. Every stage of life has something to teach us.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"tongue","title":"Language","paragraphs":["Language is a flower; it grows in the mouth,","without care and nourishment, it withers.","Moral: If the language is not protected, it will wither and disappear like a flower that is not watered. Keeping your mother tongue alive means using it lovingly every day.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"silence","title":"Silence","paragraphs":["The answer to some questions is silence.","Either it's not the time for an answer,","or the person asking has not earned a reply.","* * *","Darvish Jalal Ruhavi","Moral: You do not have to answer every question. Sometimes the time has not come, and sometimes the person before you is not worthy of an answer.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"silent-corridor","title":"The Silent Corridor","paragraphs":["There were two organizations working together — one was the sponsor, the other was the project development agency.","The project team worked tirelessly for months, prepared the documents, and one day, when everything was ready, they sent the materials to the sponsor and began to wait for a response. Days passed, weeks passed. The result — silence.","The uncertainty within the team grew, spreading like a shadow along the corridor:","“Have the documents arrived? Have they been reviewed?”","No one knew. Inquiries to the sponsor brought only evasive, uncertain answers.","A few months later, the team decided to go directly to the sponsor’s office to clarify the matter. There, the truth finally emerged:","“Yes, we received the documents, but we have not accepted them. The submission format was wrong, and the required procedure hadn’t been followed.”","The team froze in place.","“Why didn’t you tell us that?” they asked.","“We could have corrected it right away.”","Frustrated, the employees returned to the office and complained to their management:","“We should have followed up, checked the status, and spoken clearly with the other side. Our time and effort were wasted, and our trust was shaken because the corridor between us stayed silent.”","Moral: When communication is unclear, even the best work can go unnoticed. When there is no consistent follow-up, even the strongest partnership weakens. Sometimes the greatest harm to a partnership is done not by failure, but by silence.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"saying-the-word-in-its-place","title":"The Right Words at the Right Time","paragraphs":["Whoever can say a word in its proper place will not be forced to apologize.","Moral: Weigh a word before you say it, so that you will not have to apologize later. The right words at the right moment are precious.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"say-what-you-know","title":"Speak What You Know","paragraphs":["Share what you know without lecturing others. Listen carefully without assuming that everything you hear is true.","Silence does not prove ignorance, any more than endless talk means knowing a great deal. Belittling someone is foolish; excessive flattery shows a lack of courage.","Moral: Speak honestly, listen thoughtfully, and know when to be silent. See people as they are, without contempt or flattery.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"candles-conversation","title":"The Candles’ Conversation","paragraphs":["Four candles burned in a room so quiet that their voices could be heard.","The first candle said, “I am FAITH. But no one helps me keep burning. I think I will soon go out.”","Its flame dwindled until it went out altogether.","The second candle said, “I am LOYALTY. Sadly, no one needs me anymore. There is no reason for me to keep burning.”","As soon as it finished speaking, a gentle breeze put it out.","The third candle spoke sadly. “I am LOVE. I have no strength left to burn. People have forgotten me, and my worth has disappeared. Even those closest to me have forgotten how to love.”","LOVE could no longer endure it and went out too.","Just then, a child entered the room and saw the three dark candles. “Why aren’t you burning? Weren’t you supposed to burn forever?” the child asked, beginning to cry.","The fourth candle spoke. “Don’t be afraid. As long as I am burning, we can light the others again. I am HOPE.”","The child’s eyes sparkled with joy. Taking the candle of HOPE, the child relit the others one by one.","Moral: When faith, loyalty, and love go out, hope is what lights them again. If we protect the light of hope, the other values will revive too.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"one-who-knows-and-the-one-who-does-not","title":"The One Who Knows and the One Who Does Not","paragraphs":["(Chinese Proverb)","Those who do not know and recognize it yearn to learn. Teach them.","Those who know but do not realize it are asleep. Awaken them.","Those who do not know, and do not know that they do not know, are ignorant. Stay away from them.","Those who know and know that they know are leaders. Follow them.","Moral: Teach the one who yearns to learn, awaken the one who is heedless, stay away from the ignorant who thinks he knows everything, and follow the one who really knows.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"shepherds-word","title":"The Shepherd's Word","paragraphs":["Once upon a time, in a village, there lived a poor but honest shepherd named Hasan. One day, the village agha called him and said:","“Hasan, I hear you know the mountain pastures well. Look after my herd this summer. If in the autumn my animals come back healthy and well fed, I’ll give you a cow.”","Hasan agreed. All summer, through rain and heat, he tended the herd and kept watch at night without closing his eyes. In the fall, he brought the animals home even fatter and healthier than before.","Hasan came to the agha, hoping to receive the promised cow. The agha smiled mockingly and said:","“I never meant to give you a cow. I only said that to motivate you to work. Besides, what would a poor man like you do with one?”","Hasan's eyes filled with tears. But he answered calmly and with dignity:","“You’re right that I’m poor. But I have a conscience, and I am the master of my word. You may have money, but if your word has no value, you are the one who is truly poor.”","The story spread through the village. People lost respect for the agha and avoided doing business with him. Too late, he learned that a person’s worth is set by loyalty to one’s word, not by wealth or position.","Moral: A person’s true wealth is not property, but his word, his conscience, and his trustworthiness.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-is-a-word","title":"What Is a Word?","paragraphs":["A word can wound;","a word can be a pearl.","Moral: With a word one can break a heart, and with a word one can win a heart. What turns a word into a pearl is how and where you say it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"word-and-silence","title":"Words and Silence","paragraphs":["Sometimes a person says more by keeping quiet. Because words are not just sounds — they are a mirror of intention.","If a word will break a heart, if a sentence will extinguish hope — it is not worth saying.","If the words you are going to say will not warm a heart, if they will not cause a smile — be silent.","Remember... not every word blooms. Some are like thorns — they hurt the one who touches them.","Silence is wisdom. Sometimes it is the most valuable answer.","Words spoken from the heart turn into wisdom. Words spoken without thought cause harm and open a wound.","Silence. Because silence also speaks — sometimes more deeply, sometimes more honestly.","Moral: Not every word blooms; some hurt like thorns. Silence is wiser than words that will not bring good.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"shepherd-must-be-called","title":"Call the Shepherd","paragraphs":["A student who wanted to complete the education he had pursued for many years asked his master for a blessing.","The master replied:","“All right, but I have one condition. I will ask you a question, to see how you have taken in what I taught you over these years. If you answer correctly, I will know my effort was not wasted and I will give you my blessing.”","The student said confidently:","“Ask me, Master.”","The master said:","“Imagine you are returning to your village when a shepherd’s pack of rabid dogs attacks you. You are face to face with danger. What will you do to save your life?”","“I’ll throw stones to drive them away.”","“You may throw a stone at one and at a second, but you cannot deal with all of them. They will join together and tear you apart.”","“Then I’ll run.”","“Be sure they will run faster than you and catch you. What then?”","The student realized that he was helpless and asked his master for help.","The master explained:","“My child, there is only one way out of this: you must call the shepherd and ask him to hold his dogs. The moment they hear his voice, they will freeze where they stand.”","Moral: Not every problem can be solved by force. Sometimes taking a step back and looking at it differently is the shortest way out.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","blurb":"Meaning, choices, and ways of thinking.","count":47,"stories":[{"stem":"different-path","title":"A Different Path","paragraphs":["One day, a cow returning to its shed crossed a forest where there was no path. Unable to choose the right path, it went uphill and downhill, leaving a crooked, difficult trail behind it.","The next day, a dog came to the same place. To reach the other side of the forest, it followed the cow’s trail. Then came a flock of sheep. Seeing a ready-made path, the shepherd led the flock along it.","Before long, people began using the trail as well. They complained about its difficulty as they turned left and right, ducked beneath branches, and twisted their way through the bushes. Yet no one thought to look for a better route.","Through constant use, the trail gradually became a road. Unaware that the other side of the forest could be reached in only half an hour, people and heavily burdened animals spent three hours following the cow’s winding path.","Years passed. The small road first became the town’s main route and eventually its most important street. Although it was the least convenient of the existing roads, everyone merely complained about the traffic.","Watching it all, the old and wise forest pitied the people who blindly followed the familiar road without ever asking whether it was the right one.","Moral: The path everyone follows is not always the best one. Sometimes those who step beyond habit and search for a new route reach their destination more quickly and easily.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"human-emerged","title":"A Human Being Emerged","paragraphs":["I joined thought with the heart—and love emerged.","I gathered love with labor—and loyalty emerged.","I shared bread according to my conscience—and justice emerged.","I brought love, labor, conscience, loyalty, and justice together—and a human being emerged.","— Yaşar Kemal","Moral: When love, labor, conscience, loyalty, and justice come together, a true human being is formed. Humanity is the union of these values.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"life-lesson","title":"A Life Lesson","paragraphs":["While teaching his cub about life, a father wolf pointed to some sheep:","“Their meat is delicious.”","Then he pointed to the shepherd:","“But a blow from his staff is bitter. Be careful.”","Seeing the dog beside the shepherd, the cub exclaimed happily:","“Father, look! That one resembles us.”","The father replied:","“Run the moment you see it, my son. We have suffered most from those who look like us but are not truly one of us.”","Moral: Sometimes the greatest harm comes from those who resemble us but whose intention is different. To protect yourself, do not be deceived by outward likeness, and recognize hypocrisy in time.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"more-effective-way","title":"A More Effective Way","paragraphs":["A hunter walking through a forest came upon an unusual scene. A woodcutter had been struggling for a long time to cut down a tree, swinging the axe with all his strength. His face was drenched in sweat and his whole body was tense.","The hunter approached and asked:","“Why is the work going so hard and slowly? Your axe is blunt. Why don’t you sharpen it?”","The woodcutter stared at him in surprise:","“What are you talking about? Where would I find time to sharpen my axe? I still have twenty more trees to cut!”","Moral: Hard work is valuable, but labor that has not been thought through can be ineffective. Sometimes a short pause to sharpen the tool makes all the work that follows faster and easier.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"windmill-turning-in-still-air","title":"A Windmill Turning in Still Air","paragraphs":["If a windmill turns when the air is still, someone must be blowing on its sails.","Eskimo proverb","Moral: Nothing happens without a cause. Even if we do not see the force behind an event, we can understand its effect from the result.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"bookstore","title":"At the Bookstore","paragraphs":["After a long search, a medical student found the book he needed at the Bilge bookstore in Bursa. When he saw the price on the back, his smile vanished: he could not afford it.","Hiding his disappointment, he leafed through the pages, found the section assigned by his professor, and—seeing that the owners were busy with customers—read it quickly before leaving.","For the next month, the student returned every other day and studied among the shelves. One week before his graduation examinations, he came again, but the book was gone. He searched every shelf; it seemed to have vanished.","As the pale-faced young man was about to leave, the owners stopped him:","“Please sit down. We have something to tell you.”","One bookseller reached from under his table and produced the book:","“A customer asked the price recently and wanted to buy it. I took it off the shelf and set it aside for you. You can keep reading it here.”","After completing his studies and becoming a doctor, the young man never forgot the two booksellers. He never charged patients who could not afford treatment.","Moral: A small act of kindness can leave a lifelong mark on the heart. Those who receive kindness often carry it forward to others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"doctors-appointment","title":"At the Doctor’s Office","paragraphs":["I was waiting for my turn at a doctor’s office. Having missed breakfast at home, I bought a sandwich and apple juice from the café and began to eat.","A boy sitting near the window was watching me closely. His expression seemed to say, “I am hungry too.” Feeling sorry for him, I returned to the café and bought him a sandwich and apple juice.","The boy ate eagerly, while I felt pleased with what I believed was a good deed.","At that moment, his mother emerged from the registration room. Seeing the scene, she hurried over in alarm:","“What have you done? The doctor had told us to bring the child to the examination hungry. I took time off work, and we’ve come such a long way. Now we’ll have to do it all again tomorrow.”","The woman could not hold back her tears. I stood frozen: while believing I was helping, I had undone an entire family’s effort.","Moral: Judgment based only on emotion and assumption can lead us to the wrong decision. Asking about the situation before helping is itself part of compassion.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"day-gratefully-lived","title":"I have lived another day...","paragraphs":["The day began... The day ended...","Every morning when we open our eyes, we see that a new day has begun. Yet often we do not notice when evening arrives or when another day slips away from our lives. Time flows like a quiet river, while we stand on its bank, watching the passing moments drift away.","Sometimes we ask ourselves: What wisdom lay behind God's gift of this day to me? Did I cherish this divine trust? What did I add to this day, and what did I take from it? What light did I bring to my soul, and what opportunity did I let slip away?","The greatest treasure in a human life is time. For other things we lose may sometimes return, but not even a single moment that has passed comes back. That is why every day, every hour, and even every breath has its own value and meaning.","And so, I have lived another day... In the endless book of time, another page of my life has turned. Only God truly knows what was written on that page.","Moral: Every new day is a precious trust given to us. Reflecting at the end of the day on how we have spent it helps us live more meaningfully tomorrow.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"small-changes-big-meaning","title":"Small changes, great value...","paragraphs":["A monotonous life quietly wears down the human spirit. When every day is the same as the one before, when the same tasks are repeated, or when time passes without our doing anything at all, an invisible emptiness and a sense of inner unease take hold. For the spirit, like life itself, longs for movement, change, and something new.","Sometimes a small change enters our lives, and it is as though a new window opens within us. Ordinary days suddenly take on color, and our hearts fill with a pleasant excitement, a zest for life, and a sense of well-being. In truth, the beauty of life lies not only in the chance events that come our way, but also in how much beauty and freshness we ourselves can bring into it.","We should not be mere spectators in our own lives. Sometimes choosing a new path, meeting someone new, seeing a new place, learning, creating, or making time for something we love is enough to change the color of life. For something new need not be a major event; sometimes a small step is the beginning of a great uplift in spirits.","Life is not a page handed to us already written. We write each day ourselves. It is therefore in our own hands to fill the pages of our lives not with repetition, but with new feelings, new dreams, and beautiful memories.","Moral: We do not need to wait for major changes to bring something new into our lives. Even a little time devoted to learning and the things we love can give meaning to our day.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"blind-who-have-eyes","title":"The Blind Who Have Eyes","paragraphs":["The world is full of blind people who think they see because they have eyes.","Moral: True sight requires understanding as well as eyes. A person who looks but does not see, and hears but does not understand, is truly blind.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"blind-well","title":"The Dry Well","paragraphs":["One day, a villager’s donkey fell into a dry well. For hours it cried out in distress. Its owner and the neighbors came to help, but after much discussion they decided there was no way to pull it out. They saw no option but to fill the well with earth. Each took a shovel and began throwing soil into the well.","The donkey shook each shovelful of earth off its back and stepped onto it. Little by little, the rising ground carried it higher, until it reached the rim and climbed out. The villagers stared in amazement as it emerged unharmed.","Moral: Shake off the burdens that fall on you and use them to climb higher. The way out of helplessness is not in whimpering, but in shaking it off and struggling.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"diderot-effect","title":"The Diderot Effect","paragraphs":["The eighteenth-century French writer and Enlightenment thinker Denis Diderot fell deeply into debt. News of his difficulties reached the Russian empress Catherine.","The empress offered to help by buying his library and allowing him to keep it. She also paid him twenty-five years’ salary in advance to serve as its librarian. This unexpected fortune cleared his debts and lifted a great weight from his shoulders.","Then one day, a friend gave him a luxurious red velvet robe. That was where it all began.","Diderot put on the robe and sat down to work. Suddenly, he thought that the beautiful velvet robe and the desk did not suit each other.","Emboldened by his newfound wealth, he bought a magnificent desk to match his robe.","But now the old carpet seemed out of place beside both the robe and the desk, so he hurried and bought a carpet suited to his money and to himself.","Still, something felt wrong. The armchairs, cupboards, chairs, paintings, and ornaments all looked old and mismatched.","Now everything offended his eye.","Before long, Diderot had replaced everything in the house.","Eventually he realized that his endless desire for more had brought him back to where he had started.","He later wrote his famous work titled “I Regret Parting with My Old Robe.” He expressed the situation he had fallen into in a single sentence:","“I was the master of my old robe, but I became the slave of the new one.”","Moral: Buying something new can make us feel compelled to replace everything around it. This is known as the Diderot effect. Desire has no natural stopping point; it is better to be master of the old than slave of the new.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-dream","title":"Dreaming","paragraphs":["Allah does not make you dream of something that He will not grant.","Moral: Every dream that falls into the heart has a purpose. Allah does not place in the heart a wish He will not grant — do not lose hope.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"spare-time","title":"Spare Time","paragraphs":["Some people talk to you when they have time to spare. Others make time to talk to you.","Moral: Time is the only wealth that never comes back. Appreciate the person who willingly spends it for you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"from-the-memoirs-of-a-lady-from-istanbul","title":"An Istanbul Woman Remembers","paragraphs":["It was 1919. Istanbul was completely under British occupation. I had just finished middle school.","I was a pretty young woman, and matchmakers often visited our home. One day, a handsome, tall young man came with his representatives. He introduced himself as a lawyer. I liked him, and we became engaged. I loved him and dreamed of making a happy home. Until morning I stayed up by lamplight, weaving little towels for my dowry.","One day, a rumor spread in the neighborhood.","“They say Ayesha’s fiancé isn’t a lawyer at all. He earns his living carrying coffins in front of the mosque.”","I was devastated. My father and I went to the mosque, watched from a distance, and saw that he was really carrying a coffin.","I was heartbroken. We returned the engagement and parted.","Five years passed. I married someone else and had a child. Our country was free again.","One day I met him in the city. My son was with me.","He stopped when he saw me. He said hesitantly, “If you have time, I would like to offer you a cup of tea.” I agreed. We went into an office.","It was a law firm; his name was written on the sign on the door. His assistants were working inside.","“Are you really a lawyer?” I asked.","“Yes,” he said.","“But if you are a lawyer, why were you carrying a coffin in front of the mosque?”","He bowed his head and said, “Forgive me.”","“Istanbul was under occupation. British soldiers were everywhere, searching people and inspecting everything. We disguised arms shipments as funerals, sending coffins full of weapons to the National Forces in Anatolia. It was a vital duty for the country. I couldn’t tell even you.”","Moral: Do not judge hastily by what you see; behind every behavior there may be a truth that you don’t know. Our freedom is owed to the labor and the sacrifices of selfless people.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hayats-life-story","title":"Hayat’s Life Story","paragraphs":["When my mother left our home, my father, and us, I was six and my brother was eight. Even at that age I understood why she left my father, but I could not understand why she left us. Would a mother abandon her children?","My father used to beat my mother. He beat me and my brother too. I came to believe that all men beat their wives and children…","After my mother left, home became four walls. In the mornings, no one said, \"Wash your hands and faces and come; breakfast is ready.\" No one even asked whether we were hungry, though we might go days without food. The hardest lesson was this: without a mother’s scent, children grow up in an instant, however old they are. I grew up at six.","About ten days after my mother left, the police came to our house. They said she had taken her own life. In her hand she had been clutching an envelope. On it was written: “To be given to my daughter and my son.”","I could not read then — how was I supposed to read it? My brother read it. I cried as I listened. My brother cried too.","My mother had gone to her father’s house, but he flew into a rage. In his eyes, a woman had to stay with her husband. Even with a mouth full of blood, she was expected to say, “I’ve been drinking red sherbet.” My mother begged him: “Let me stay for a month. Then I’ll find a way to take my children and start again.”","But a woman living alone? To my grandfather, that would make his honor worth five kopecks. He would never be able to hold his head up again; he said he would rather see his daughter dead. That evening, my mother hanged herself. My grandmother told me this years later, on her deathbed, when I was nineteen.","When my father heard of my mother’s death, he was not upset at all. He said he would put us in an orphanage. From then on we would live there; that would be our home. Brother and sister might be parted, forget each other, and years later fail to know each other — so we were both to keep Mother’s letter.","But before I could ask how the two of us would keep one letter, my brother cut it in half with scissors. He gave me the beginnings of the sentences. Since I had the openings, once I learned to read I would have to guess the rest. My brother had already memorized the letter anyway.","When my aunt heard we were to be sent to an orphanage, she came. She told my father: “A girl is not given to an orphanage. I will take Hayat and raise her myself.” Living with my aunt, I understood why: a girl meant an unpaid servant ordered about the house. Not once did my aunt call me by my name. My name became “Come-here”: “Come-here, fetch water… Come-here, wash these dishes…”","Once a month my brother visited me at my aunt’s. He said he was fine in the orphanage. I said I was fine too — I did not want to upset him. Did he also say “I’m fine” so I would not worry? I never dared ask.","I had started school. I was the first in the class to learn to read. How could I not — I was going to read my mother’s letter. The part I still had began: “Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I you…” Every night in bed I tried to finish that sentence:","“Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I love you so much.”","“Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I miss you terribly.”","“Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I am waiting for you.”","I built a different dream around each ending. In those dreams, I was happy. Who chooses to dream of sorrow?","I finished middle school. My aunt said I would not go to school anymore. But I wanted to study — to stand on my own feet and live under one roof with my brother.","That summer the wife of Uncle Hasan, the neighbourhood grocer with three children, died of cancer. I was heartbroken. What would the three children do — how would they find a mother’s scent? Why did mothers die? Would those three forget their names as I had, and become “Come-here” too?","While I was grieving for Uncle Hasan’s children, it came out that I was betrothed to him. It turned out I had been sold for eight bracelets and three thousand liras. Because I was too young for a civil marriage, forty days later our religious marriage was performed.","I was fifteen. I was Uncle Hasan’s wife. I had three children, aged two, five, and six. And my aunt insisted we must have a child of our own… I was supposed to have a child so my place would be secure and Uncle Hasan would not look at other women.","The first slap from Uncle Hasan came because I called him “Uncle Hasan.” Does a woman call her husband “uncle”? When I lost my mother at six, I learned to keep silent. So I did not ask, “Does a man call a fifteen-year-old girl his wife?” — I stayed quiet.","When Uncle Hasan hit me, all three children clung to their father’s legs: “Don’t hit Sister Hayat — she plays with us, she tells us stories,” they begged. I was those children’s older sister. What I called stories were my dreams.","When Uncle Hasan went out, I practiced saying “Hasan” in front of the mirror. But every time I said it, I pictured his thinning hair, his glasses slipping down his nose, and his large belly. It was as if I had sinned; I was ashamed, and I could not bring his name to my tongue. While I was practicing in front of the mirror, his six-year-old son came up to me. “Sister Hayat, Mother used to call him ‘Bey,’” he said. “You could call him that too.”","“Bey” — of course, “Bey” would be better. I bent and kissed dark-eyed Sami, who listened to my dreams as if they were fairy tales. I called five-year-old Elif and two-year-old Zahra and began to tell them a story. That day I began: “Once upon a time there was a six-year-old girl named Nagil. Nagil had lost her mother. She searched everywhere; when she could not find her, she went to the land of dreams. There Nagil was so happy that she never returned to the real world.”","In my tale Nagil was always happy, always smiling. Every day I told the children that story. We were very happy.","Uncle Hasan was good too. I now called him “Bey.” From time to time he grew angry, but I understood why: he had thought he was buying a wife for eight bracelets and three thousand liras, but he had brought home a sister who told the children stories.","My brother came to visit. Each time he wanted to give me his half of Mother’s letter. I would not take it. I was afraid that if I read the other half, I would fall from the land of dreams into a cruel real world and be unhappy. All my dreams were built on that other half.","By the time I was forty, the children who had listened to my stories were grown. Sami had become a doctor and, on assignment, moved to another city. Whenever he felt low, he called and said, “Sister Hayat, tell me a story.”","Elif had become a teacher and married. I had a granddaughter. They badly wanted to name her Hayat; I would not allow it. Elif said, “Then let her name be Nagil.” So my granddaughter’s name was Nagil.","My youngest, Zahra, became a veterinarian. “Sister Hayat, whenever an animal is restless I tell it a story, and it calms down,” she would say. Zahra married too, and I had a grandson.","When I was forty-five, Uncle Hasan—my Bey—died. I grieved for him. His last words were a plea for forgiveness, and I forgave him with all my heart. He was a good person.","But there are others I cannot forgive:","those who see no wrong in marrying off fifteen-year-old girls, and those who support them;","those who call it a disgrace when their daughters, whom they know suffer violence every day, seek a divorce, and those who justify that way of thinking;","those who show no mercy to their own children left helpless and hopeless like wounded birds, and those who support them —","These people I cannot forgive.","Moral: Blind custom and a false idea of “honor” can destroy a woman’s life, a child’s life. Those who look away from early marriage, violence, and neglect are partners in that oppression.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-i-dont-do-it-these-days-the-world-wont-collapse","title":"If I Don't Do It These Days, the World Won't Collapse","paragraphs":["When I asked one of our employees where the assigned task stood, his reply disappointed me deeply.","“The world won’t fall apart if I don’t do it these days.”","True, I thought, the world would not fall apart. But what about the trust between us? Will that not collapse too? Would that attitude not harm our working relationship, our mutual respect, and the work we shared?","A delay of a few days was not the real issue. It was his attitude toward a commitment. Responsibility is not only doing the work, but standing by the word you gave, living up to the other person’s trust, and making sure people can rely on you.","Approaching any commitment you have taken on so irresponsibly is not right. Even more regrettable was how that attitude was expressed. To me, such an answer sounds at least rude and shows that the other side’s time, labor, and trust are not valued enough.","I admit that these words broke my heart. I knew then that we could not continue working together in quite the same way. A professional relationship depends on trust, and when trust is shaken, the partnership also faces a serious test.","Most disappointing of all, my colleague neither recognized that his words were wrong, nor apologized, nor completed the task. Because my trust in him had been shaken, I was forced to solve the problem another way.","The experience reminded me that an organization’s most valuable asset is not time, money, or technology, but the trust people place in one another. It can take years to build and can sometimes be lost with a single sentence.","Moral: Trust is an organization’s most valuable wealth. It takes years to earn and one sentence to lose. The person who stands by his word protects that wealth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-the-door-before-you-wont-open-it-is-not-your-door","title":"A Door That Will Not Open May Not Be Yours","paragraphs":["One day Jamal went to his manager, Salim Bey, and told him he had heard there would be a promotion at work and that he himself had expectations. Salim Bey said he was satisfied with Jamal’s work, but then dismissed him. “You’re not the right fit for us,” he said. Jamal left deeply disappointed.","Two years later, they met again and exchanged friendly greetings. Jamal said he had started his own company and that business was going well. Salim Bey was delighted.","“In fact, I owe you thanks,” Jamal said. “You let me go instead of keeping me somewhere I didn’t belong. Leaving that job opened new opportunities before me. I began doing the work I love. I’m very happy now.”","Moral: A closed door can lead to a better path. Being deprived of something good sometimes leads to something better.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"it-is-in-vain","title":"It Is in Vain","paragraphs":["It is futile to advise a fool, to argue with an ignorant person, to befriend a two-faced person.","Moral: When you choose whom to spend time with, you also choose how you will live your life. Time spent arguing with the ignorant and on friendship with the two-faced is wasted.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"it-wont-go-to-waste","title":"It Won’t Go to Waste","paragraphs":["A kindness done from the heart,","an act shaped by love,","a word spoken in sincerity,","behavior guided by courtesy,","a smile warmed by affection,","a step taken for truth and justice,","respect shown to moral values —","never goes to waste. It lights the way for others. It also brings light, health, and beauty to your own life.","Moral: Kindness, words, and deeds from the heart never vanish; they light others’ way and brighten your life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"lecturer-and-the-groom","title":"The Lecturer and the Stablehand","paragraphs":["A lecturer was invited to a village to enlighten the people. Only one man sat in the hall. After greeting him, the lecturer asked, “No one else has come. Should I give the talk anyway?”","The man replied:","“Sir, I look after twenty horses. If nineteen wandered off and only one remained, I would still feed and water that one.”","The lecturer began his talk. He spoke at great length, then asked:","“Well, how did you like it?”","“I’m only a stablehand, and I didn’t understand much,” the man replied. “But I can tell you this: I would not give one horse the fodder of twenty and make it burst.”","Moral: Good speaking is not measured by length. What matters is whether your listener can understand and absorb what you say.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"life-is-short","title":"Life Is Short","paragraphs":["Life is short. Spend it with people who bring you happiness and make you feel loved.","Moral: Live your short life with people who love you and make you smile. Do not spend your time on relationships that bring you no good.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"liver","title":"The Liver","paragraphs":["Why does the word for “liver” appear in expressions such as “jan-jiyar,” for a very close, sincere friend; “jiyarguşam,” for a beloved child; “aghjiyar,” for a cowardly and helpless person; and “jiyarparam,” for someone dearly loved?","The liver’s warm and bloody appearance impressed ancient peoples. They associated warm blood with life and its absence with death, and for a long time regarded the liver as the center of life.","The Babylonians, for example, believed the soul resided there. In some cultures, because the liver was thought to represent the emotions above all, in sayings and writings the liver comes before the heart.","In ancient Greece, virtue and related qualities such as courage and determination were attributed to the liver. If a person had a healthy liver, he was considered virtuous—he was perceived as someone who followed his principles, overcoming all obstacles.","Moral: Language does not arise by chance; behind every word there is a history, a belief. Our words are the memory of our past.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"living-in-the-past","title":"Living in the Past","paragraphs":["Living in the past is like walking while looking behind you. With your eyes turned away from the road ahead, you will keep stumbling and falling, unable either to change what lies behind you or to open the doors in front of you.","Moral: Someone who always looks back does not see the obstacle in front. You cannot bring the past back; turn your face forward and see the opportunities before you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"newtons-second-law","title":"Newton’s Second Law","paragraphs":["A physics teacher returned to the staff room after class. “How brainless these students are!” he complained to his colleagues. “I explained Newton’s second law three times. By the third time, even I understood it—but they still didn’t!”","Moral: Before teaching a subject, make sure you understand it yourself. Explaining something you do not understand helps neither the teacher nor the student.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"nothing-is-ever-truly-lost","title":"There Is No Such Thing as “I Lost”","paragraphs":["Do not see only loss in life. You also have successes you have gained, and lessons you have learned from failures.","Moral: There is no such thing as “losing” — you either gain or you learn. Every failure is in fact a lesson.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pawn-and-the-king","title":"The Pawn and the King","paragraphs":["Do not look down on a pawn.","One day, it may become a king.","Do not feel safe merely because you are a king.","One day, you may be checkmated.","Moral: Do not underestimate the humble or place too much faith in rank. A pawn can rise, and a king can fall.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sowing-millet-at-the-bottom","title":"Sowing Millet at the Bottom","paragraphs":["One day, the king went on a walk with his courtiers. In a village on the way, he saw an old man planting seedlings in his field. He called out to the elder from a distance:","“Old father, why plant saplings at your age? You may never taste their fruit.”","The elder replied to the king:","“That doesn’t matter, my son. We ate the fruit of trees planted before our time. Those who come after us will eat from these.”","This answer pleased the king and he ordered the elder to be given a bag of gold.","The elder pointed to the bag full of gold and said:","“You see? My trees have already borne fruit.”","Delighted, the king ordered another bag of gold. The old peasant was no ordinary man — he was worldly and shrewd.","“My son, the seedlings everyone else plants bear fruit once a year. Mine have already borne fruit twice.”","Pleased by this answer too, the king ordered the elder another bag of gold. But this time the vizier stepped in and tried to talk him out of it:","“Your Majesty, we should leave. At this rate, he’ll sow millet at the bottom of our treasury instead of seedlings in his field!”","Moral: A person does not live only for himself. We ate what those who came before us planted; we must plant for those who come after us.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"properties-of-water","title":"The Properties of Water","paragraphs":["Consider water:","If it befriends a tree, it keeps the tree alive.","It puts out a fire.","It washes away dirt.","Mixed with flour, it turns it into dough and makes it ready to bake.","Meeting sunlight, it forms a rainbow.","Flowing, it brings life.","If it stands still, it turns into a swamp and begins to stink.","The heart is like water: in company it is alive, and in loneliness it is lifeless.","Moral: The human heart is like water: it comes alive in company and stagnates and withers in loneliness. Other people are what keep a person alive.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"teacher-hello-do-you-remember-me","title":"Teacher, Hello — Do You Remember Me?","paragraphs":["“Teacher, hello! Do you remember me? You taught me at school.”","“Yes, in primary school. How good to see you after so many years. What do you do now?”","“I'm very glad to see you too. I've become a teacher like you.”","“Wonderful. What made you choose teaching?”","“Because you were an example to me and encouraged me to become a teacher. I wanted to help children grow and give them confidence to succeed in life.”","“I'm glad you think this way and chose to become a teacher. But you said I once inspired you and was an example. What exactly do you mean?”","“One boy's parents had bought him a fine watch. My parents couldn’t afford one, and I wanted it badly. During the long break, I stole it from his pocket. When he told you his watch had been stolen, you asked for it back. No one answered. You closed the door and told all the boys you would search their pockets. I thought this would be the most shameful moment of my life.","“You lined us up and asked us to cover our eyes. After finding the watch in my pocket, you kept searching everyone else. Then you returned it without saying who had taken it. All year you did not say a word about it to me or to my parents. That day you did not let my dignity be lowered; you kept me from being called a thief and a liar for a childish, thoughtless act, and you forgave me.","“That taught me what a teacher can be: a true teacher does not only give knowledge, but instills the right values, does not punish the mistake but teaches the student to learn from it, and binds the student to life. I decided to become that kind of teacher.”","The teacher listened and was astonished. The young man asked:","“Don’t you remember finding the watch in my pocket?”","“I remember the incident, but I didn’t know you had taken it.”","“How could you not know? You found it yourself!”","“Because while I was checking the students’ pockets, I had closed my eyes too.”","Moral: To protect a person’s dignity without shaming them — by closing your eyes — is true teaching. A person whose dignity is kept learns from the mistake and rises.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dead-remain-in-life","title":"The One Who Dies Is the One Who Remains in Life","paragraphs":["When two people love each other wholeheartedly and one dies, the one who truly dies is the one who remains in life.","Moral: The one left behind lives with longing and feels dead while still alive. Separation can make the depth of that love painfully clear.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"most-beautiful-places-in-the-world","title":"The Most Beautiful Places in the World","paragraphs":["The most beautiful places in the world to be are in the thoughts, prayers, and hearts of those you love.","Moral: The most beautiful place a person can be is in the hearts and prayers of those they love. The true place is not an address, but love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"road-to-the-cotton-field","title":"The Road to the Cotton Field","paragraphs":["We could barely read or write when a new teacher came to our village. Aydin became more than a teacher to us; he was like a brother and a father. Seeing the pitiful state of our school and of us pupils and learning that we would be forced to work in the cotton field after finishing school brought tears to his eyes.","He asked where one village road led. “To the cotton fields,” we answered. He also asked where the other road went. None of us knew where it went. We had never left the village and we thought the world consisted only of our village.","That day, he also asked us what we wanted to be when we grew up. We all remained silent because we didn't know what to answer...","He soon taught us to read and write. Days passed, and we could already read and write with ease. Then one day, he brought us many letters from people he said would be our pen pals: doctors, engineers, scientists, and others. For years, these strangers wrote about their work, the meaning of life, and the importance of science and education. Until then, none of us had imagined a future beyond growing cotton.","We had believed there was only one road open to us: the road to the cotton field.","The letters changed that. We learned where the other road led. Thanks to Teacher Aydin’s great effort, years after we graduated, we achieved great successes. Some of us are now preparing to become doctors, others engineers or teachers.","Years later, another letter came to us:","“My children... Always dream. Dreams will take you where you want to go. It is enough that you strive, work, and stay resolute until you reach your aim. I congratulate you with all my heart. I knew that you would be able to, and that you would succeed.”","“Your pen pal, Teacher Aydin.”","We were astonished. Our own teacher had written the letters that for years showed us the way and changed our lives.","Moral: A teacher not only showed the way to children, but also created new paths for them. For a person who can dream, the world no longer consists of just one path.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"secret-of-living-well-and-longevity","title":"The Secret of Living Well and of a Long Life","paragraphs":["Halve your portions, double your walks, triple your laughter, and place no limit on your love.","Tibetan proverb.","Moral: Eat in measure, walk a lot, laugh a lot, love from the heart. The secret of a happy life is sometimes in such simple things.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"no-need-to-be-sane-when-everyone-is-mad","title":"No Need to Be Sane When Everyone Is Mad","paragraphs":["A radiant man received a warning from above: “From tomorrow the taste of the water will change, and everyone who drinks it will lose their reason.” He hurried home and filled whatever jugs and bowls came to hand and sat down. The next day, the warning came true. For a week or two, he drank only his stored water. At last, unable to endure it, he went outside. But everyone he spoke to mocked him. “Are you mad? You belong in an asylum!” The poor man returned home, smashed his containers, drank from the same fountain as everyone else, and went to bed.","Moral: The crowd may condemn you for knowing the truth. Protect your mind, but do not exhaust yourself trying to prove yourself to everyone.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"this-boat-is-empty-too","title":"This Boat Is Empty Too","paragraphs":["My master used to say:","“When I was young, I often went to the lake to think in solitude. I had a little boat and would spend hours on the water, finding peace in the world of my thoughts.","“One morning at daybreak, as usual, I was sitting in my boat, sunk in deep thought.","“Suddenly, another boat struck mine. Furious at the interruption, I opened my eyes, ready to shout at whoever was responsible. But the boat was empty. There was no one to pour my anger on. I closed my eyes again and returned to my thoughts. By the time the sun rose, my heart was completely calm.","“That empty boat taught me something. Now, when someone tries to hurt me, I tell myself:","“‘This boat is empty too.’”","Moral: If a person wants to maintain his peace, he should not look for evil intentions behind every incident. Sometimes what worries us is just a coincidence; it is our choice whether to magnify it and turn it into anger.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-questions","title":"Three Questions","paragraphs":["The king called the vizier to him and said:","“Answer three questions by tomorrow or you will be expelled from the palace. What does Allah patiently bear? Which truths does Allah keep hidden? And what does He bring about through His wisdom and power?”","The vizier thought hard but found no answers. In the evening he returned home in despair. His clever servant noticed his distress and asked what was wrong, and the vizier told him what had happened.","The servant said:","“I know the answers. I’ll give you two now and the third tomorrow. Allah bears His servants’ sorrows and conceals their faults.”","The next day, the vizier went to the king and told him these two answers. The king said:","“Correct. Did you find these answers yourself, or did someone teach you?”","The vizier answered honestly:","“My servant taught me.”","The king ordered the vizier to hand his robe to the servant. The vizier took off his robe of office, put it on the servant, and himself put on the servant’s old clothes.","Stunned, the vizier asked:","“And the third answer? What does Allah bring about through His wisdom and power?”","The servant smiled.","“Have you still not understood? In one moment Allah makes the vizier a servant and the servant a vizier.”","Moral: The one who is vizier today may be a servant tomorrow, so let no one be complacent about his seat.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-landscapes","title":"Three Scenes","paragraphs":["On a journey, a young man witnessed three puzzling scenes.","First, he saw people sowing wheat that sprang up and ripened as soon as it touched the soil. Yet instead of harvesting it, the farmers burned the field.","Next, he saw a man struggling to lift a large stone. Only after he placed a second stone on top of it could he raise it. With a third, it became easier still. Bewildered, the traveler continued.","Finally, he saw a man riding a sheep, with others clinging to his shoulders. The sheep ran so swiftly that the people chasing it could not catch up.","However much he pondered what he had seen, he could find no meaning in it, and he reached a city. At the city gate he greeted a radiant old man. The old man returned the greeting, stopped him, and drew him into talk. The young man told what he had seen along the way, and the elder explained:","“The farmers represent people who do good, then talk about the good they did here and there and reduce its value to nothing.","“The stones represent sin. The first wrongdoing weighs heavily. But without repentance, later sins come very easily to him. A sin without repentance is a door opened to another sin.","“The sheep is an animal of paradise, carrying its riders there. The first rider represents scholars, and those holding on are believers from every walk of life. Those left chasing behind are people without faith.”","Moral: The three scenes speak of good whose value is talked away, of sin without repentance, and of following a true guide — each is a hidden lesson of life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-statues","title":"The Three Statues","paragraphs":["Two neighboring rulers were close friends. For one ruler’s birthday, the other invited the best sculptor in his country and commissioned three identical gold statues, each a handspan high. A hidden difference between them was known only to him and the sculptor.","The statues were ready and were sent to the neighboring ruler on his birthday. The letter accompanying the gift said: “I congratulate you on your birthday with these three golden statues. At first glance, the statues may seem exactly the same. But one of them is more valuable than the other two. When you find that statue, let me know.”","The recipient weighed the statues, but they weighed exactly the same, to the gram. Further examination revealed nothing.","He summoned experts from across the country. They studied the statues for days without solving the puzzle.","At last they found a man who could solve it, brought him to the palace, and he asked for a fine wire. He passed it into the first statue’s ear; it emerged from the other ear. In the second, it came out through the mouth. In the third, it stayed inside. The difference was clear.","The ruler had a reply written to the neighbor who sent the statues and sent it:","“These statues represent three kinds of people:","those who let what they hear go in one ear and out the other;","those who immediately repeat it;","and those who keep it in their hearts.","“The third is the most valuable: a person who keeps a confidence and knows when it is right to speak. Thank you very much for this valuable gift.”","Moral: The most valuable person is the one who can keep what he hears as a secret in his heart and only speaks when it is appropriate. Knowing how to hold your tongue is also wisdom.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-full-or-to-be-gone","title":"To Be Full, or to Be Gone","paragraphs":["By sharing, we become full.","If we are divided, we cease to exist.","Yunus Emre","Moral: Sharing strengthens us; when we are divided we weaken and cease to exist. To protect our unity we must increase our knowledge and hold our spirituality high.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"searching","title":"Searching","paragraphs":["To search for something where it is not is not searching; it is deceiving yourself—like looking for loyalty in a disloyal person, decency in a shameless person, or looking for mercy in someone without a conscience.","Moral: Expecting from a person what is not in them is deceiving yourself. Do not expect everything from everyone; know each person as they are.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"two-bowls-of-water","title":"Two Cups of Water","paragraphs":["In ancient times there was a ruler famous for his wealth. Wherever he went, he took part of his wealth with him and boasted by showing it off. He had a wise teacher whom he trusted most of all. One day he asked him, “You are a man who has seen the world. Tell me, what do you think of my rule and my wealth?”","Looking into the ruler’s eyes, the wise man said, “Suppose, my lord, you are in a very hot, boundless desert. Would you give half your wealth to someone who gave you a cup of water, so that you would not die?”","“Of course.”","“And if, after a while, your thirst grew still worse, would you give the other half for another cup?”","“To stay alive, yes.”","“Then do not boast too much,” the wise man said with a smile. “All your wealth is worth just two cups of water.”","Moral: The value of riches depends on the circumstances. In the desert, all your wealth is worth only two vessels of water. Do not boast of it — its value does not last.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-do-i-need-it-for","title":"What Is It to Me?","paragraphs":["Suleiman the Lawgiver (Qanuni) (1494–1566), who ruled from 1520 until his death, turned the Ottoman state into one of the most powerful states of its time. Yet he sometimes wondered whether the state of the sons of Osman, too, might one day weaken and fall.","As with many other matters, he decided to confide this anxious thought as well and wrote to his milk brother, the scholar Yahya Efendi, in whose unveiling and miraculous gift he believed. “You are privy to divine secrets. Please make them clear to us. In what condition does a state collapse? What will be the fate of the sons of Osman? Could a day come when this state goes bankrupt?”","The reply was brief: “What is it to me, my Sultan?”","Reading the reply at Topkapı Palace in astonishment, the sultan could not make sense of it, and his unease grew. “I wonder whether this answer holds a meaning we do not know,” he thought. A man like Yahya Efendi would not give so simple an answer to a serious matter. He rose and set off for Yahya Efendi’s dervish lodge in Beşiktaş. This time he repeated his question in a reproachful manner: “Elder brother, please answer my letter. Take the matter seriously.”","Yahya Efendi:","“My Sultan, how could I not take your question seriously? I considered it carefully and gave you my answer.”","“Well, I understood nothing from your answer. All you said was, ‘What is it to me.’ You seem to want nothing to do with the matter.”","Yahya Efendi set these words of counsel down on the pages of history:","“When oppression spreads and injustice rises to the heavens, those who hear it say, ‘What is it to me?’ and turn away. Then the sheep are eaten not by wolves but by the shepherds. Those who know the truth conceal it. The cries of the poor rise to heaven, and no one hears them but the stones. Then the end is near. The treasury empties, the people’s trust and respect are shaken. The obedience that upholds public order departs, and respect for the people disappears. Thus collapse and bankruptcy become inevitable.”","The old sultan began to weep, bowed his head, and affirmed what he had heard. He left, thanking God that his country had a scholar who could give him such advice.","Moral: A society weakens when people see oppression and say, “What is it to me?” To look away from injustice is to take part in it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-it-means-to-be-late","title":"What Does It Mean to Be Late?","paragraphs":["Being late means not doing something on time. In our dealings with people, being late can cost even more.","Expressing love, accepting affection, forgiving someone, or offering an apology all matter while there is still time.","We all make mistakes. What matters is that we not be late to admit and correct our mistake, to apologize, and to make peace. Life is short.","Let us not be late, so that we may live a just life full of happiness, with no “if only”s and many “how good that”s.","Moral: Expressing love, forgiving, and apologizing on time is beautiful. Life is short - let us not postpone the affairs of the heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-matters-in-life","title":"What Matters in Life?","paragraphs":["A teacher entered the classroom, greeted the students, took a glass jar from his bag, and placed it on the desk:","“We have only one life,” he said. “If we use our time well, we can achieve everything we want in this life.” He filled the jar with golf balls. “Is it full?”","“Yes,” the students answered.","He took some large pebbles from the bag and added them to the jar.","“What about now?”","“Yes.”","Then he poured some more sand into the jar.","“And now?”","The students confirmed that the jar was full this time as well.","He explained:","“Think of this jar as your life.","“The golf balls are what you value most: your family, friends, and loved ones.","“The pebbles are other important things, such as your work and possessions.","“The sand symbolizes other things you may need that are not very important.","“If I put the sand in first, there would be no room for the balls or pebbles. Fill your life with small matters, and you may leave no time or energy for what matters most.”","Moral: Fill the jar of life with the most valuable things first—family, love, and health. If you get caught up in the little things, you will have no time for the most precious things.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-we-learn-in-life","title":"What Life Teaches Us","paragraphs":["Throughout life, we keep learning from others:","some teach us patience, love, loyalty, and integrity; others show us how to recognize two-facedness, fraud, malice, and betrayal.","Moral: Life teaches us something from every person: from one, fidelity; from another, what betrayal is. Both are a lesson.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"where-does-this-road-go","title":"Where Does This Road Go?","paragraphs":["A young traveler asked an old man sitting beside the road:","“Uncle, where does this road go?”","“Nowhere, my son.”","“How can a road go nowhere?”","“By God, I have been here for fifty years, and it hasn’t moved. It’s the people who come and go.”","Moral: The road stays where it is, and you are the traveler. Do not ask where the road will take you without knowing where you are going.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","blurb":"Labor, provision, and our view of wealth.","count":19,"stories":[{"stem":"organization-without-an-action-plan","title":"An Organization Without an Action Plan","paragraphs":["An organization without an action plan is like a ship that knows its destination but has no chart, no chosen route, and no assigned duties for its crew. It may reach port through luck, a skillful captain, or favorable weather. But reaching port would be the result of chance, not of systematic management. Lasting success needs more than fortunate circumstances: it requires careful planning and consistent action.","Over time, an organization without a plan also begins to lose its members. People are sustained not only by ideas, but by the hope of seeing those ideas put into practice. When hope fades, people leave; when people leave, the organization gradually weakens and eventually ceases to function.","Moral: A goal provides direction; a plan defines the path toward it. An organization may advance by chance without a plan, but lasting success requires deliberate and consistent action.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"fish-shop","title":"At the Fish Shop","paragraphs":["After work, a man was walking toward the bus stop when he passed a fish shop. Remembering how much his wife and daughter loved fish, he went inside to gladden them.","He asked the seller for one kilogram, then changed the order to two. At that moment, an elderly woman in a worn coat approached timidly:","“Son, would you buy some fish for me too?”","Without hesitation, the man told the seller:","“Make it four kilograms—two for me and two for her.”","He then asked whether she had bread. When she said no, he hurried to the shop across the street, bought four loaves, and gave her a share.","“Is there anything else you need?”","The woman took an empty medicine bottle from her pocket. It was eye drops she had last been able to buy two months earlier; without them, her sight was worsening.","The man went to the pharmacy. The fish seller, who had watched everything, offered the woman a chair. Returning with the medicine, the man was heartened by the seller’s compassion as well.","He gave the woman the medicine and then all the money left in his pocket. Blessing him, she made her way home.","As the man headed toward the bus stop, he remembered that he had not paid for the fish. His pockets were empty. The seller simply smiled:","“Go on, brother. The fish is my gift.”","With no bus fare, the man walked home. He called his daughter, told her he had bought fish, and promised to arrive soon. As he walked through the rain, he gave thanks that he had been able to help someone today; the tears on his cheeks mingled with the rainwater.","Moral: Do not wait for great means before doing good; kindness depends not on wealth, but on a generous heart. A person rich in spirit finds a way to help even while possessing little.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"baklava","title":"Baklava","paragraphs":["When I was a child, our family had very little money. One day, my father brought home some baklava, and we enjoyed it with tea that evening.","The next day was a holiday. My mother packed one piece of baklava, a few olives, and a piece of bread for me to take to school.","My classmates had many different foods. A child I disliked saw my modest meal and laughed:","“Did you bring that baklava for the mice?”","When the whole class laughed, I was ashamed, ran home in tears, and shouted at my mother:","“Why did you give me only one piece? Everyone else had so much food—you humiliated me!”","My mother answered sadly:","“My child, that was my own piece.”","Her words broke my heart. She had saved her only piece of baklava for me instead of eating it herself. I was too ashamed even to hug her. Ever since, I have asked for forgiveness whenever I remember how I behaved. I have never been able to eat baklava again.","Moral: Mocking another person’s poverty is among the greatest sins. Never forget that a mother may surrender her own share—even her final bite—for her child.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"chief-accountant-and-the-ceo","title":"The Chief Accountant and the CEO","paragraphs":["The chief accountant asked the CEO:","“What if we spend time and money on our employees’ development, and then they leave?”","The CEO replied:","“What if we spend neither time nor money on their development, and they stay?”","Moral: Investing in people is not a waste. Saving on this today can lead to a much greater loss tomorrow.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-you-have-spending-money","title":"Do You Have Spending Money?","paragraphs":["Value your parents while they are alive. Instead of merely asking, “Do you have spending money?” do what you can to meet their needs. No one can take their place.","Moral: Value your parents while they are alive, and meet their needs with deeds, not words. No one and nothing can replace them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"everyone-has-work-to-do","title":"Everyone Has Work to Do","paragraphs":["I have been fortunate to learn from wise teachers. One was my literature teacher, the esteemed Baladadash. He taught us: if someone asks you for something or assigns you a task and you cannot do it, never say, even if it is true, “I had so much work that I had no time and I could not get it done.” Those words make the other person think, “Don’t I have affairs of my own? Am I bothering you for nothing?”","Everyone has things to do. When someone asks for help, it is usually because they need you, not because they have nothing better to do. Do not ignore the request or leave them waiting in silence. If you cannot do it, say so at once and do not leave them in uncertainty, because they hope in you and trust you. Coming back much later to say “I had no time” destroys that trust.","Moral: It is a very painful feeling to live knowing you are regarded as unreliable and untrustworthy.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"former-minister-at-the-seminar","title":"The Former Minister at the Seminar","paragraphs":["A former minister was invited to speak at a seminar. He stepped up to the lectern holding a plastic cup, but seemed distracted. Raising the cup, he let out a deep sigh and addressed the audience.","“Do you know what I am thinking? Last year I spoke from this same lectern,” he said. “The difference is that I was a minister then. A business-class ticket had been bought for me. A limousine and escort cars met me at the airport and took me to a luxury hotel suite. The next morning, the same limousine brought me here. There was a special welcome, an expensive porcelain cup of coffee, and a reserved seat. But today I am not speaking before you as a minister. Yesterday, I paid for my own flight.","“No one met me at the airport. I took a taxi to the hotel and found my own room. This morning, they checked my document against the list and let me in. I found a seat myself. I felt like coffee, and they pointed me to the coffee machine in the hallway. That’s where I filled this plastic cup.”","The audience laughed. “Last year’s porcelain cup wasn’t for me,” he continued. “It was for my position.” The room broke into applause. When it quieted, he added:","“That taught me something. The praise, privileges, and special treatment that come with office belong only to your rank, your role, or your position, not to you. Once you leave, the porcelain cup becomes a plastic one. That is what you deserve.”","Moral: The respect shown to you is often for your position, not for yourself. Therefore, do not brag about your position — your true value will be seen when you leave.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"garden-and-the-gardener","title":"The Garden and the Gardener","paragraphs":["A teacher said to his students:","“Picture a gardener tending a garden. Can you see it?”","“Yes,” they replied.","The teacher says in a serious voice:","“Remember this: it would be wrong to say the garden grew thanks to the gardener. But it would be just as wrong to say it grew entirely by itself.”","Moral: Recognize both your own efforts and the help you receive. No one grows entirely alone or entirely through the work of others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"grocery-shop-and-the-supermarket","title":"The Grocery Shop and the Supermarket","paragraphs":["An elderly woman ran a grocery shop in our neighborhood. When a supermarket opened opposite, both shops began advertising prices outside. The supermarket put up a sign: “Butter: 70 manats.” The woman’s sign read “Butter: 60 manats.” The next day, the supermarket lowered its price to 50. She promptly advertised 40.","One of the residents of the neighborhood, concerned about this situation, turned to the woman:","“Auntie, you shouldn’t compete with them. They can afford these price cuts. You’ll go out of business.”","The woman replied without losing her composure:","“I don’t sell butter. I just want people to be able to buy it cheaply.”","Moral: A person who lives by the question “Whom did I help?” rather than “What did I gain?” is different. The one who gives without expecting anything in return is actually the one who gains the most.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"henry-fords-choice","title":"Henry Ford's Choice","paragraphs":["It is said that Henry Ford, founder of the Ford Motor automobile company, once invited two young friends to dinner. Both hoped to work for his company, and both were talented graduates of the same university.","As they were leaving the restaurant, Ford told one young man he was hired and told the other that he wished him well but could not give him a job. The disappointed young man gathered his courage and asked:","“Mr. Ford, we haven’t discussed cars or engineering all evening. We only talked about everyday things. Why did you offer my friend a job and turn me down?”","Ford replied:","“For two reasons. First, I watched you both during dinner. Your friend tasted his food before adding salt. You added salt before tasting it. I value people who examine something before deciding to change it.","“Second, your friend treated the waiters courteously. You ignored them and were polite only to me. Everyone deserves respect, whatever their position.”","Moral: Small actions reveal character, both in deciding before we have weighed the matter and in how we treat people weaker than ourselves.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"his-hand-is-at-work","title":"His Hand Is at Work","paragraphs":["A man bought ten eggs for ten qepiks and sold them for nine. When acquaintances asked the purpose of this strange trade, he replied:","“Never mind the loss. As long as people see me trading, they think I’m a merchant. At least everyone can see that I’m working.”","Moral: Doing meaningless work purely for the sake of appearances brings harm. Looking busy does not bring real benefit.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hot-bread","title":"Hot Bread","paragraphs":["The smell of hot, fresh bread spread throughout the street.","The old man approached the baker. He needed to buy hot bread for his grandson. The child had been asking for fresh bread for days.","People were standing in line to buy bread. He took his place in line.","There weren't many people, and his turn came quickly.","He took the change from his pocket. If he had had enough, he would have bought two loaves. But when he held out the coins, the baker did not take the money and handed him a bag.","“Take these, uncle, and enjoy them.”","There were three loaves of bread in the package.","“Son, I was going to buy one loaf.”","“Take all three. You can save some for later.”","The grandfather slowly approached the baker and whispered in his ear:","“I only have enough money for one.”","The baker smiled:","“Uncle, this is a share from us. It’s Friday, and every Friday we give away three hot loaves. Enjoy them.”","“May Allah be pleased with you, my son.”","“Amen.”","He had just left the bakery when someone called out to the grandfather:","“Uncle! Uncle!”","The grandfather turned and looked:","“Yes, my son?”","“This is my mother’s zakat. I would be very glad if you accepted it.”","The young man put an envelope in the old man's pocket.","“May Allah accept it, my son. May Allah be pleased with your mother.”","As he passed the market, a woman called to him:","“Uncle, please wait a moment.”","The old man stopped and looked in the direction from which the voice came. A young woman handed him two full bags:","“Uncle, during Ramadan we are giving food to people passing by. Please accept these bags. We’d be so glad if you took them.”","“May Allah accept your kindness, my daughter.”","“Amen.”","The old man reached home with difficulty, exhausted.","“Welcome home, Father.”","“Where’s your mother?”","“In the kitchen. She said she would cook eggs; Orkhan loves kaygana.”","“Take these bags to her, please. My back is aching.”","“Grandpa, did you get hot bread?” his grandson asked.","“Yes, I have bought many things.”","“Hooray for Grandpa!”","“But Dad, this morning you didn’t even have money for tea.”","“Allah provided, my daughter.”","His wife entered the room:","“You’ve bought so much, husband!”","“These are a share,” he said.","The man told what had happened one by one.","When the call to prayer sounded, they broke their fast with kaygana and hot bread. The hot bread was just right. The bags also held butter and cheese, which they enjoyed with tea.","“Yes, it turned out wonderfully. Thank Allah. May He accept their kindness. The halva is delicious too.”","After eating, his grandson stood up.","“I’m going to my room to pray again,” he said, and his grandfather was surprised.","“Isn’t it early? You can pray before bed,” his grandfather said.","“I prayed at this time yesterday for hot bread and cheese, and look what came! Now I’ll ask Allah to send money for your teeth. Your teeth are bad, and you can hardly chew your food.”","His mother smiled:","“I wish your late father could see these days.”","“He comes into my dreams every night and sees all this. He told me to pray.”","The old man suddenly remembered his jacket. He went to the hanger, put his hand in his pocket and took out the envelope. There was a large amount of money in the envelope.","His eyes filled with tears.","“What is it? Why are you crying?”","“The Lord has sent everything our grandson wished for. Look—there’s enough here for both of us to have our teeth fixed before the holiday. Praise be to Allah!”","Moral: A child’s sincere prayer and a few generous hearts can remedy a family’s trouble. In a society that knows how to give, no one goes hungry.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"credit-ledger","title":"The Credit Ledger","paragraphs":["A plainly dressed stranger entered a neighborhood shop. After looking at the shopkeeper and the counter for a moment, he asked:","“Brother, would you let me have some bread, tea, or eggs on credit?”","“There’s a man who has been taking bread on credit for three months,” the shopkeeper replied. “To be honest, I don't believe he will pay this debt either. But he’s caring for three orphans. I can’t let those children go hungry. Allah is great; He will provide for us too. Take it and go; may it be lawful and pleasant for you. You don’t have to pay.” A smile spread across the stranger’s face. “It seems I’ve found what I was looking for,” he thought.","“How much debt is written in that credit ledger of yours?” he asked.","Surprised and a little irritated, the shopkeeper asked:","“Why do you want to know?”","To reassure him, the stranger went on in a calm voice:","“Please don’t be upset, brother. I am not so idle a man as to come here, fray your nerves, and waste your time. I want to buy that ledger. Would you add up all the debts and tell me the total?”","The shopkeeper looked from the stranger to the well-filled ledger. Seeing that the man was serious, he added up the debts and named the sum. The stranger took a bundle of banknotes from his jacket pocket and laid the money on the counter.","“May Allah be pleased with you, brother. You trust your neighbors and, for His sake, refuse to let orphans go hungry. You are a good man. I’ve paid everyone’s debts. Let this be my Ramadan gift to the neighborhood. Give me the ledger now. I’ll take it away and bury it, trusting that this record will speak for me on the Day of Judgment.” He took the book and left.","The shopkeeper counted the money and put it in the till, still stunned by what had happened. He looked out of the little window, but the stranger was gone. It was nearly time for iftar, the meal that breaks the daily fast, and everyone was hurrying home. Just then, a seven-year-old girl appeared in the doorway. She was the daughter of Suraiya, whose husband had died of tuberculosis.","“Uncle Ahmed,” she said shyly, “Mama asks for a loaf of bread and three eggs for iftar. We’ll pay you next month, when my uncle comes.” Her words brought the shopkeeper out of his daze. Out of habit, he reached beneath the counter for the ledger. Then he paused and smiled.","“Come, my child. Take the bread, the eggs, and some butter too. Tell your mother that uncle came and left.”","Moral: Kindness done quietly is the highest kindness. Relieving someone’s debt and keeping an orphan from hunger are the true wealth of the heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mercedes","title":"Mercedes","paragraphs":["Mercedes was neither the founder’s surname nor the name of an already famous automobile company. The car took its name from the daughter of a man who loved automobiles.","Her father believed her name would bring him good fortune.","And so it happened...","The car he named Mercedes went on to win race after race.","Thanks to these successes, countless Mercedes cars have been produced since 1902.","A father’s love for his daughter turned the name Mercedes into the brand of one of the world’s largest automobile manufacturers.","Mrs. Mercedes, who had two unsuccessful marriages, lived in Vienna. In 1929, at the age of thirty-nine, she died of cancer and was buried in Vienna.","Moral: A name given with love, a work done with love can change the world. A father's love immortalized a name.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pay-rent-for-the-water","title":"Rent for the Water","paragraphs":["A man bought a well from a villager. But when he tried to draw water, the seller stopped him. “I sold you the well, not the water.” Unable to settle the dispute, they went before a judge.","“If the water is yours,” the judge told the seller, “you cannot store it in another man’s well. Take it away or pay him rent.”","Moral: Dishonesty can trap the person who practices it. Fair judgment considers both sides of a claim.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"scarf-seller","title":"The Scarf Seller","paragraphs":["The night’s frost had covered the city, and it seemed my breath would freeze too. I picked my way over the icy pavement toward the bus. I thought, “Thank God, I got here safe and sound.” A boy approached, offering scarves for sale. All I wanted was to get on the bus.","“Would you buy one, sister?”","“No, thank you.”","“Please take one. You’ll need it.”","“I haven’t any money,” I said, hoping he would leave me alone.","“Then take it as a gift.”","“No, I already have a scarf.” I hurried onto the bus, thinking only of myself.","Before sitting down, I put my hand into my coat pocket to take out my phone. My hand touched a package. I took it out and looked. I was very surprised to see a package with a scarf inside. So the child had slipped the package into my pocket without my noticing.","I hurried back outside. The boy was still offering scarves to passengers. He was twelve or thirteen, in a thin coat with no gloves. His nose was red, but his black eyes shone like stars in the night, and when he smiled, his white teeth showed. I touched his shoulder and held up the package. “What’s this?”","“A gift, sister. You might need it.”","“But how will you earn anything? Aren’t you to be pitied in this cold?”","“It’s all right,” he said, his eyes full of love.","I held out the money.","“No, please. It’s my gift.”","If he had asked where the money had come from when I claimed to have none, I would have wanted to disappear with shame. But he was straightforward, innocent, and pure, and he thought everyone else was as pure as he was.","“Please take it,” I insisted. He accepted, then began searching his pocket for change.","“Keep the change.”","“It’s too much!” Flustered, he tried to separate the money, and some of it fell to the ground.","“It’s nothing; may you be well, and let it be my gift,” I said, smiling.","Ashamed, with the smile that melts the ice, he said: “Thank you, sister. May Allah be pleased with you.”","“And thank you,” I thought as I returned to my seat. “You be well too, generous-hearted, easily contented child of my poor country.” Outside, an icy cold still gripped this child's small, lovely city, where children like him remain pure in their innocence, keep their trust in people, turn winter into spring with their laughter, and work to help their families.","The bus pulled away, leaving both the city and the scarf-selling boy behind in the dark. My heart ached. I thought of everyone out in that cold with nowhere to go. Mixed feelings filled me. My throat tightened, and I struggled to hold back tears.","Our bus continued on its way, winding through the snow-covered mountains.","Moral: Sometimes the most destitute person shows the greatest generosity. The pure heart of a child turns material poverty into wealth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"weight-of-the-oil","title":"The Weight of Butter","paragraphs":["A baker regularly bought a certain amount of butter from a peasant. One day he noticed that the weight of the butter he received was decreasing day by day. He accused the peasant of cheating him and complained to the judge.","“I have no scales,” the farmer explained. “I hold the butter in one hand and the one-kilogram loaf I buy from him every day in the other. I give him the same weight of butter as he gives me in bread.”","Moral: The measure you apply to another will one day be applied to you. As you give bread, so you receive butter.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"we-are-rich","title":"We Are Rich","paragraphs":["Near the end of the fourth century, Emperor Nintoku of Japan climbed a high tower one day and surveyed his country. Seeing not even a wisp of smoke rising, he realized his people were in poverty and that no one could cook at home.","He immediately issued a decree ordering the people to work only for themselves for three years. He even sent the palace workers home. At the end of three years, the people who worked for themselves and paid no taxes reached abundance.","Three years later Nintoku climbed the tower again, saw smoke rising on every side, and was sure that hearths were burning across the land. Joyfully he said to the lady beside him:","“Now we are rich!”","But the empress pointed to the palace, neglected for three years — roof leaking, flowers withered — and asked:","“You call this wealth?”","Nintoku’s answer has been remembered by the Japanese for centuries:","“When our people are poor, we are poor, and their wealth is our wealth.”","Moral: True wealth is not in the treasury, but in the people’s well-being. What makes a leader great is not the post, but the work done for the people in that post.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-am-i-poor","title":"Why Am I Poor?","paragraphs":["Once a poor man met Buddha and asked him:","“Buddha, why am I poor?”","“Because you share none of your wealth.”","“But how can I do that? I have nothing to give others!”","“You have five kinds of wealth you could share, but you do not use them.”","“What are they?”","“All right.”","“With your face you could give others a smile, but you do not.","“With your eyes you could look at others with compassion and love, but you do not.","“With your tongue you could say kind words to others, but you do not.","“With your heart you could wish others happiness, but you do not.","“And with your hands you could do good things for others, but you do not.”","Moral: There is always something to give: a smile, a kind word, a compassionate look, a helping hand. A person is as rich as they share.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","blurb":"Right, community, and shared responsibility.","count":10,"stories":[{"stem":"bell-to-hang-on-the-cats-neck","title":"Belling the Cat","paragraphs":["A group of mice gathered to discuss how to get rid of a cat that had been terrorizing them.","They put forward many ideas, but could agree on none. At last, a young mouse suggested fastening a bell around the cat’s neck.","Whenever the cat approached, they would hear the bell and have time to escape. The mice greeted the plan with enthusiastic applause.","Then an old mouse, who had been listening quietly in a corner, spoke up:","“That is a clever idea, and I’m sure it would work. But there is one thing I’d like to know: who is going to put the bell on the cat?”","Moral: Making a plan is easier than carrying it out. Even the cleverest idea is of little use unless someone can put it into practice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"humanity-rebuilds-in-peace","title":"Humanity","paragraphs":["How full the world is of wonders and delicate beauty... In every flower, in every whisper of the wind, in the color of the sky, there is a hidden wisdom. This beauty is like a silent trust placed in the human soul. Sadly, however, people sometimes disrupt this harmony with their own hands, burning not only the land in the flames, but also hopes, destinies, and memories.","War damages not only the land but also the light within us. Every life lost is, in truth, a part of the world falling silent. Yet our purpose in existence is not to destroy, but to protect, create, and sustain life.","Perhaps the greatest question is this: When will humanity find its strength in sustaining life rather than destroying it? When will the earth be filled with the sound of peace rather than silence? Perhaps then the world will be not only beautiful, but also a place that is cherished.","Moral: Humanity is elevated by protecting life and serving peace and goodness. The more we devote our strength to sustaining life, the more beautiful the world becomes for us all.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"woman-whose-house-was-robbed","title":"The Woman Whose House Was Robbed","paragraphs":["Long ago, an elderly woman in a certain city had her home robbed. Distraught, she went to the king to seek justice.","After hearing her story, the king asked angrily:","“How could you sleep so soundly that you didn’t even notice a thief entering your house?”","The woman replied:","“We thought you were awake, Your Majesty. That is why we slept peacefully in our house.”","Moral: When the people trust the state, they live at ease. When people know their ruler is keeping watch, they can sleep peacefully in their own homes.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"homeland-and-land","title":"Land and Homeland","paragraphs":["Mix earth with water and it becomes mud. Mix it with blood and it becomes a homeland.","Moral: What turns land into a homeland is the blood shed for it. It is more than a place; it is the sacred land for which we sacrifice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sharing-justice","title":"Sharing Justice","paragraphs":["Let no one weep,","or let those who are always laughing not laugh.","Either let no one have this affliction called money,","or let the wealthy learn to share.","Either let no one speak of their love,","or let them give the definition of true love.","Either let these holidays not be celebrated at all,","or let no one be left without meat on the holiday.","Moral: Humanity is created when joy, bread, and love are shared. If one side laughs and the other side cries, there is no justice there.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"just-verdict-of-the-frankfurt-judge","title":"The Just Verdict of the Frankfurt Judge","paragraphs":["This event was recorded in the city of Frankfurt in 1506. A merchant lost a purse containing 800 guilders on the road. A carpenter passing that road by chance found it. Deeply religious, he told no one he had found it and kept it safe, certain that whoever had lost such a sum would come looking for it.","One day, after a church service, a monk announced that a visiting merchant had lost 800 guilders and offered a reward of 100 for their return.","The carpenter brought the purse to the monk.","When the merchant collected it, however, he offered only five guilders. The carpenter asked him to honor his promise. To avoid paying, the merchant claimed the purse had contained 900 guilders and accused the carpenter of taking part of the money. The monk stepped in and defended the carpenter, saying he knew him and that he was an honest man, but when the argument grew heated he took them both to the city court.","The judge asked the merchant to swear on the Gospel that he had lost 900 guilders. He did so without hesitation. The carpenter also laid his hand on the Gospel and swore that he had found 800 guilders.","Everyone waited impatiently. The judge said that everything was as clear as day and announced his decision:","“The carpenter found 800 guilders. The merchant lost 900. Clearly, this cannot be the merchant’s purse. Since its owner has not been found, the carpenter may keep the 800 guilders. The merchant is free to continue searching for his 900.”","Moral: Greed traps a person. The stingy person who denies the truth eventually becomes a victim of his own lies; justice is on the side of the righteous.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"skins-of-the-lambs","title":"The Skins of the Lambs","paragraphs":["“It is better to shear the wool of rams than to skin lambs.”","This saying is associated with Azmzadeh Asad Pasha, who was the governor of Damascus province during the Ottoman period. The story goes like this:","One day Asad Pasha’s treasury was empty and he was in great difficulty. His advisers, as a way to fill the treasury, proposed an extra tax on the weavers of Damascus. “How much would it raise?” he asked.","“Fifty or sixty bags of gold.”","Asad Pasha objected: “These people are already struggling to make ends meet. How will they pay this tax?” The advisors said: “They will sell the gold and jewels in their houses, Pasha,” and tried to persuade him.","“I can raise it by a better way,” the pasha replied.","His advisers fell silent.","The next evening, he summoned the mufti to a private meeting.","“Mufti Effendi, According to information we have obtained, in your private life you act contrary to the sharia and drink alcohol secretly at home. I must report this to Istanbul, but thought I should tell you first.”","The mufti pleaded with him and offered a thousand mecidiyes to keep the matter quiet. The pasha refused. The mufti offered twice as much. The pasha refused again. In the end they settled on six thousand.","The next day he called in the judge. “Reliable sources tell me you accept bribes and use your office for personal gain.”","The judge began to beg and, like the mufti, bargained with Asad Pasha: “Sir, do not remove me from my post, or I shall be disgraced among the people.” He too agreed to pay six thousand mecidiyes.","Then Asad Pasha invited the treasurer, the chief of security, the merchants and the wealthy to a meeting one by one.","By the end, he had collected two hundred bags of gold. He called his advisers together. “Have you heard of any new tax on the people of Damascus?” They had not.","“Although I imposed no tax, I have collected not fifty but two hundred bags of mecidiye gold.”","“How did you do it?”","The Pasha replies: “It is better to shear the wool of rams than to skin lambs.”","Moral: It is more just to take the unjust profit of the wealthy than to rob the poor. The burden should be placed on the strong, not the poor.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"bedouin-whose-camel-was-stolen","title":"The Bedouin and the Stolen Camel","paragraphs":["A Bedouin riding through the desert came across a man who could barely stand, his lips parched with thirst. The man begged for water. The Bedouin dismounted and gave him a drink. As soon as he had finished, the stranger shoved the Bedouin aside, climbed onto the camel, and rode away.","Realizing that he could not recover his camel, the Bedouin shouted after the thief:","“Take the camel! It’s yours. But never tell anyone what happened here.”","Puzzled by this request, the thief stopped, looked back, and asked why.","“If you tell anyone, the story will spread,” the Bedouin replied. “Then no one will ever again help the needy people they meet.”","Moral: Sometimes a person hides what happened to him to protect others’ faith in human kindness. One piece of bad news can close the doors of thousands of good deeds.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"with-this-nation-the-world-can-be-conquered","title":"With This Nation the World Can Be Conquered","paragraphs":["A king went into the city in disguise to see how his people lived. He walked through the markets, learning the quality of the goods, the prices, and the shopkeepers' livelihood.","The king entered the first shop at the beginning of the street. He greeted and said:","“Half a batman of butter, half a batman of honey, and some cheese, please.”","The shopkeeper greeted him warmly and weighed out the butter, not knowing he was the king.","“I can sell you the honey and cheese too,” he said, “but this is my first sale today. My neighbor has not yet had one. If you buy the rest from him, I would be very pleased.”","Pleased, the king paid and crossed the street. There he asked for half a batman of honey and cheese. The elderly shopkeeper weighed the honey, then said:","“Thank Allah, thanks to you, I’ve also made my first sale today. Would you buy your cheese next door? That shopkeeper is still waiting for his.”","The king, who was very impressed by what he saw, went to the shop next door and made his purchase, and said:","“With such high morality in this nation, one could conquer the world,” he said, leaving the market delighted.","Moral: True strength is not in weapons, but in the people's honesty and integrity. Winning hearts, not taking land, is the true victory.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-injustice-when-there-is-justice","title":"Why Injustice When There Is Justice?","paragraphs":["Haji Bektaş Veli:","Why hatred when there is love?","Why war when there is peace?","Why quarrel when there is brotherhood?","Why enmity when there is friendship?","Why intolerance when there is tolerance?","Why captivity when there is freedom?","Why injustice when there is justice?","Moral: When the path of love, peace, brotherhood, and justice is open, turning to hatred, strife, and injustice is a person's own choice, and the responsibility is theirs.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Aging and time","slug":"yaslanma-ve-zaman","blurb":"The seasons of life and what time changes.","count":4,"stories":[{"stem":"aging","title":"Aging","paragraphs":["Aging is like climbing a mountain: the higher you rise, the more tired you become and the shorter your breath, yet the wider your view of the world grows.","Moral: Growing older brings more than a loss of strength. Experience increases, and one's view of life grows broader.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"cherish-today-with-loved-ones","title":"Time — life flowing silently","paragraphs":["Time is a strange wonder... We can neither see it, nor hold it, nor bring it back. It passes silently—in the hands of clocks, in the changing days, in the gray appearing in our hair, in children growing up, in memories receding into the distance...","Often, we understand time only after we have lost it. A day that seemed ordinary yesterday becomes our most treasured memory years later. A conversation, a glance, a smile that we once considered insignificant, or a simple evening spent with someone we love, may later become one of the most beautiful moments of our lives.","Time stops for no one. It yields neither to wealth, nor to fame, nor to human desire. It is the greatest treasure, given to everyone in equal measure, yet valued differently by each of us. A person's life is measured, in truth, not in years but in the meaningful moments they have lived.","Sometimes we think that life still lies ahead and that we have plenty of time. We will do it tomorrow, go next week, talk someday, meet later... But the time we call ‘later’ does not always come.","Time takes much from us: our youth, certain people, certain dreams, certain opportunities... Yet in return it gives us memories, experience, and the chance to understand the true meaning of life.","So let us not measure time simply by the years that pass. Let us value our time not by how long we live, but by how we live. Let us tell our loved ones today that we love them. Let us not put off meeting our friends. Let us look at beautiful landscapes, watch the sunrise, listen to the sea, and live the moments that bring joy to our hearts.","For time passes...","Moral: Let us not put off expressing our love or making time for our loved ones. A moment that has passed does not return; we must appreciate this day today.","Etibar Siracsoy"],"hasAudio":false,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"for-those-who-have-reached-sixty","title":"To Those Who Have Reached Sixty","paragraphs":["It is time to use the money you have saved up so far. Use it and enjoy it. Don’t deprive yourself of everything just to keep it for those who know nothing of the labor you spent to earn it. This is the ideal time to enjoy peace.","Do not worry about the financial situation of your children and grandchildren, and do not be upset that you have spent your money on yourself. You have taken care of them for years, taught them everything you could. You have given them education, food, shelter and support. Now it is their duty to earn their own money. Let them find their own way.","Take care of your health without overexerting yourself. Exercise moderately, walk each day, eat well, and get enough sleep.","At this age, it is easy to get sick and difficult to stay healthy. Therefore, you should keep yourself in good shape, pay attention to your medical and physical needs. Contact your doctor; get check-ups even if you feel fine.","Do not let small troubles consume you. You have already overcome many difficulties and carry both happy and painful memories. Do not let the past hold you back or the future frighten you. Enjoy the present; many everyday worries will soon be forgotten.","Whatever your age, keep loving your partner, your family, and life itself. As long as your mind is sound and your compassion is intact, you are not old.","Keep caring for your appearance and well-being. Go to the hairdresser, care for your nails, see a dermatologist and a dentist, and use perfume and creams. A neat appearance will also lift your mood, and you will feel more confident and more vigorous.","Don't lose sight of age-appropriate fashion trends, but maintain your own style. Read books, flip through newspapers, watch the news. Go online, find out what people think. Make sure you have an active email account and use at least a few social networks.","Respect younger people and their opinions. They may not share your opinion, but the future is theirs, and they will steer their lives as they wish. Do not criticize; give advice, and try to remind them that yesterday's wisdom is still valid today.","Never use the phrase \"in our time...\". Now is your time. Be a part of this time as long as you live.","Try to stay away from negative people and spend your time only with those who give you a pleasant mood and positive energy.","Do not give up your hobbies. You can travel, walk, cook, read, dance. If you want, you can keep a cat or a dog.","Get out of the house, meet people you have not seen for a long time, and see new places and renew old memories. The main thing is not to shut yourself in at home. Go to museums, walk in the park.","Try to always be polite, do not complain and criticize unless necessary. If someone has broken your heart, forgive them. Apologize to those whose hearts you have broken.","Don't carry your pain with you. It will only hurt you. It doesn't matter who is right. They say that holding a grudge is like poisoning yourself and waiting for someone else to die. Never accept that poison. Forgive, forget and move on with your life.","Laugh, rejoice. At this stage of your life, savor a peaceful life... Be happy!","Moral: The season of sacrifice ends one day; in the autumn of life, give yourself the right to live as well. A happy person also spreads happiness around them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-changed-after-sixty","title":"What Changed After Sixty","paragraphs":["I asked a friend nearing seventy what changes she had noticed in herself as she grew older. She replied:","“I no longer tell older people, ‘You’ve already told me that story.’ Telling it again lets them revisit a happy memory.","“I no longer feel I must correct every mistake. It is not my job to make everyone perfect. Peace of heart matters more than perfection.","“I give more compliments. They brighten my day as well as the other person’s.","“I worry less about a crease or a stain on my clothes. A person's worth comes not from outward appearance, but from within.","“I have learned to stay away from people who are alien to my world. They cannot build or keep a good relationship; I, for my part, have learned to do that too.","“I am no longer ashamed of my feelings. It is my own feelings that make me human.","“I would rather set pride aside than destroy a relationship. Undue pride leaves a person alone, while good relationships protect against loneliness.","“I make time for things that bring me joy. I myself am responsible for my own happiness.","“I have learned to ask before helping. Someone who needs help may not want that help to come from me.","“I try not to spend my days complaining about health, neighbors, relatives, or retirement. Despite all the difficulties, I should be grateful that I am alive and that my loved ones are beside me.","“I have stopped saying, ‘When I was your age,’ or ‘I gave you my best years.’ Each age has its own conditions, and today cannot be measured by the standard of the past.","“I pay more attention to my spouse. I remember that he was once young, strong, and full of joy, even if he is now a wrinkled, helpless, and sorrowful old man. Perhaps he is the only person who truly needs me now.","“I no longer blame myself for everything. I have done what I could for myself and my children.","“And finally I learned to ask the Lord for strength for what I can change, patience for what I cannot change, and wisdom to tell the two apart. As a human being, I am not capable of everything.”","Moral: Age teaches a person the greatest wisdom: peace is better than perfection, and understanding is better than being right.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","blurb":"Lessons told through animals and images.","count":8,"stories":[{"stem":"fly-in-the-china-shop","title":"A Fly in a China Shop","paragraphs":["A fly could never wreck a china shop on its own. But if it flew into a bull’s ear, the panicked animal could destroy the whole place.","The fly represents superstition, falsehood, and prejudice. The bull represents an ignorant person who acts without thinking.","Moral: A small lie whispered into an ignorant ear can cause enormous destruction. Knowledge, critical thinking, and strong moral values are our best protection against it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"elephant-and-the-rope","title":"The Elephant and the Rope","paragraphs":["A traveler passing an elephant enclosure was surprised to see the enormous animals tethered by one front leg to a stake with only a short rope. There were no chains or cages. They could surely snap the ropes, yet none tried to escape. He asked a nearby trainer why they stayed so obediently.","The trainer explains:","“When they’re young, a rope like that is strong enough to hold them. As they grow, they keep believing they can’t break it. They never realize they now have the strength to free themselves.”","The traveler was astonished. Imagine: The elephants could break free at any moment, yet stayed where they were because they believed freedom was impossible.","Moral: Often it is not the rope that binds us, but the lack of confidence born of past failures. To become free, we must also overcome the fear of what others might say.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"donkeys-law","title":"The Donkey’s Law","paragraphs":["One day, a donkey became king of the forest. Soon afterward, he decreed that everyone must go to bed at six in the evening and rise at six in the morning. For those twelve hours, the forest must be completely silent. Anyone who disobeyed would be punished.","For a time, the forest was quiet.","Then one morning, a rooster began crowing at five. The forest erupted in uproar. A court was hastily convened, and by the donkey's verdict the rooster was executed. After that, for fear of the king, no one dared break the law.","Years passed, and the donkey grew old and weak. As he lay in a quiet corner of the forest awaiting death, a lion came to visit him. After asking how he was, the lion said:","“You have little time left, and there is something we want to know. What was the wisdom behind your law about sleeping from six until six? We often thought of rebelling against it, but held back for fear of disturbing the peace. We assumed there must be some wisdom in it that we failed to understand.”","The donkey smiled and replied:","“I’ve wondered about that myself for years. Why did all the wild animals follow like sheep a law born of nothing more than a donkey’s foolishness?”","Moral: A senseless law is kept standing not by the power of the one who made it, but by the silence of those who obey. Behind an order there is not always wisdom; sometimes there is simply a donkey's foolishness. Do not be afraid to question it; nonsense left unchallenged can become an accepted rule.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hunters-description","title":"In Praise of the Hunter","paragraphs":["Until the lion learns to write, every story will praise the hunter.","African Proverb","Moral: The one who tells the story often presents himself as the hero. If you do not speak your own truth, others will make you known as they themselves see you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"lions-footprint","title":"The Lion's Track","paragraphs":["A boastful but cowardly hunter entered a forest while tracking a lion. He met a woodcutter and asked:","“Brother, have you seen a lion around here, or his track?”","The woodcutter replied:","“I’ve seen the lion itself. Come with me, and I’ll show you where it is.”","The hunter turned pale.","“No, my dear, thank you. It’s only the tracks I’m looking for, not the lion.”","Moral: A person is known not by his words, but by his action in the moment of trial. Many people \"look for a lion\" from afar, but when they come face to face, most of them retreat.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"turtles-wrong-calculation","title":"The Tortoise’s Wrong Calculation","paragraphs":["Someone asked a tortoise:","“How long will it take you to reach the next village?”","“It’s a three-day journey, allowing for rain, mud, wind, and hills. I’ll give myself six.”","Six days later, the tortoise was still missing. They searched and found it halfway there. “What’s this — you could not even finish a three-day road in six days?”","“Don’t even ask, friends. I allowed for the rain, the mud, the wind, and the hills,” the tortoise said in distress. “But I forgot to allow for unkind people. Every time I tried to go a little faster, they caught me and turned me on my back.”","Moral: You can calculate every difficulty in life, but not people with bad intentions. May Allah bring you together not with those who keep you from moving forward, but with those who support you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"two-donkeys","title":"The Two Donkeys","paragraphs":["Two donkeys saw a monkey in a tree and decided to tease him. “Let’s ask whether he has a friend. If he says yes, we’ll say what fool is friends with you. If he says no, we’ll say what fool would be friends with you?” They approached and called, “Monkey, do you have a friend?” The monkey looked down at them.","“All I can see are donkeys. Who can I be friends with?”","Moral: An attempt to make someone else look foolish can expose your own foolishness instead.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"ant-that-carries-water","title":"The Ant Carrying Water","paragraphs":["According to legend, Nimrod, the biblical ruler of Shinar, wanted to punish the prophet Abraham, whom he believed stood in the way of his tyranny. He ordered a huge fire to be built in the town square, intending to throw Abraham into it. Every creature that saw the flames fled.","Meanwhile, an ant hurried toward the fire, carrying a drop of water in its mouth.","Another ant saw it and asked:","“Where are you going in such a hurry?”","“Haven’t you heard? Nimrod is going to throw the prophet Abraham into the fire!” the first ant replied anxiously.","“You can’t have seen the size of that fire,” the other ant scoffed. “There’s nothing to do but run. Do you really think you can put it out with a single drop of water?”","The first ant replied:","“Even if I can’t put it out, at least everyone will know whose side I’m on.”","Moral: Even when you cannot change the outcome, you can stand up for what is right. Being there for someone you love in a time of trouble matters in itself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","blurb":"Lessons from history and well-known names.","count":5,"stories":[{"stem":"magnificent-lesson","title":"A Magnificent Lesson","paragraphs":["As a child, Mehmed the Conqueror, son of the Ottoman Sultan Murad II, would not sit quietly in class and was constantly disruptive. Whenever his teacher, Akşemseddin, corrected him, the boy replied:","“You cannot tell me what to do. I am the sultan’s son!”","Troubled by the boy’s behavior, the teacher hesitated to complain to the sultan. At last, he found the courage to explain what was happening.","The sultan thought for a moment and whispered a plan into the teacher’s ear. Akşemseddin protested that it would be impossible, but Murad II insisted:","“It will be done.”","The next day, young Mehmed again misbehaved and answered his teacher with the same words. At that moment, the sultan opened the door and entered the classroom.","Following the plan, Akşemseddin pretended to be furious. He reprimanded the sultan for entering without permission, even slapped him, and ordered him to leave immediately.","Murad II lowered his head, apologized, and left. Seeing that even his powerful father had to obey the teacher’s rules, the prince was stunned.","A short while later, someone knocked. This time the sultan asked permission to enter and apologized again.","The plan worked. From that day forward, Mehmed no longer disrupted his lessons. For the sake of his son’s upbringing, Murad II had set aside even a ruler’s pride.","Moral: In upbringing, personal example is stronger than any word. A responsible parent may even set aside personal pride to help a child find the right path.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"albrecht-durer","title":"Albrecht Dürer","paragraphs":["In a small village near Nuremberg lived a family with ten children. Their father worked eighteen hours a day to support the household. Despite their hardship, the two eldest sons, Albrecht and Albert, dreamed of becoming renowned artists. They knew their father could not afford to educate them both at once.","After long discussions by candlelight, the brothers decided to draw lots. The winner would study at the academy in Nuremberg, while the other worked in a nearby mine to support him. After four years, they would exchange places.","Albrecht won the draw. He left for the academy, and Albert began working in the mine.","For four years, one brother studied art while the other labored underground. Albrecht’s work soon attracted wide attention; some said that it surpassed the work of many of his teachers.","By the time he completed his studies, Albrecht was earning a good income. He returned home joyfully and told his brother:","“Dear brother, now it is your turn. Go and pursue your dream; this time I will support you.”","Albert held out his hands and answered softly:","“No, brother. It is too late. Four years in the mine have damaged my hands and every one of my fingers. I can no longer draw.”","Albrecht took his brother’s hands in his own and wept.","Centuries later, the works of Albrecht Dürer (1471–1528) continue to be displayed in leading galleries around the world. The famous drawing associated with his brother’s sacrifice is known as Praying Hands.","Moral: Behind success there often stands an unseen sacrifice. To recognize and honor that sacrifice is one of the finest expressions of humanity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mihrimah-sultan-and-architect-sinan","title":"Mihrimah Sultan and Architect Sinan","paragraphs":["When Mihrimah Sultan, the daughter of Suleiman the Magnificent, reached the age of seventeen, it is said that two men wished to marry her. One was Rüstem Pasha, the beylerbeyi of Diyarbakır; the other was Architect Sinan, the great master of Ottoman architecture. The name “Mihrimah” is of Persian origin: “mihr” means sun, and “mah” means moon.","Sultan Suleiman married his daughter to Rüstem Pasha. Architect Sinan, already married and approaching fifty, did not see his wish fulfilled. According to the legend, he nevertheless continued to love Mihrimah Sultan deeply.","Unable to share his life with Mihrimah, Sinan could not erase this love from his heart and tried to keep it alive in his art with all its delicacy.","At the palace’s commission, Architect Sinan built the Mihrimah Sultan Mosque in Üsküdar and turned it into a magnificent architectural monument. Legend says the mosque’s graceful silhouette recalls the lines of a woman whose skirts fall soft to the ground.","Later Sinan raised a second mosque for Mihrimah Sultan at Edirnekapı, one of the highest points in Istanbul. Though relatively compact in scale, it stands out for its exquisite lightness and brightness. Its slender minaret, the many windows ringing the dome, and its refined interior give the mosque a special airiness. Legend holds that some of the patterns and ornaments were worked to evoke Mihrimah Sultan’s long hair.","Thus two magnificent works of architecture said to be dedicated to one love arose in Istanbul.","The legend’s most moving moment is the meeting of the Sun and the Moon. It is said there is a spot in Istanbul from which both the Edirnekapı and the Üsküdar Mihrimah Sultan mosques can be seen. At the spring equinox, from that point, a crimson sun sets behind the minaret of the Edirnekapı mosque while, at the same moment, the moon rises behind the mosque in Üsküdar.","Mihrimah — Sun and Moon…","This sight fills people with wonder and gives wings to their imagination: did Sinan calculate it precisely in advance, or did this beautiful legend arise later?","Whatever the truth may be, this story has become one of Istanbul’s loveliest legends, joining Architect Sinan’s art with the name of Mihrimah Sultan.","Moral: Being united with the one you love is not always granted. Sometimes a person turns that love into a work that outlives the one who felt it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"architect-sinan-dies-in-a-house-without-water","title":"Sinan Brought Water to Istanbul but Died in a House Without It","paragraphs":["During the reign of Suleiman the Magnificent, Istanbul was growing rapidly and faced a severe water shortage. The task of solving it fell to the great architect Koca Sinan (1489–1588).","One day, the sultan summoned him and asked:","“Chief architect, people pay dearly even for a packhorse’s load of water. Can Istanbul be supplied with enough water?”","Sinan requested permission to survey the surrounding country. With his assistants, he examined wells, valleys, and streams from Çekmece to Beşiktaş and calculated where dams and aqueducts could be built.","When he returned, he told the sultan:","“It can be done, but at a heavy price: It will take countless sacks of gold.”","The sultan replied:","“Tell me only that it is possible. However much gold it takes, I will give it.”","Sinan began work and created a magnificent system that brought water to the city's various squares and was completed with forty fountains. Taps were fitted so that the precious water would not be wasted.","The sultan declared the public fountains the property of the people and prohibited secret pipes into private homes. In recognition of Sinan’s service, however, he verbally allowed a private line to the architect’s house.","Years passed. Suleiman and those who had witnessed Sinan’s service died. One day, the elderly architect was summoned to the palace, where the council was investigating a complaint about the private water supply to his home.","Sinan explained that the sultan himself had granted permission. When asked for a written decree, he admitted:","“At the time, I was ashamed to ask the Ruler of the World for a document. I have no written order.”","After a long debate, the council decided not to punish Sinan for his past use of the water, but ordered the private line cut off.","Near the end of his life, Sinan fell gravely ill. When he asked for water to moisten his lips, they saw that the house tap was not running. The great architect said:","“We did not serve for profit, but for Allah’s pleasure. We await our reward in the hereafter and do not grieve that this world has denied water to our home.”","Moral: Do not place excessive trust in fame, the world, or people’s loyalty. True service is rendered not for personal gain, but for what one knows to be right.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-essential-things-for-a-city","title":"Three Things a City Needs","paragraphs":["Every city needs sewers, bathhouses, and libraries. Sewers cleanse the city, bathhouses cleanse the body, and libraries cleanse the soul.","Fatih Sultan Mehmet","Moral: One must care for the cleanliness of the soul as well as of the city and the body. The library is a place that cleanses the soul.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]}]}
